      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>2 TAVAILEIM MoDOUGALL'S PATENT SELF RAISING FLOUR</head>
        <p>2 TAVAILEIM MoDOUGALL'S PATENT SELF RAISING FLOUR</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: The awards have not been made yet in some cases. . . . . .</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERToN: Then they might be left out. The Local Government Board is strict and we ought to be strict. It will be found that there is such a thing as a surcharge. You have got an auditor, aLd if the members of the Council have to pay £5OO or £6OO apiece they will know something about it. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) The MAYOR: 1 don't see that there is any danger. I think you are finding a mare's nest. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) The TOWN CLERK in response to the wish of one or two members explained that the Local Government Board d:rl not wish the Council to apply to them every time they purchased a hit of property. They bad instructed him to make the application from time to time when the Corporation had spent a considerable amount. They had now, in his opinion, spent a consider- able sum and he had therefore asked for the authority of the Finance Committee to make the necessary application. Councillor Pemberton knew very well that he (the Town Clerk) had to supply the Board with a complete statement in respe,t to every item and that would come before the Council. Councillor PEMBERTON : I want to know what they are. . .</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK: We cannot tell you at present. Councillor PEMBERTON : The question ought to be adjourned until we know what we are applying for. Councillor IZOBERTs said that any member who took the trouble to follow the business of the Council knew that not one single property referred to in the resolu- tion had been purchased without the full sanction of the Council. Now the time had come when application should be made to the Local Government Board for power to borrow the capital sum required to pay for the property purchased. He repudiated very strongly Councillor Pemberton's abominable suggestions that there was anything in the resolution v.hich was not psrfectly straight and honourable. (Hear, hear.) There was a suggestion in what Councillor Pemberton had said that the Town Council were going to the Local Government for sanction for something the Council had not already passed. It was most improper—he could characterize it by a stronger term. ODuncillor PEMBERTON moved an amendment that the minute he referred hack. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor LASTIFORD, in seconding, said it was all very well for Councillor Roberts t,) say that members would know all about these matters if they would only take an interest in the business of the Council. Councillor ROBERTS: I said they ought to do. I would not suggest for a moment that you know much about Council business.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : Councillor Roberts, don't interrupt. Councillor LASTIFOR.D : We don't all spend the time in the Town Hall that Councillor Roberts does. We haven't a:1 the time to spend that he la.-,s—simply for his own ends, for his own convenience. Councillor RonERTs rose to protest against this ttack.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: That is an attack. You must with- draw it.</p>
        <p>Alderman SINIETHURST : It is top bad. (Hear, her.) The IVIAyon, (to Councillor Lathford) : I must ask you to withdraw. ijouncillor LASHFORD : He gives it me pretty hot sometimes, and I think he ought to take it hack again. Councillor ROBERTS : Not that sort of thing. The worst thing 1 have said about you is that you lack common sense and T repeat it. Councillor Ls.EFFoRD : There is DO pulling your leg about me, I tell you. The MAYOR: You ought to withdraw. It is certainly a very insulting personal skaternent. Councillor LASHFORD : What is Councillor Rohe:ts doing here every day ?—You don't see me here every morning._</p>
        <p>The -MAYOR : I should be glad to see sou. (Laughter.) Please withdraw. The statement you have made is unusual,.lnd I must ask for it to be withdrawn.</p>
        <p>Alter a short silence the MAYOR laid to Councillor Lashford, " H you have not sufficient gentlemanly fe'ling to withdraw it, I shall have to leave you to your own devices. You are stamping your conduct in u very bad manner. That is all I can say." Councillor LASHFORD : On your ruling I will with- draw. (Hear, hear). Councillor GOUGH in supporting theamendment. said the Council ought to be informed of the expenditure involved. Why should they be called upon to pass a resolution for an open cheque ? Councillor WILKINSON: The whole of this talk h.s been as empty as an egg shell when the contents have been taken out. (Laughter). Councillor LASH FORD : A pretty picture you can paint. ( Laughter). Councillor I,VILKINSON pointed out that the resolution had no reference to any specific purchases. (" Shame.") Before they applied to the Local Government Board a list would be brought before the Council for sanction</p>
        <p>to the borrowing powers. Wby this air as if there was something under the table? " Where lamitis on the top," he concluded. COMlCillor PEMBERTON: No, it is not. Councillor COUGH: Then give us the properties and the proper amount. Councillor WILKINSON : There are no properties specified. That is where you are wrong, simply because you don't understand the meaning of this. Gouncillor COUGH: Spending money and you don't know what you have spent. Councillor WILKINSON: I am surprised at you, above the aleis. You have a bit of sense. (Laughter.) It does not refer to any money spent. The MAYOR said he was pleased Councillor Lasbford had withdrawn the observation he had made, and it was only right that he (the Mayor) should ask Councillor Roberts to express his regret for the hasty remark he made about Councillor Lashford. Councillor ROBERTS: what was the remark? The MAYOR: It was not complimentary. It was not unparliamentary, and might be considered some- what of a witticism in a way, but it was not compli- mentary,and it was,therefore, only right that both sides should recipror ate. Councillor ItoBERTs: I at once accede to your request. If I have unknowingly committed a witticism I have pleasure in withdrawing it. (Laughter and hear; hear). The amendment was lost, only Councillors Lasbford, Pemberton and Gough voting for it. The minutes were then agreed to. more</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Health Committee a letter, dated the sth December, 1012, from the Local Government Board was read, in which it was stated that the Board were prepared to approve the proposed arrangements of the Town Council in regard to the provision of a Tuberculos:s Dispensary, and hospital accommodation for purposes of observation, educational treatment, and the treatment of advanced cases of the disease, and the appointment of a tuberculosis officer to act also as a resident medical officer, at the hospital; subject (a) to the submission to the Board of satisfactory plan of the proposed alterations in connection with the Dispensary at the Public Health Offices, and of the necessary additions and alterations to the Sankey Hospital (b) to the provision to the Board's satisfaction of other accommodation for the isolation of cases of smallpox occurring in the Borough in substitution for that at the Sankey Hospital, arid (c) to the making, if necessary, of arrangements with the County Councils of Cheshire end Lancashire for the treatment at the proposed Dispensary and at Sankey Hospital of ceses of tuberculosis from adjoining are)s in the Counties, and the Town Clerk reported thereon ; the Borough Surveyor also reported toot he was pieparing plans for the proposed extension, and it was : resolv, d that they be torwareed with the Board at as early a cote as possible. Councillor aF.NKINS, in moving the adoption of this minutes, said the negotistrous with the Local Govern- -menu Board and the Insurance Committee, with regard to the Sankey hospital, were exceedingly favourable to the Health Committee's wishes. The point they desired above all things was to preserve mai develop their powers with regard to the Sankey Icatitution. It was a place capable of very great development end of great value to the borough from the health standpoint.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>COUNCIL SCENES.</head>
        <p>COUNCIL SCENES.</p>
        <p>TOWN PLANNING PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT, in moving the adoption of the minutes of the Street Improvement and Lighting Com- mittee, mentioned that the Local Government Board bad sanctioned the preparation of a town planning scheme for the borough. They had been longer over it than he considered necessary, but Warrington was one of the first four towns in Lancashire and one of the first twenty-four local authorities in the United Kingdom to get this far. He hoped the Council would make up for lost time by diligently applying them- selves to the matter now. Frankly,he hid been greatiy diFappointed by the apathy of the Town Council. Town planning was immeasurably the most important work with which they had t ) deal. He hoped every member would study the question,and approach it in a broad-minded spirit, and would help the committee to prepare a place which would be a real credit to the town. It was no exaggeration to say that the whole future of Warrington would very largely depend upon their attitude during the next few mouths.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>"HISTRIONIC DISPLNY."</head>
        <p>"HISTRIONIC DISPLNY."</p>
        <p>On the minutes of the Street Improvement and Lighting Committee,Alderman Tinnion called attention to an agreement for the purchase of property in Buttermarket-street from the executors of the late Mrs Caldwell. He contended that opportunity ought to he afforded for further discussion by the Council, as the proposed method of dealing with tho property constituted a departure from the usual lines and was, in his opinion, the wrong basis to adopt. Councillor PEMBERTON said that, unlike Alderman Tinnion, he believed in the principle, hut he supported the Alderman's plea on the ground that property ought not to he bought without the consent of the General Purposes Committee. Councillor WILKINSON : Whenever is it done ? Councillor CHANTLER. : It has been done. Councillor PEMBERTON : It really is surprising to see the sublime and child-like innocence assumed by Councillor Wilkinson. (Laughter.) Councillor Wilkinson rose to reply when the IdAYott observed: 'No interruptions, pleise."</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON: I ani not interrupting, your Worship. I rise to a point of order. Am I not in order in asking any member of the Council who makes a rash statement of that description to give a concrete case. Ceuncillor CIIANILER : Do you allude to me. Councillor WILKINSON : Ob, no. You say properties have been purchssed without the sanction of the Council. Councillor PEmBERToNt Yes, lots of them. Councillor WILKINSON : It is an illegal act. Councillor PEMBERTON I quite agree. The MAYOR : This is not a conversation. You should addreSs the chair. Councillor PEMBERTON : When it snits him Coun- cillor Wilkinson assumes such innocence that one would think be was an infant. (Laughter). The MAYOR : That is a very good histrionic exhibi- tion, but it is not debate.</p>
        <p>COuncillor-P-EmitEaToN lam only showing what we have to put up with from Councillor Wilkinson. The MAYOR Keep to the minutes. Councillor PF,MBERTON : I quite agree, if everybody else is going to keep to them. At this stage the Mayor left for the Children's Ball in the Parr Hall, and the Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Forshaw) was voted to the chair.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said there were two principlas On which they could acquire property in the main streets. One was to buy tne property outright and simply retain it in the hope of selling it at some future date. The other was to endeavour to keep people to rebuild on the site of their existing premises. The committee as a matter of broad policy, thought that in view of the difficulty of getting people to build shops in the main streets of Warrington at the present time, it might be a desirable policy to endeavour to arrange, i when the property n question had to come down, to rebuild on the same site. Councillor Pemberton was one of the strong advocates of that principle and the committee unanimously approved of it in this parti- cular case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>SANATORIUM TREATMENT.</p>
        <p>Councillor BIRTLE3 expressed the opinion that the answers were satisfactory, as the tender had been dealt with in the way mentioned in order to expedite the work.</p>
        <p>understood. opened an d initialledA t t he spsflanh, :aortoni rsotdutvl Councillor OSBORSE said be was a member of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee, and the explanation which had been given was certainly not in agreement with what the majority of the members n:gbragooiti tsu) eiytge, hashneisme :sstawateueaksnanklec. sfvdee:sen:pyur tenders bprsrusyr twtoi washsnteeehtr (lye ' ltetdtbhoy' ameenoer Surveyor t :BnvaTto entitledcct brhaiattiicrinstructedmsisuencn:h.ho etfaolAßtcr loeodwipueooorruisrmi Borough would Alderman (Councillor Osborne) thought that in justice to the Council, if the alderman could not have been present the deputy-cheirman (Councillor Dicl.enson) should have attended because he would have borne out what he (Coupcillor Oeborne) was saying. It was not right to ask members of the Copuoil to attend the committee and then allow the Chairman to do the work without consulting them as to their wishes. He protested against it. The DEPUTY-MAYOR : Have you seen the resolution of the committee accepting the tender ? Conncillor OSBORNE : Having bound the bands of the committee, having let the contract and the work being in band, WA too were bound to allow the work to go on. I say it is decidedly unfair for anyone to do that and then to lorce the hands of the members of cry committee. Alderman SMI4:Tiit;RST disagreed with Councillor o.,beene. It was decided that the Borough Surveyor sh uld obtain the 1 inch glaos That was not done by Aldermen Bolton or any; ether individual, but by the Committee. Councillor OSB,RNE : I beg in protest against that. i As a matter of fact any other member cl the committee : who was present will hear me out. The DEPUTIr-NIAYOR : You are out of order. Councillor OsuortNe : I appeal te the Borough Surveyor. (Cries pf " cheir.") I thick, on a point of explanation, I am entitled to appeal to the Borough Surveyor as to whether the work was not in hand at the time of the secend meeting. Councillor PEMBERTON : Councillor Osborne does not quite understand the ruling here. He will have a great many members of the Council against him if he tries to act like this. It. is absolutely refreshing to hear a gentleman of independent mind getting up , here— Aldermen TINNIoN : Hear, hear. Councillor PENIBERTON : Splendid. It is only another instance of what I said before, to-night. This would not have been done twelve months ago. There is something coming over the Council. No private firm would allow this sort of thing to go on. You are not the masters, let me toll you. You are only repre- senting the public and they determine the constitution of the Council. This contract was let against the wishes of the committee. Councillor BeerreEs : I protest against that. It was brought before the committee and sanctioned by them. Councillor PEMBERTON: I would sooner believe what Counciilor Osborne says. It only shows you how things are denied here. It is abominable for a gentle- ma u like Councillor Birtles to get up and make such mm assertion when he knows very well that it is entirely Wi tug. Ceur cillor CUNNINGTON: I agree with every word that Councillor Osborne has Mid and disagree with Counci Ifor Birtles and Alderman Smethurst. I say he is right and they are wrong. It never crime up. Only teo men—the Chairman and the Borough Surveyor— have attended to it. Councillor GOUGH said the ieason he asked the questions was because Councillor Osborne raised the matter in the ente-room in the presence of Aldermen Boltou, and complained that he had let the contract without the committee's sanction or knowledge. Too ouch of that sort of thing went on in the Council and it was time a stop was put to it. Councillor BROADIIIIRST Inked for information regarding an arrangement with Mr Hindle, engineer, of Manchester, to supervise the work in connection with the baths extension. Ile also inquired whether the cheap oaths for workingmen were to oe ready this summer? Councillor OSBORNE; said it was rather unfortunate ,1 that Councillor Broadhurst had raised the first matter, in as much as he must know that tbe work was terribly neglected. He did not wish to cast any reflection npon coy (Alicia' of the Cerporatioo, hut they had to acknowleage facts. It had been the desire of the couemittee to get on with the work so as to enable men and women to use the baths at the earliest possible date. To do that, it had been necessary to call in outside help and Mr Hindle, who had had some- thin g to do with the extension, was suggested as one of the most likely to get the work done rapidly and well. Councillor PEMBEFtTON : The contract ought to have been finished last June. It is only another instance of . the way things are going in this Town Council. Councillor CHANTLER said that, in common fairness to the Borough Surveyor's department, it ought to be . stated that they had been understaffed during the past twelve mouths. Alderman TEINION explained that Mr Hindle was responsible for the bills of quantities for the baths extension. The matter was in the hands of the late Borough Surveyor and Mr Hindle and no one else knew anything about it. Unfortunately the present Borough Surveyor was not consulted by the late Borough Surveyor and as Mr Hindle was the only man who knew anything abctr it, his services were obtained in Order that the baths might be completed. The minutes were paEsed. MORE QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gouott asked the Chairman of the Water Committee :- 1. What clsss of fuel was advertised for when asking for tenders for last contract. 2. Was another class afterwards substituted without asking the whole of previous tenders to compete. 3. What was the Lame of the firm whose tender was accepted and was it the lowest tender. Alderman JOHN EVANS replied (1) The word "fuel' was used in the advertismfmt only. In the particulars of contract it was " firing fuel " (2). No. (3) The names. of the accepted firms were the Pearson and</p>
        <p>Knowles Coal and 'lron Co., Ltd., and the Warrington Slate Co., Ltd. The committee did not accept the lowest tender. In the course of a further explanation, the alderman said the tender was given to the firms named on the result of exhaustive tests made by the engineer. The policy of the committee had been justified because from April Ist to December 31st, 1912, there had been a saving of over £ioo in the amount of fuel used.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturdays January 11, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>TRAFFICKING IN TRAM TICKETS.</head>
        <p>TRAFFICKING IN TRAM TICKETS.</p>
        <p>The Electricity and Tramways Committee resolved that on and after the Ist January next workmen's tickets issued before 8 a m. be available for the return journey Or 4 the day of issue after 2 p.m. and after 12 noon on Saturdays. Councillor WILSON moved an amendment that the minute be referred back. He said it was very hard that workingmen should be compelled to make use of the tickets on the day of issue. Many of them had a score of tickets which would now be useless to them. Councillor HENSHALL seconded.</p>
        <p>-Cou-ncillern PEACOCK, as the one responsible for the propcsition, slid workmen's tickets were scarcely ever issued in other towns except on the condition that they were available on the day of issue only. The new rule would meet the convenience of the employees who worked eight hours' shifts. Many workmen had as many as twenty or thirty tickets in their possession and a lot of them bad been re-sold. Hitherto those vtho had worked from 6 a.m. to 2 p.m. had been unable to make use of the return halves on the day of IESUEL The old rule encouraged workmen to traffic in the tickets and it had been done openly. It vas the intention of the committee that the tickets already issued should be available at any time, but no new tickets would be sold except on the conditions that they were used on the day of issue.</p>
        <p>Councillor LINDLEY said the workmen's tickets were irsued at a cheap rate, and the proposed new rul was not a great restriction. He believed the conimittea intended, however, that a ticket should be available on the corresponding day of the following week. (" No, No.") Councillor ROBERTS supperted the amendment because a workman taking out a ticket in the evening and returning before 8 a.m the following day would not. be able to use the return half.</p>
        <p>Councillo HAWTHORN said workmen's tickets were not issued in the evening. The new rule would be a concession to men who went to work at 6 a.m. and worked for eight hours. After further discussion, 11 voted for the amendment and 13 against. The minutes were accordingly con- firmed.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.—MORE SCENES, Councillor CUNNINGTON asked the Chairman of the Bridges Committee the follbwing questions :—(1) What is the amount of the contract for building War- rington Bridge? (2) What is the additional cost for carrying out Mr Webster's advice to the Committee to alter the conditions of the original tender? (3) What is the amount certified as due to contractor up to date? (4) What is the amount paid up to date by the Corpora- tion on this work not included in Mr Thorn's contract? Alderman SMETHURST replied as fellows :—(1) There is no fixed amount for this contract. The Corporation have agreed to pay the suns of £14,310, after adding thereto or deducting therefrom as the case may require such sum or sums of money as ...hall he necessary to repreeent the value of the works executed in accord- ance Niith the amended designs and amended specifica- tion at the schedule of prices so far as the same is applicable, and in other cases in which there shall be no price in the schedule of prices or bill of quantities for any work the mice for the latter shall be agreed upon with the Engineer, subject to the approval of the Cor- poration. (2) 'AU; sum was estimated by the Engineer at £2,292 29 7d. but it cannot be given definitely until the close of the contract. (3) The amount certified to the contractor up to the date of the last certificate is £7,441 89 11d. (4) £1,256 123 Gd up to 23rd December, the last available date. Alderman 'Smethurst added that the whole of this information was given to the com- mittee at their last meeting when Councillor Cunnington was present. In addition, there were on the works materials amounting to the sum of £1,750. Alderman SMEI HURST added that full details of the extra £2,000 referred to in the answer were given in a published statepient of accounts, over 100 articles thebridge gridegneumweorusltd cost e,cl.Cnizooilo or rrPemberton had stated h R 50,000. Up to now it had cost £lB,OOO,</p>
        <p>Councillor LASHFORD : It is not finished. Councillor PEMBERTON denied ever having made such a statement. If the members of the Council weuld speak out, they would have to aomit that they did not know the facts which Aldermen Smethurst had just disclosed. He bad no idea they had already spent £1,260 outside the contrect. Then they had the salar:i of the clerk of works Councillor ROBERTS I rise to a point of order. 1 submit that this is entirely outside the question before the meeting. Counciller BROADHURST : hear, hear, it is. Councillor -.PEMBERTON, to place himself in order, moved an amendment tbet the accounts passed by the Bridges Committee, amounting to £SGO 15s 3d be not paid. Continuing he said the contract was already much behind its time, and at the present rate of pro- I gress, the bridge would not be completed in less than ; three years. Even allowing for the materials en hand, over £7,000 of the £lO,OOO had already been expended, but only one third of the bridge had been built. (" Shame.") It was a serious matter for the public— in fact the job was becoming a scandal. It was time the Council put its foot down and got to know exactly how things stood before sanc.ioniug any more payments. Councillor BROADHURST asked what policy Coun- cillor Pemberton had to offer in lieu of the present system? Could he show them any method by which the bridge could be constructed at a greater speed ? He did not wish to disparage Councillor Pemberton for a moment. If he were constructing a house or even a slopstone for a back kitchen —(laughter)—he might be regarded as an authority, but he had no experience of the difficulties which had to be contended with in laying the foundations of a leridge. The first part of such a contract was always the more difficult. Alderman BENNETT said these continuous attacks on the contractor were quite unnecessary and stupid. Obviously the contractor could only have one idea before him—the sooner he got the work done the sooner he got paid and freed from responsibility. To suggest that he would not accelerate the work to the utmost limit, consistent with safety, was ridiculous. It was better to be slow and sure in such an undertaking I then to rush a lot of men on the job without proper precautions and calculations. Councillor LastiFoßD asked who was Councillor Broadhurst representing—the builders or the ratepayers —in his graud speech. Councillor BROADHURST (warmly) : lam represent- ing those 501 electors of St Austins Ward who had the confidence in me to raura me to this Council Chamber a second time. I would like to say that the ten open air meetings I addressed on behalf of Councillor Lashford are one of the reasons why be is sitting het e to-day. (Laughter and Applause.) I assure you that he will never get ten meetings again—to be the tool of John Lord and Councillor Pemberton—that's what he is. Alderman TINNION said the dark and dreadful pictures which Councillor Pemberton painted were largely drawn from his own imagination. Of course, they agreed that the bridge was not making that progress which they would like to see, and he suggested that a small sub-committee should be formed to facilitate the work. Alderman S:nethurst was taking too much of the responsibility upon his own shoulders and was worrying his hairs grey. He (the speaker) was glad to see so many of the public present that night.He would like to inform them that at all the meetings of the Bridges e;ornmittee they had similar disgraceful pro- ceedings—only worse. Until there was a real business spirit and some sensible proposal made— Councillor PEMBERTON : Hear, hear. Alderman TINNION : You may say " bear, hear," but you never make a sensible proposal. You simply find ' fault from the beginning to the end of the meeting. You quarrel with everybody who happens to disagree with you. We never get a sensible proposal from you although we get a great many Jeremiah prophecies that everything is going wrong, and the only man who knows about it is Councillor Pemberton. The progress of the works is not satisfactory to the majority of the people in town but the continual bickering at the Bridges Committee meetings is not the way to bring it to a head and effect a desirable improvement. Councillor PEN:BERTON : There is no doubt the cora-</p>
        <p>mittee are in a muddle and they don't know what to do about it. They won't listen to anybody's suggestions Councillor RiglnnTS : I wish the Council Chamber had been crowded with ratepayers, for a more sorry and disgraceful exhibition of absolute obstruction to progress has never taken place here. If there is any gentleman among the ratepayers oresent,who is satisfied with the repiesentation of Councillor Pemberton, I am sorry for his intelligence and business capacity. The atmosphere in which this question has been discussed to-night, is the atruospliere in which this yvork is dis- cussed at every meeting, and the man prineirily and almost entirely responsible for that condition of things Councillor LASTIFORD: Is this on the minutes ? Councillor WILKINSON : Are you on? Sit down. Councillor GoUGII : He is only having a go at Councillor Pemberton. Councillor RoBERTS : Well, I will have a go at Councillor Gough, if he likes. Councillor GoUGH : I will have a go at you, too. Councillor ROBERTS : In all the remarks I have made with regard to Councillor Pemberton, I associate Councillor Gough, so far its his entry to the Council is concerned. I sincerely hope the intelligent ratepayers who are here to-night will take note of the kind of representation which has been permitted oocasionally to come into this Council Chamber. I say with solemeity that until the ratepayers of Warriegton—l don't mean those who claim to be the ratppayers' repre- sPntat ices, whose aggregate rateable value will amount to something considerably less than .£l5O, but I .nean those who do represent the ratepaying interests of War- rington—that the sooner they rise to a sense of responsibility— Councillor PENIBERTON : This is abominable. Con the Town Clerk, by any stretch of imagination allow a thing like this to go on? Speak to the minutes, It is abominable. Councillor GOUGH : Those who have got interests in the Council are here to make an attack on Councillor Pemberton because he is trying to expose it. Councillor ROBERTS : I think the effect of my remarks is pretty evident on the persons concerned,and I think I can afford to leave it at that. Alderman SmwritunsT said the appointment of a new sub-committee would not de the wonders that some people seemed to expect. The minutes were then approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>SERIES OF ACRIMONIOUS DEBATES</head>
        <p>A SERIES OF ACRIMONIOUS DEBATES</p>
        <p>RECRIMINATIONS AND WITHDRAWALS.</p>
        <p>A series of acrimonious debates, in which personali- ties were freely indulged in, caused the monthly meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening to occupy three hours. The Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph) presided over the first part of the proceedings and there were also present :—The Deputy Mayor (Alder- man Forshaw), Aldermen A Bennett, T Burton, John Evans, J Sruethurst and D Tinnion ; Archer, T Birtles, J BroaOhurst, T Ciaantler, J Chrimes, G Craik, E Connington, S Gough, J Halliwell, J Hawthorn, R Henshall, W H J Jenkins, F T Lashford, F Lindley, J 'l' Locker, Dr McKee, E Osborne, P Peacock, J Pemberton, H Roberts, J Skelland, S Taylor, J Vvilkinson, aud W J Wilson ; Nlessrs J L Whittle (town clerk), A T Hallaway (deputy town clerk), TII Spinks (assistant town clerk), W II Woodcock (aisistant 'treasurer), A M Ker (borough surveyor), NT S Haddock (gas engineer), J M !Murray (director of education), Dr J Coote Hibbert (medical officer), and J Gray (water engineer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>GRANINIAR SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>THE GRANINIAR SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Councillor H ROBERTS was re-elected a represents the governor of the Roteler Great uer School. THE COST OF THE PARLIAMENTARY BILL. Councillor LASHFORD asked the Chairman of the Finance Committee (1) What was the cost of the last Parliamentary Bill, and (2) what was the Town Clerk's estimate of the cost giT en et the statutory meeting of ratepayers held in Parr Hall? Councillor WILKINSON said the cost of the Bill was £3,330 16s. 9d. ("Phew," and the MAYOR; "Order.") At the meeting in Parr Hall on Janusry 21st, 1910,-the Town Clerk replying to a question, said if the Bill was riot opposed £l,ooo, ho thought, would cover the whole of the expenses. The Bill was opposed and very strongly toe by the Mersey Docks and Harbour Board, and, without mentioning names, by many people in the town. If a Bill was opposed, as cne might naturally expect would he the case, the legal expenses • became very heavy. The Town Clerk merely gave what, in his opinion, would he the cost without upposition, and Councillor Lashford might have saved his time and that of the Council, and also the cost of printing a very unnecessary question,if he had referred to the published abstract of accounts. Councillor PEMBERToN : I rise to a point of order In answering a question is the Chairman of a Com- mittee entitled to make a speech. without the inter/rogator having the right to reply ? Councillor LAsiiForto rose to reply. but was ruled Out of order. Councillor WILKINSON: I admit I am somewhat strayim: away from thequestions, although I am making Some remarks The MAYoR : You are not entitled to make any comments. You have merely to answer the questions. Coonciilor WILKINSON : If Councillor Lashford had perused the abstract of accounts—(" Chair, chair ") he would not have need to ask this question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>COST £3,000 AND SOLD FOR £505.</head>
        <p>COST £3,000 AND SOLD FOR £505.</p>
        <p>On the minutes of the Finance and Estates Com- mittee, Councillor Chantler asked for information uith reference to the following resolution of the committee of the 16th December: " That lot seven, being the plot of land at the corner ot Howley-lane and Church- street, he sold to 111 r Thomas Williams for the sum of £505." Was it he asked, the same piece of land TA hich the Corpordtion bought for £3,144? Councillor WILKENSoN said he must ask for proper notice to be given of the question. Councillor PEMBERTON moved, as an amendment that the minutes in que,tion te not confirmed. The MAYOR : The land was sold by auction. Councillor PEMBERTON said the Council had never fixed any reserve sum. The cost of the land was over £3,000 and only a very small strip had been taken from it. Did anyone contend for a</p>
        <p>moment that an improvement in that part of the town was worth £2,500? Too much of this sort of thing had been going on—there had been quite al revolution in the Town Hall during the past six months. The land cost £3,000 and had been sold for £505 without the consent of the Council hlving been obtained. It was most improper, and it was oifficurc for him to find words in which to express himself. He did not know how men who professed to represent the ratepayers could stand it. Councillor Gouott socended the amendment. The MAYOR said the Council decided some months ago that all surplus land should be sold, and the sum named was the best that could be got. Councillor PEMBERTON : But the Council—not the Committee—has a right to fix the reserve price. During the last six months eve-ything has been taken out of the hands of the Cuncil. Alderman TINNION said this was one of the unfortu- nate pieces of land about which they went to arbitra- tion, and the Council g(it by far the dirtiest end of the stick. The price awarded was very considerably more than the land was worth. The site had stood idle for a considerable time and the committee, with the approval and consent, of the Council, decided to sell it. The result only confirmed the opinion of many mem- bers—that although they gave such an extraordinary sum for the land, the market value to-day was some- thing like £5OO. What good would be done by referring the matter back ? The land had been sold by public auction and could not be got back. Councillor ..-IF.NKINS Is it a fact that the conveyance of the land is completed? The MAYOR : No. Councillor LASHFORD : The matter ought to have</p>
        <p>been brought before the Council for the members to sanction. I don't suppose ten members of the Coupe i knew anything about it. The MAYon. : The Council decided that the land should be sold by public auction. The TOWN CLERK said the actual purchase was £2,670 the cost of pulling down, legal expenses, stamp duty, ac., £95 7s Gd, and street works yet to be executed, £378 12s 9d. In answer to a member, The Alkveß said the Corporation would retain as much land as they required for street widening. Councillor WILKINSON said they got the best price they could get—in fact, a better price than he had expected. Only Councillors Pemberton, Lashford and Gough voted for the amendment, which was accordingly lost. A BREEZE.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLORS LASHFORD AND ROREItTSI " WITHDRAW " OBSERVATIONS.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON called attention to the following minute of the Finance Committee: "That the Town Clerk be authorized to apply to the Local Government Board for their senction to the borrowing of the moneys spent and incurre.4 up to date in the acquisition of properties for street improvement." There was, he said, no price mentioned ; and he did not know whether the amount was £5OO or £50,000. He was a member of the Street Improvement Committee, and he did not know what was included in the resolu- tion.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : We don't know ourselves set. Ccuncillor PEMBERTCN : Is this Council being seriously asked to give a blank cheque? Councillor Rouse.Ts : It is not. Talk sense. Are we to listen to this kind of blather all night ? Councillor W I LKINSO'N : Let him go on. Councillor PEMBERTON : I may say here and now that I shall object, and that I take my objection under Standing Order 16. Councillor ROBERTS : Oh. take it outside. Councillor PEMBERTON : You will get put outside, perhaps, before the night is out, if you talk like that. I am here to represent the ratepayers—not myself. I have no interest at all, neither for myself, nor my family is any way. The fewer interruptions I get, the sooner shall we get through the business. I take my rights under standing order 16 and I shall not give way, as I have done before, to any person who wants to interrupt. Certain members invariably try to take an unfair advantage of me by these means. I wish to ask your support, Mr Mayor, in this matter. It is simply becoming intolerable. If this expenditure hss been incurred up to date, why have we not an account of it? Councillor G 0110 If : We ought to have a rough estimate. Councillor PEMBERTON : Quite a different system has come about recently. These agenda papers have become a mystery to re. The MAYOR said they had been instructed by the Local Government Board to apply from time to time in the manner named in the resolution, and on this occasion they did not know the exact amount. Councillor PEMBERTON said he quite agreed that that was so. That was one of the very grounds on which the Chairman of the Committee on the Warrington Parliamentary Bill was so severe in his comments on the Council. There were several Cases on record where property had been purchased, without the sanction of the Council, and this was the way in which it was done. He was going to kick most vigorously against this sort of thing. There had been a change in the formation of the Council, and it appeared that they were going back to the old ways. The M A YOR : Has it ever struck you that the Local Government Board are not likely to sanction anything that is improper ? (Hear, hear). Councillor PEMBERTON : No, sir ; it has not struck me. On the first blush thiit looks right, hut the Local Government Board have demurred to many purchases. Only recently an inspector of the Board criticised the action of the Council most severely. If this property has been purchased legitimately and straightforwardly, we ought to know what we are going to pay for it, lipproximately.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>. . .</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION : No, I objected all through. Alderman BENNETT having intimated that the terms would be a matter for further consideration, the minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>^-aw</p>
        <p>MINIMINOMI</p>
        <p>It was stated at a London inquest, yesterday, on a man who committed suicide by shooting himself while travelling in a train,that the bullet pierced the window of a house fifty yards from the railway line, and only missed a young man's head by inches.</p>
