      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>ALLEGED NUISANCE.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>COttneilior JENKINS referred to the committees visit of inspection to the works of the Warrington Products Company at the invitation of the firm, and said there had been a great improvement with regard to a nuisahce filch bad been complained of.</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGH asked whether the stench alleged to arise from the works had ceased, as be believed it was a great nuisance and dangerous to health. When ii similar works was situated in Arplev field it wag soon " cried down "because of its proximity to Wi:son- Patten-street.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said it was so absolutely unusual for him to he able to agree Wit h Conncillor Golo;h, hut he could not help saying how glad he was to find him championing pure air and the exti;:ction of nuisances. lie was not without hopes of rall3 jug the Councillor rindcr the "no smoke" banner. (Laughter). Councillor PEMBERTON said he was pleased to hear the favourable report from Councillor Jenkius. Some time ago it requisition was sent up by IS residents in the neighbourhood who ageged that, the conditions were most offensive end unfit to live in. • Councillor LASHFortn said th,it only the previous night the smell WEIS 01Hr/if:eons. Councillor JENKINS wild the firm were undoubtedly doing their hest with improved inethods and machinery to bring ab, sit a better state of things. The minutes were approved, INCREASES OF WAGES.</p>
        <p>The Sanitary Works Committee submitted a com- prehensive list of increases of wages to their workmen Councillor WILsoN. in moving the adoption of the recommendation said the sub-committee which bad dealt with the matter thought the application of the workmen was only fair and just. The work which they carried out was uot very agreeable and it was usually done di:ring the night. The works were a credit to the town. As the men had to face all kinds of weather the committee had doeidsd to provide them with pilot coats. The total cost of the increases of wages and the extra uniform would be about £3OO, but it was anticipated thaL tue sum would be more than counter-balanced by ttle increased profits for the aale of manure during the coming season. Councillor HAWTHORN seconded. He said the com- mittee had lists from towns of similar population and found that Warring,t on did rot compare favourably with the majority of there in the amount of wages paid for this class- of werk. The ilicn,ases ranged from Is. to 2s. per week and over 89 men would benefit by them. Councillor PEMBERToN i•aid the increeses would be of very little benefit to the men owing to the higher cost of living.</p>
        <p>The minutes Were confirmed. SALARIES ADVANCED.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee resolved, that the salary of Mr John 9 Cannon, second clerk in the Boreugh Treasurer's department, be inereased from £l5O to £165 per annum as and from the Ist February next. This recommendation was approved, without comment.</p>
        <p>The Nluseuna Committee reiolved that the salary of Mr J McAdam he increased from £l2O to .C.,127 tOs per annum, end afterwards to £l4O per annum by two annual instalments of .£7 lOsand £5 respectively. Councillor PEMBERTON pointed out that when Mr McAdam wits appointed at a salary of £Bo,the maximum was fixed at £l2O. How long were these advances to continue?</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY-MAYOR said the increase was providA for out of the lid. rate allowed to the committee. Councillor PEMBERTON : But this is a good cadging ootnruittee. They ought to cot down their expenses. I hink £l2O is sufficient for the position and if it weres tthrown open to-morrow, at that salary, we should have 200 applicants. Councillor BROADHURST said they could not judge a man's capabilities by the number of applicants there might be for his position. He had found the Library of great value in furnishing information with regard to trades in which Warrington's workmen are interested. Alderman BENNETT said that if Councillor Pemberton ever had a chance of saying anything unkind about the Museum Committee, he always went out of his way to do so. Having referred to the extension of the work of the Library by the establishment of branch and school libraries, the alderman said Mr MacAdarn's position could only be filled by a man of intelligence, culture and all-round reading. He was a most useful and capable servant. The minutes were approved. THE BATHS EXTENSION. COUNCILLOR OSBORNE'S CRITICISM OF PROCEDURE.</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGH had given notice to ask the Chair- man of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee : (1) If he, the Chairman on his own authority let a Contract without consulting the Committee. (2) Was the contract the lowest tender one. The tender referred to wrA that of MEssrs Mellowes S; Co., Ltd., of Sheffield, for the patent glazing required the baths extensions, at 01d. per square foot. The DEPUTY-MAYOR, replying in the absence of the Chairman of the Committee (Alderman Bolton) stated ; (1) When the tenders were opened an initialled by the Chairman of the Committee on November 20th, they -were referred to the Borough Surveyor to report upon, and both the Chairman and tue Borough Surveyor understood that authority was given to the Chairman to deal with the matter, as the contractor for the work was urging the necessity of the building being covered in as soon as possible when the tender was accepted, subject to the approval of the Council. (2) At the meeting of December 12th, at which Mr Gough was present, the Bolough Sbrveyor reported fully on the matter and explained that the lowest tender provided for 3-16th inch.glass, and he recommended inch glass. The Committee agreed to his recommendation and con- firmed the acceptance of the tender by the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
