      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was quite a New Year's Sunday at this meeting. presided over by the president, who shared in the, addresses with Mr William Beswiek. The president spoke open the closing of 1912 and the beginning of 1913, and gave suggestions for making the Brotherhood more prosperous, and its meetings more useful and helpful. Mr Beswick took for his subject "The comrany of Jesus, its influence and helpfulness." What the disciples end Jesus' earthly followers experienced, we might experience day by day, communion with Jesus brings peaceltnd joy. Mr Jos Owen read the lesson and the president led the prayer. Appropriate hymns were sung, closing with "Forward be our Watchword."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>Brick-street Mission.</head>
        <p>Brick-street Mission.</p>
        <p>The annual tea party and concert in connection with the above took place on New Year's Day, when the President of the Church (Mr William Barton, sen) occupied the chair and gave a few interesting remarks on the prospects of the church for the ensuing year. The Secretary of the Cnnrch (Mr S Gallop) gave his report on the Church's financial position, which was very satisfactory, especially in view of the scheme of rerovat ion and alteration which had teen carried out. Mr William Yates presented an excellent report on the Sunday school. An interesting programme was then submitted by the following artistes :—Mr Goostry, tenor ; Miss Savory, Mr Attwell, bass ; Mrs E Sanders, contralto ; Mr S Ankland, humorist ; Miss Eva Shaw, accompanist. Mrs Sanders and Mr Goostry, who pleased their listeners greatly, were at their best and their songs were quite a treat to hear. Mr Dunkley introduced his" living waxwork " figures, v.ho gave an excellent performance with songs, &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>Wycliffe Congregational Church.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev A J Viuer, F.T.S., secretary of the Lancashire Congregational Union, who has acted as moderator to the Wycliffe Congregation during the vacant pastorate, occupied the pulpit on Sunday. Addressing the young people he told a story illustrating the journey of life, and advised them to let the. Word of God he their guide. His sermon was based on God's assurance to Moses: "Certainly I will be with thee." Referring to the reluctance with which Moses under- took the task of freeing the children of -Israel from the thraldom of the Egyptians he said a man who was capable of seeing duty On its 'large side was usually a man who was modest in his appreciation of his ability to perform that duty. These people who only saw things in a small way were always leap- ing up to do them regardless of their ability to perform them. We were now at the beginning of another year. We could not see into it, we were like children in the dark. If we set ourselves to think about the year we might find ourselves in some respects like Moses shrinking hack from the pilgrimrge. He (the preacher) therefore biought them this assuring message : " will be with thee." None of them knew what the year might bring forth—health, strengtb, prosperity, or loss, sickness, adversity, hut ot this they might be sure, that whatever they met with, they would not meet it alone. God would he with them, and the cultivation of the sense of the nearness of God was the privilege of every one of them.</p>
        <p>The Wycliffe motto for 1913 "Thy will be done as in Heaven so on Earth (Matthew vi, 10) was the subject of the evening discourse. The preacher observed that when he was asked by the deacons of the church to suggest a motto it occurred to him that he could not do better than suggest this portion of the Lord's Prayer to them so that the mind and heart of the church should be fixed upon the splendid endeavour to bring God's will into the whole province of human life. He pointed out that it was not whist we did that mattered hut how we did it. He did not think the thristiin Church would restore itself to the place it, ought to occupy until it realised more fully what it was here for. Sueely it was the will of God that men and women should be saved from their narrowness, selfishness and wickedness. It was the will Of God that the Christian church should hive eyes to see, hearts to feel, and love to be expressed in glorious acts of human redemption. Mr Viner afterwards conducted the Communion service at which there was a large attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>Religious Outlook.</head>
        <p>The Religious Outlook.</p>
        <p>Preaching at Golborne-street Baptist Church on Sunday evening on "The rejigious and social outlook for 1913," the Rev H Ogden took for his text. Deuteronomy xvi., 20, " That which is altogether just shalt thou follow, that thou mayest live." He said that in the religious outlook, organised Christianity bad little room for boast. There was no part of the Church that is growing, save the Roman Catholic, and Protestants could not and did not regard that progress, as an advance. Roman Catholicism was alien to English. character. The growth of a sane agnosticism would be more welcome to some of them. As Baptists, they were 2,000 less in number than last year. We had in these islands sitting accommodation for 1,500,000 and a membership of 410,000.1 hat made patent that there was ample elbow room and space to grow, and what was true of Baptists was equally true of the sister church the Congregationalists. In fact it was true of all the Free Churches and even the Establishedi Church. People were not attending church S, any better in 1912 than in 1911, and there was no immediate prospect of an improvement for 1913., Where was the fault and who was to blame ? "Oh andsaid yet r i some" t,11,1,eb ePreacher ohro itihe declaredpre a preaching is the e wrong,": averagerng preaching was never higher in our Free Churches. The, root of the matter lay in the changed outlook. Twenty' or 30 years ego it was the admitted custom in all respect- able families to go to church or chapel. Such plays as, Stanley Houghton's "The younger Generation," which' shocked and confounded all Wesleyan customs and/ prejudices. Shaw's " Superman" where Jack Tanner was compelled to be worried by the super- woman; "Hindle Wakes " where woman was allowed to share the licence of a man, and expounded practically that what was good for the gander couhl not be bad for the goose. Added to that the press, the library, the stage, the street, had joined. together to affirm that church-es had not a monopoly" of religion and that worship of God was not hound up . with pulpits and pews. Views that were now pro- claimed from the house top were not whispered in the cellar 30 or 40 years ago. The man who had dared would have become an outlaw and all his books would, have been upon the social index. \Ve could not roll' back the ocean of change. The waters simply curlede and laughed at our feet and would soul engulf us whilst we shouted " Thus far and no farther shalt thou go." The times demanded an intelligent study, because Free Churchmen could PityPs t7hneuiu church C at ht al le° Cnohturcahndsaidwo" uAI dl w anyost t hsea ys,amaes.,, the forgot nothing end learnt nothing. If men could not, be conquered by love they were not, worth winning by, threats. The banner of Free Churchmen was to lead; men, not dragoon them by threats of Purgatory. The preacher made a strong plea for the interest in Sun- day School work. He believed he quoted accurately' 7hen he said that the 3,000 increase of Roman Catholics Liverpool during the last ten years was attributable' their care for the young. At a perilously early age, they had been admitted to Communion, and there train- ing had been carefully and sedulously fostered. The best investment of a church was the Suridey School, and the very best we poesessed ought to be found there. The Sunday school and church must be transformed from a Sunday institution to a week day need; the hallowing of the buildings by making them the larger home for the people must affect our times, but the greatest need of all was is craving by tlie people for a larger vision and the satisfaction of deeper needs. In, the mass the world was never better. The hewers of wood and drawers of water were coming to their own.' There was daybreak everywhere in the social realm as, a brief survey would reveal.</p>
        <p>w, in to</p>
      </div>
