      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</head>
        <p>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</p>
        <p>Three successive victories have placed us in ft most dignified position in the league table. The twelfth on the list! with a possibility Of creeping a little higher after to-days' engagement? One of the supporters even claimed that a position in the honoured four was not outside the bounds of possibility. There's nothing like being optimistic, but 'tis rather a tall order!</p>
        <p>It is quite evident that Warrington are more fit home wben performing in a sea of mud. The result of the last five engagements is ample proof of the truth of such a statement. Have we not vanquished Leigh, Leeds,- Rune ,rn and Coventry, and been unluckily defe4ted at Oldham under conditions which are decidedly unco •genial and against good football? The credit is eiitirely due to the forwards, who have admirably adapted themselves to the conditions and their relentless energy, has led up to a big crop of points Eight more fell to their lot on Saturday out of the fifteen scored at Coventry</p>
        <p>Rarely if ever has a game been played under such vile conditions as prevailed at Coventry. Those spectators who braved the elements to watch our " A. " team against Barton will have some idea. But the Coventry ground is not a Wilderspool, and there the conditions were worse. Rain fell pitilessly right throughout the game and pools of water lodged in portions of the ground. Biith the players and the noble 500 who were present heaved a sigh of relief when the whistle blew for time. Warrington were determined that Johnston who once wore a Warrington jersey should not have the pleasure of consoling us in the event of our downfall. He was there to assist in that end, but a Warring- tonian who was present at the match declares be did not know he was playing) he wits so out of the picture. This is rather hard criticism for a player who was so much in the limelight only a few seasons ago. The game was mainly confined to the forwards but in order to break the monotony of things one of our backs had the audacity to score a try. Tranter broke the sequence with an effort under the posts. It came about in this way. The ball was passed out by the halves, and the Coventry defenders were paying attention to Renwick with the result that Tyanter " foignted " to pass and had a clear run in for Renwick to maiirise.</p>
        <p>-- The other two tries were secured by G Thomas and Cox. The former scrambling over at the corner. Quite the feature of the game was the wonderful goal kicked by Thomas from near the touch line. It was really a fine effort and roused even the dejected spectators to make a cheer. Renwick also added the goal from the try by Cox which came in the closing stages as a result of a forward rush. Apart from the excellent work of the Warrington forwards was the brilliant display of Daintith. Ue was the most prominent back on the field being ever in the thick of the struggle in the mud. Nicholas rendered very able assibtance, his fielding of the slippery ball was very commendable. Coventry had a hard working set of forwards but 'were inferior to Warrington. The backs were not at all safe with the exception of Parkinson who played a sound game at full back. The debit against his side would have been greater but for his safe fielding and tackling.</p>
        <p>The game at Wilderspool against Barton was quite pantomimic from a spectatois point of view, but not so for the players, who were continually slipping an&amp; wallowing in the mud. Under the circumstances play suffered and the locals won a poor game by eleven points to nil. Brooks made his re-appearance but he had evidently had enough at the interval and took no further p trt in the game. The new forward Pounce created a far nirable impression.</p>
        <p>To-day Warrington will be fully extended in their engagement with St Helens. We have already beaten them at St. Helens in a mid week match.</p>
        <p>There will be a been struggle for supremacy but I have a sneaking regard for our chances in view of the improved form recently shown. Our forwards ought to be good enough for the Glassblowers who have been rather erratic of late. The team will be the same es at Coventry. Full hack;-Jolley threequarters, Williams,Renwick,Tranter and Davies; half backs, Dainteth mid Nicholas; forwards, G Thomas, Skelhorn, Chesters, Fernley, Cox and It Thomas.</p>
        <p>The A Team to make the journey to St Helens is : Full back, Jordan ; threequarters, Welsby, Hudson, Mclntyre, Clare ; half backs, Brown and Douglas; forwards, Naylor, Harmer, Arnold, Bevan, Gibbs and Critchley.</p>
