      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL. Results of Saturday's Matches.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL. Results of Saturday's Matches.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T P. Broughton R Salford 1 311 Leigh 0 1 3 Wigan Oldham 2 725 Halifax 0 1 3 Warrington Huddersfield Bradford St Helens 1 517 Wakefield T 0 1 3 Keighley Leeds 2 4 16 Dewsbury 0 0 0 Batley Widnes 1 4 14 Bramley Warrington's Easy Win at Coventry. Warrington journeyed to" the City of Three Spires" on Saturday and after a trying game on a very heavy and water logged ground, achieved a decisive victory. Owing to the wretched weather the attendance numbered barely 500. The home side was at full strength and the Warrington back division was improved by the return of Daintith to the half-baok position. Teams : Warrington—Jolley, back; Williams, Renwick, Tranter and Davies, threequarters ; Nicholas and Daintith, half-backs; G Thomas, Cheaters, Skelhorne, Fearnley, R Thomas and Cox, forwards. Coventry—Parkinson, back; Houghtoa,Davis,Thomas and Littlewood,threequarters ; Ruddock and Battersby, half-backs; Oldham, Steel, Beaver, Johnson, Brown and Naylor, forwards. Referee, Mr GI Moore, Runcorn. The Warrington forwards had no difficulty in securing control of the packs and play was soon forced to the Coventry end, Parkinson having his hands full. The rear line got its passing machinery into action,Nicholas repeatedly gaining possession and passing to Daintith, hut for a time nothing came of these movements. Williams kicked when a good opening presented itself and was consequently beaten in the race for the ball, Phil Thomas saving the situation. Coventry were still penned in their own quarters and Tranter, receiving , from Nicholas, was pulled down within n yard of the line. The Warrington forwards rushed the ball and R Thomas, Cox and Fearnley had hard luck in not opening the score. The home backs next made determined efforts which were, however, frustrated by Renwick and Jolley. A subsequent passing movement started from the centres gave Davis an opportunity to execute a good run. Parkinson cleared well, but the visitors kept up the attack, for Treater, pickingup from some loose play, brushed his opponents aside and romped over. Renwick added the extra points. Following the restart from the centre the locals had a look in at the Warrington end, and Johnston when ening strongly was pulled up. Later Nicholas and Daintith again paved the way to a score, and their good work being sec,nded by the forwards, a chance came to George Thomas, who completed the try in fine style. The same player added the goal. .and at the interval Warrington led by two goals two tries (ten points) to Coventry nil. On resuming, Coventry forced matters and actually took the ball over, but the try was disallowed for glaring off-side play. The Warrington passing fell off and the forwards also slackened the pace. Several dashes by the home forwards were met in daring style by Jolley, who kicked with precision. Ty short stages the Wirepullers " again got the upper hand, and following this invasion the Coventry men never threatened danger. Daintith and Tranter missed easy chances through the slippery nature of the ball. Near the close the ball was forced over, Cox making a dive for it and scoring the try in a pool of water. Renwick converted and the visitors retired winners by three goals three tries (15 points) ; Coventry, nil.</p>
        <p>BROUGHTON RANGERS v. RUNCORN: A PENALTY THE ONLY SCORE.</p>
        <p>Under wretched conditions the above match was decided at Wheaters' Field, a mere handful of spectators. The game throughout was principally con- fined to the forwards, and in what handling was seen the Rangers had the pull, but they were met by a very sound defence. It was in the initial half that the Rangers obtained the penalty goal that won the match, Davidson &amp;ding when the visitors were penalised well in their own "25." The concluding portion was prac- tically a repetition of the first, the forwards holding sway, but no further scoring took place. Result : Broughton Rangers, I goal (2 points) ; Runcorn, nil. Teams and officials : Broughton Rangem—Danson, back; Davidson, Barnes, Barlow and Vinton, three-quarter backs; Me id and Jones, half backs; Beetham, Gorry, Hurst, Roberts, Cheetbam, and G L Ciampitt, forwards. Runcorn—Shaw, back; Thornton, A Hughes, Steven- son and Wand, three-quarter backs; B Hughes and Watton, half backs; Inman, Peacock, dlaasey,Donoghue, Rawlinson and Tickle, forwards. Referee, Li Edmondson, Bradford. Touch judges, M D Jones, Wigan, and A Lee, Leigh.</p>
        <p>WIDNES v BRAMLEY : SMART COMBINATION</p>
        <p>At Widnes, before a very poor attendance. Widnes combined well, and were much superior to their opponente in all departments, the home forwards getting possession almost every time. Early on Reid scored after the ball had been handled by Noon, J O'Garra and Taylor. The latter failed at the goal kick. Reid again crossed the lino after a smirtly combined effort by Noon and J O'Garra. Brassington failed at the goal kick. The third try went to the credit of J O'Garra, who scored under the poste after a fine run down the centre of the field from half-way. Taylor improved. In the second half S Aspey Scored from a strong forward movement, and a similar try was secured by Thorpe for Bramley. Result:—Widnes 1 goal 4 tries (14 points), Bramley 1 try (3 points). Teams and officials:—</p>
        <p>Widnes.—Brassington, back; Raid, Taylor Kinsella, and Laws, three-quarter backs; Noon and 1 O'Garra, half backs; S Aspey, Corcorran, Cosgrove, Lloyd, Higgins, and II CrUarra, forwards. Brarcley.—Brabam, back; Turton, T Dinsdale, J Dinsdale and Townsend, three-quarter backs; Abbey and Midgley, half hacks • McManus, Thorpe, Unsworth, Walsh, Dibb and Haley:forwards. Referee, GI, Dickenson, Halifax. Touch judges, W Cross, St Helens, and G C Dickenson, Flixton.</p>
        <p>ST HELENS V WAKEFIELD TRINITY: TRINITY</p>
        <p>OVERPLAY-i 6:</p>
        <p>For theirkLeague game at St Hele-ns,Wakeflepldiaw yeerrs.e without several of their most prominent Wakefield for several minutes took up an attacking position on the home line. Newbould broke away and gave to Smales. and he to Clay, who grounded at the coiner, but G Taylor failed to improve. St Relens burst away, and Waddell scored from Barton's transfer, Molyneux failing. From passing between_ Kelly, Richardson, and Belshaw, the latter scored an unimproved try. Changing ends St Helens broke away, and Belshaw gave to Prescott, who scored, Molnenx failing. Belshaw next crossed witlran unimprovt3d try after a brilliant round of passing, and in the last few minutes the whole of the home backs passed, and Kelly eluding Stevenson, scored at the corner. Molyneux's shot at goal was successful. Result :—St Helens 1 goal 5 tries (17 points), Wakefield Trinity 1 try (3 points). reams and officials : St Helens—Molyneux, Barton, back; Flanagan, Belshaw and U Kelly, three-quarter backs; Richardson and Browning; half backs ; Waddell, Wilcock, Heaton, Parr, Manchester and Prescott, forwards. Wakefield Trinity—Land, back • Clay,Lyncb,Poynton, and Stephenson, three-quarter backs ; Newbould and Smales, half backs; McNally, Burton, Johnson, Taylor, Beattie, and Crossland, forwards. Referee, W H Wood, Huddersfield. Touch-judges, Bennett, Liverpool. and J Blaokburn, Leigh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Tith 6utIIBINATION.</head>
        <p>Tith 6utIIBINATION.</p>
        <p>Pemberton Wigan Leigh 3 4 18 Salford 0 0 0 Halifax 2 3 13 Old Lam 1 2 8 Leigh Shamrocks 0 2 6 Rochdale H 0 0 0 Featherstone Dewsbury Normauton St J... 2 4 16 Hull Purston Hull Kingston ... 1 2 8 Beverley . 0 1 3</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES. WARRINGTON "A" v. BARTON.</p>
        <p>This match attracted only a very small gate to the Wilderspool ground. Brooks re-appeared on the home side after a month on the injured list. A trial was also given to a young forward named Pounce, from the Wigan district who promises well. The visitors were early in difficulties, for following on a weak clearance by the custodian,Douglas crossed the line for Critchley to add the extra points. The visitors rallied and mainly through the smartness of their half-backs Warrington's teak was not an easy one. A line effort by Jordan placed Warrington further ahead, but this time Critchley struck the uprights from a difficult angle. At half-time the home side led by one goal two triai (eight points) to nil. Turning round, the Barton backs played up pluckily and repelled many dangerous movements. The second stage wns well advanced before anything tangible resulted, and smart work by Douglas led up to the closing score, the half-back crossing for his second try. Result :—Warrington " A," one goal three tries (eleven paints) ; Barton, nil.</p>
      </div>
