      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Tho Examiner Saturday January 11,1913,</head>
        <p>Tho Examiner Saturday January 11,1913,</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>NORTH•CHESBIRE LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION. RESULTS TO SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Goals.</p>
        <p>t P. W. L. D. For Ag'st PO Lostook Gralam Lymm ' Mobbarley 11... 5... 4... 2... 18... 19... 12 Bowdon Vale Barnton Villa Oughtrington Park 10... 4... 4... 2... 13... 14... 10 Broadheath Orightriogton Amat 11... Sm. 6... 2... 21... 30... 8 Latchford St Vincent's l3 3... 9... 1... 17... 30... 7 Altriucham Lads 8 0... 5... 3... 11... 19... 3</p>
        <p>Sr. VINCENT'S V OtIGIITRIN'GTON AMATEURS. At Stamford Park on Saturday. In the early stages the Amateurs showed the better football and Davies opened their account by easily defeating McPenland. The Saints retaliated, and a promising movement was brought to a close by Coornbes shooting past from a centre by Smith. After a good run and centre by Gresty and Burrows on the visitors' right, Owen again defeated McPartland with a fast shot. No fur,her scoring took place before the interval, when the score was, Oughtrington Amaieurs, two goals; St Vincent's. nil. After the interval F Owen with a long shot placed the Amateurs further ahead. Aroused by this reverse the Saints applied pressure and Coombes opened the home team's score. After a spell of even play a com- bined run by the visitors' forwards ended in Davies scoring the fourth point for the visitors. After some good work by Ross and Salt, Coombes put in a sharp run and again reduced the lead, whilst a few minutes later Hennerley raised the hopes of his side by notch- ing a third. With only a goal separating the contest- ants and five minutes to play, the game became exciting Result: Oughtrington Amateurs, 4 goals ; St Vincent'u 3 goals.</p>
        <p>BARNTON VILLA v. LATCHFORD. At. Barnton on Saturday under miserable conditions. On the‘general run of the game Barnton showed the best form, but half-time arrived with the score sheet blank. Latchford forced matters on resuming, but found the home defence firm. Webb after an ie.dividual effort extended Carter to the full with a good shot. Play was transferred to the other end, and from a per- fect centre by Capper, Cotton opened the score for Barnton with a good header. Aroused by this reverse, Latchford applied pressure, and Sharps did well to prevent Taylor equalizing. Even play followed for a time. Gradually the home side asserted their superi- ority and by the time this half was 20 minutes old Bowyer further increased their lead. In the closing stages Latchford had the best of matters and tried desperately to reduce the lead, but their efforts were not rewarded, the Barnton defence withstanding all attacks. Final: Barnton Villa, 2 goals ; Latchford, 0</p>
        <p>BOWDON VALE V. BROADHEA.Tri. At Vicarage-tane, Bowdon, on Saturday. Result: Bowdon Vale. one goal; Broadheath, nil.</p>
        <p>, LOSTOCK GRALAM V. MOBBERLEY. At Lostook Gtalam on Saturday. Score: Lostook Gralam, 8 goals; Mobberley nil.</p>
        <p>SECOND DINUSIOI4.</p>
        <p>RESULTS TO SA.TURDAY. P. •W. L. 1). for agst Pts Weaver RefiningCo 9... 6... 0... 3... 23... 6... 15 Stockton Heath Latchford Recss 10.. 4... 5... 1... 23... 14... 9 Padgate Amateurs Cadishead Wesleyan 6... 3... 1... 2... 13... 9... 8 Latchford Reserve 7... 2... 3... 2... 12... 9... 6 Latchford Britannia 5... 2... 3... 0... 8... 14... 4 Warrington CL B</p>
        <p>LAT-CHFORD RECREATION V WEAVER REFINERS. At Latchford. Play opened keen and in favour of the Rees, Vaughan (Weaver) repeatedly being called upon to clear his lines, Woodward checked Cherratt and Willcox when a score seemed certain. A grand passing run by the [teas' forwards met wi%l2 reward, Hawthorn- opening their score with a good effort. Up to the interval the Recreation had the best of matters, but failed to add to their score. They even failed to benefit by the award of a couple of penalties. Half-time : Latchford Recreation, one goal; Weavers Refiners, nil. In the second half the visitors were the better side and through Evans and Ellis scored twice. The Rees, failing to respond, bad to admit defeat With tbe score :—Weaver Refiners, two goals; Latchford Recreation, one goal.</p>
        <p>WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE. I GREAT SANKEY V IALFOUR-ROAD OLD BOYS. Great interest was centred in this match at Penketh on Saturday and, notwithstanding the miserable weather, there was an excellent attendance. Sankey were soon on the attack, and T Cooke beat Stre s with were shot which rebounded off the post. Not disc uraged by this revers!, the visitors played up splendidly, the right wing making a fine effort to get on level terms, but B J Kelly cleared, the ball going to W Booth, who, in attempting to head it, collided with Hughes, and bad to leave the field. E Forster was badly handi- oapped, having to manage the left wing himself, but he got through on his own, the ball just going outside.. T Whitfield worked a nice plsition which ended in E Darlington scoring with a fast, low shot. The visiting forwards showed some nice footwork, but E Glover, who was playing an excellent game, olearea with a long kick. Interval :—Great Sankey, two goals; Balfou:- road Old Boys, nil. In the second half E Forster scored with a splendid overhead kick. E Forster added another goal a few minutes later, but the point was disallowed. At the other end Clarke sent in a magnificent shot which J Davenport saved in masterly fashion. In the closing stages Sankey showed their superiority, and only the visiting defence kept them from adding to their score. Result :—Great Sankey, three goals; Balfour-road Old B)ys, nil. ORFORD-ST JOHN'S V ORFoRD WHITE STAR. Orford White Star were the victors in this match by one goal to nil. This is the first defeat of the season for Orford St John's. C Harrison, T Hillage, B Harding and T Jackson played a grand game for the winners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>TEAMS TO-DAYS.</head>
        <p>TEAMS TO-DAYS.</p>
        <p>EMM ANUEL CHURCH INSTITUTE V. FARLESTOWN Y.M.C.A. At Eirlestown train from Bank Quay Statioo nt 151 p.m Team: I' Knight, T Williams, S Hughep, S Banner, T Hughes, W Ayres, E E Ashby, W Knight, E Roberts, S Roberts, S Potter. Reserve: R Clarke. ADULT SCHOOL WORKING MEN'S ATHLETIC.-At Victoria Park, meet at Emmanuel Institute at 2.15 p.m. Teams A N Other, S Leigh. T Leigh, Stewart, T Williams, Barrow, Baker, Warburton, Webo, Dykes, Rowe. Reserve: Jackson. Result January 4th.—Stockton Heath 6 goals; Adult School 2 goals. • WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE.</p>
        <p>GREAT.SANKEY V RUNCORN ALBION.-At Runcorn: —Great Sankey Team: J Davenport, goal ; E Glover, Jun., E Glover Ben., full backs; R Stephens, B J Kelly. H Payne, half backs •, E Darlington, T Whitfield, T Cook (captain), E Forster, F Millingtnn, forwards. Reserves, R Clatworthv, U Ray. Train leaves Warrington Central at 1.10, Sankey 1.15 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>The first eleven won a fast game at Northern by 8-3. Minards scored twice and Knight from left half after a clever dribble ran into the circle and scored a remark able goal before the home backs had settled down- Before the interval Minards made a splendid solo run and scored a fourth and Ashcroft netted for Northern with a good follow-up effort, Bulb( having no chance. In the second half Northern played up much better and made the score 4-3, but Minards scored three' times and Marsden once for the visitors before the end. Minards played a great game hi the centre, putting up a notable performance in scoring six goals and he, Marsden and Hobin made a good inside trio. Isherwood and Dale flung across some strong centres and Bradshaw and Knight were the pick of the defence, their stickwork beiug very neat. The second eleven won 4-2. They had the assistance of Pearson who pliyed with his customary dash and vim and scored two fine giAls. Banks and Laver also scoring once. The game was a most interesting one, first Sale and then Latchford having the advantage. Sale played very hard in the second half, the defensive work of Gibbs, Moll, Rocks, and ZiNixon was sure, though the visitors had hard lines several times in not getting through, their forwards finishing rather badly. The Sale full-backs hit hard under pressure. Laver and Colton for Latchford on the extreme wings played in their best form. The first eleven v South Manchester at home to-day is :—J Bruhl, goal • W Fletcher, C hacks; C H Morgan, A E:Bradshaw, A Knight, halves • R A Dale, J E Pearson, W H Minards, D Travis, F W Isher- wood, forwards. Referee :—Mr Cobb. Sully-off 2.15 prompt. The second eleven v. Stockport A at Heaton Moor cricket ground is :—H Wright, goal: W T Eccles, T Dixon, backs; W Mull, H T Gibbs, T Ranks, halves; G Laver, F Hobin, E Banks, A G Marsden, HR Colton, forwards. Train from Arpley Station 1.14 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>Britain's Land of Promise. A week from Liver- r pool. Golden opportunities for competent ago. 4 culturists who can quickly become land- 4 lords. Dairying, cattle raising, fruit 0 growing, gardening, unlimited market. too million acres virgin soil as. per • , acre—easy terms. Improved fOliv 11.- * freehold farms can be " Grand , bought for what climate. , British farmers 1,,,, Thousands 10 , ...,0 VOOP. 1)::t• , 14. P - . peaches. Dr I Lcarried jun. 7 0 kcx. nric,aitional sociasl zrndfietmoanis.e . domesticl.t PrTheect Government f guarantee to place 'competent men and ) Yomen• Marvellous natural resottrces • awaiting investors. Write Mr. N. B. Colcock, Ontario Government Agent. ; 163, Strand, London. , '</p>
