      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>"Robinson Crusoe.",</p>
        <p>Both young and old keeply anticipate th,p visit of a, pantomime and, seeing that such form of entertain- ment usually comes but once a year, it is essential, if patrons are to be pleased, to enlist the services of a company capable of keeping up the good reputation of the "house." The ever-enterprising management of the Hippodrome have secured for this week Mr E C Jazon gorgeous comic pantomime, "Robinson Crusoe " presented by Messrs M Montgomery and E C Jazon. Judging by the large crowds awaiting admission to both the first and second "houses," the pantomime makes a strong appeal to all tastes. On taifonday night there were exceedingly large audiences and the general opinion of these "first nighters " was that the production was a decided success, although criticism of a mild character was forthcoming from some quarters. There is -an abundance of genuine comedy, specialities, and real good singing; .the dresses are magnificent and the scenery is on the most elaborate scale. The ballets and groupings are very artistic and tastefully executed, although a slight re-arrangement of some of the members could be effected to advantage; irregularities of height and form being very perceptible in the present groupings. The chief characters in this familiar story are in able bands, aud the whole company are successful in their efforts to keep the audience in good humour. kiss May Poulten as" Robinson Crusoe " is a charm- ing principal boy and no matter whether the situation she is involved in is serious or the reverse, she is perfectly at home. The graceful carriage, the sweet voice and the bewitching smile which she possesses are all factors which one and all appreciate. No wonder that at the conclusion she is accorded round upon round of applause as she gaily trips down the palace steps with the sprightly Polly Perkins, a character undertaken with a fair amount of success by Miss Lizzie Pearce, Miss Pearce is a clever dancer and thoroughly captivates. Miss Chrissie Forrester is a delightful singer and the part of Undine is most suitably elk:tut:l to her. In a demure way, pleaeing in itself, she renders a series of charming songs in such a manner that one is apt to forget the comedy just previous to her appearance. The Two Junes appear as Lieutenant Spiite and Margery and given popular music-hall turn combining singing and dancing in an acceptable manner. Then comes the great and prevailing feature, comedy. Boisterous in parts it may be, but taken generally it is of a most pleasing character. Mark Stuart leads the trio of comedy merchants in the part of Will Atkins, the " pirate "of the pantomime. Stuart knows how to please and causes roars of laughter with his "Wibbly Wobbly Walk" number. He is never dull and transforms most successlully the expressions of the audience from a smile to a laugh and thence onward almost ,on to the verge of hysterics. He is most ably assisted by Ted Young and Will Harris who appear as " Dame Crugoe " and "Billy Crusoe ' respectively. Young is equally as successful as Mark Stuart and in parts probably more so, notably "The mossy bank rehearsal" which he conducts along with "Billy Crusoe," who is very popular with his witticism and general turn of good, clean and palatable humour. The Adams, comedy cyclists, figure as Nab and Grabham and in the most uncon- cerned manner perform dangerous and intricate tricks on the bicycle, besides aiding in keeping the ball of humour rolling. Albert Lyon is a clever coon comedian who appears as Friday, incidentally intro- ducing rag-time and some smart buck dancing. In the duet along with the principal he is very successful and fully deserves all the applause lavished on him. The other characters are well portrayed and some clever artistes are included in the ballet. An augmented orchestra is under the able leadership of Mr A E Slack. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. " THE LADY IN RED."</p>
        <p>At the Palace and Hippodrome next week, the Mysterious Lady ia Red will make her first appear- ance before a Warrington audience. The noble entertainer is the possessor of a phenomenal soprano voice, also of one of the most unique contracts known in the variety world. She is always to be allowed the company of a faithful retainer, to wear a red mask and</p>
        <p>"SERENE NORD," the Beautiful Diver, who will appear at the Hippodrome uest week. '</p>
        <p>dress in red: to have the service of a private detec- tive; to enter and leave the theatre try the front entrance; she accepts no bouquets. will not travel by night, and has a guarantee 'that her identity snail at all times be safeguarded. The reason for this stringent contra ct is that the the lady comes of a very highly-placed family on the Continent and through the excesses of an only brother who was an cfEcer in a crack English regiment who ultimately committed suicide and so disgraced the family, that the lady swore no one should see her face again and she became known as the Lady in Red. The other artistes will include "Serene Notd, the beautiful diver.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>CLASSES AND THE MASSES.</head>
        <p>THE CLASSES AND THE MASSES.</p>
        <p>The Rev B Percival entered upon his 21st year as Vicar of St Peter's Church. on Sunday. Preaching in the evening from the church motto of the year, " Let us consider one another" (Heb. x. 24), he deplored the signs of decay in the power of great ideals: as weir evidenced by the decline in public worship, family prayer and .Bible reading. They were said to be Imperialists in these days, and they had been greatly impressed with the magnificent offers of help from their over-seas kinsman; yet their men at home declined to train themselves—,-not for militarism—but to safeguard their homes and their industries, preening greed and pleasure worship to national duty. England might any day suddenly find herself face to face with deadly peril, when preparation would come too late. Everywhere there appeared to be a decline in public worship. Clergy and ministers of all denominations were deploring the fact. What was the remedy ? "Let us consider one another." Let them consider one another socially and industrially. There needed to be a greater intermingling of the masses and the classes. If the classes absolutely held aloof from the masses they must not be surprised if there was a coldness and estrangement. Democracy ruled ant was coming more and more to rule. What were they going to do with the masses ? They must go amongst them if they were to raise them and kfluence them for good. There was something better than the political agitator end the Socialist atheist. There was a message that would raise them morally and materially—it was .the Gospel of Jesus Christ, the gospel of good-will to men. They were face to face with tremendous industrial, political, constitutional and religious problems. How were they to meet them ? "Let: them consider one another." Surely that principle would settle legitimate differences of opinion without resorting to the clump' method of a ruinous strike and give the right apportionment of the fruits of industry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>BEECHAM'S PILLS-</head>
        <p>BEECHAM'S PILLS-</p>
        <p>is to keep your digestive functions active under all circumstances. This is the key to good health. Low spirits, depression, nervous irritability and the like are fre- quently nothing but the consequences of some digestive trouble. If you are to be alert and clear-headed, your brain must receive a constant supply of pure blood. Stomach, liver and bowel ailments militate very much against that, because they give rise to impure blood and consequently impaired nutrition. Taken in accord- ance with directions, Beecham's Pills speedily eliminate impurities from the system, gently but firmly rouse the liver to proper activity, and give tone to the stomach, improving both appetite and digestion. An excellent plan IS TO TAKE</p>
