      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>ANONYMOUS LETTERS.</head>
        <p>ANONYMOUS LETTERS.</p>
        <p>MAYOR COMPLAINS OF ATTEMPTED INTIMIDATION.</p>
        <p>Indignation at the Council Meeting.</p>
        <p>An unusual incident marked the opening of the m-inthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening, the Mayor and several members expressing indignation at the receipt of anoLymous letters which were stigmatised as cowardly and dis- graceful. On the other band, Councillors Pemberton and Gough said that they too had had letters from unknown writers but had found them helpful and encouraging.</p>
        <p>Oa t—akrng the chair, the MAYOR said : I have received this morning an anouymous letter relating to one or two matters, the chief of which is-- but, then, I don't know whether it is necessary for me to mention the exact matter it relates to, but it is certainly very little short of intimidation (Hear, bear.) If the members of this Council cannet do their work honestly and conscientiously without such unholy spurs as the anonymous letter I allude to, I think they are not fit to be here. (Hear, hear.) I am deeply grieved to think that any ratepayer of Warrington should descend to such a low level as to send such a letter as I have received. lam not a.lawyer, but at all events I should say a part of it is libellous if the handwriting could only be identified. I have nothing further to say. Councillor ROBERTS said that, unfortunately the Mayor was not the only member of the Council who bad received a similar dastardly letter. There were other members who had received letters and he could riot help echoing his Worship's sentiments. If that kind of advocacy was to be tolerated it was good-bye to everything decent and respectable in public iife. Councillor WILKINSON also stated that he had received an anonymous letter signed " A Ratepayer." • e wished he had the • writer there. It was a most cowardly thing one could do to send any other man an anonymous letter. "I had one fr,,m a lady's maid," proceeded the councillor, amid some laughter, " a very affectionate letter. She took a deep interest in me because I was at one time kind to her widowed mother. The letter was with reference to a conversa- tion she (the writer) had heard. She said she wrote because she heard that mice had been known to help lions in their time and she wished to help me. It was a little conversation she had heard as a lady's maid. I merely put it down as piffle and disgraceful either to the person who wrote it or dictated it." Councillor PEMBERTON : I may say I have had several anonymous letters. .</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON: Have you got them with you? Councillor PEMBERTON : No; I don't carry them about with me. The ones I have received I have nothing to complain about. They have been very helpful to me in carrying out my public work, and especially in the matter which has -been before the Eaucation Committee; they have encouraged me in every way possible. I have had lots of them. Apparently all the speakers so far have had very dis- agreeable ones. I have had very pleasant ones. Councillor WILKINSON From ladies' maids? Councillor PEMBERTON ; No. I have not the pleasure of the acquaintance of ladies' maids—not being a ladies' man like Councillor Wilkinson. (Laughter). f Councillor BitoADnunsT, producing a letter, said that in spite of that commuLication threatening to crush and throw him upon the scrap heap, he intended to do his duty in the Council. He was not going to be either bullied or to permit letters of such description to interfere with the manner in which he should vote</p>
        <p>in tho Council. He was the representative of a certain number of people, and as long as he had the honour of being their tepresentative he was going to vote just as his conscience dictated to him. Alderman SMETHURST said he had received an anonymous letter, which was a very disgraceful one. After 21 years' service he was to be subjected to abuse from a cur and the sum of the country. If the writer could be found he (the Alderman) would spend some money to put him where he ought to be—in goal. Councilior Goixat : have received an anonymous letter and I feel somewhat encouraged by it. I think we ought to thank these people for. watching the rate- payers' representatives, .and seeing that they are trying to carry out the will of the people. Alderman SMETHURST : Let them put their names to the letters.</p>
      </div>
