      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <p>QUERIES AND ANSWERS. OUR READERS are invited to hend legal queries to be answered week by week. Inquiries must be short, concise, and plainly written, and the name and address of the writer given to show that the inquiry is genuine. The replies are by a member of the profession, and every effort is made to secure accuracy, but we cannot assume any responsibility for the opinion or advice given. Mark all envelopes enclosing queries "Legal,' and address them to. the EDITOR EXAMINER,' WARKINGTON. If you do not wish initials to be used in the reply please suggest a itom-cle-plume.</p>
        <p>The proprieto</p>
        <p>GAMP.—You have no remedy. undertook no responsibility. HENRY.—Yes, the magistrates can bear the case. COMPANY.—(I) By an Annual Meeting is meant one meeting in each year! You cannot hold two meetings in one year and none the next. (2) Yes, a Director may also act as Secretary and sign cheques as such. 11.N.A.—It largely depends on the custom in the trade, but in absence of a well-recognized custom we should say three months' notice. ENQUIRER.—Height of blinds from pavement is often regulated by local bye-laws; but we think this would not save the shopkeeper from having to pay damages where injury was sustained by: e.g. an un- commonly tall man. It is however a debatable point. BARBER.—Tha onus of proving "foul shave" is on the customer—but there is unfortunately rather a prejudice against the barber in the minds of jurymen, and the fact of having been shaved by the defendant about the time when the rash broke out seems to be regarded almost as incontrovertible evidence of liability. Employ the best doctor you can get.</p>
        <p>k , t OUR., : \ iii „Ito --I k -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>"TUMBLING IN RUINS."</head>
        <p>"TUMBLING IN RUINS."</p>
        <p>MRS. A. H. CROSFIELD ON FOOD</p>
        <p>TAXERS' FOLLY.</p>
        <p>FACTS FOR THE WORKERS.</p>
        <p>Mrs. A. H. Crosfield has addressed tlø following striking letter to the Liberal women of Warrington, through the indefat- igable secretary of their association, • Mrs. Osborne:— Dear Mrs. Osborne,—l hear of great activity just now among the men of our party, and I am sure you will feel with me that this is a time when we women too should be up and doing. For it looks as if the Food Taxers would soon have their house tumbling in ruins about them, hand and give a hearty welcome to those one-time and therefore I think we sbould hold out a friendly</p>
        <p>• opponents of ours who are fleeing from an im-</p>
        <p>possible situation. Let us invite the large number of people -who have been misled by the artifices, and fanfare of the Tariff Reformers to bethink themselves whether it is not time for them to</p>
        <p>throw over these Food Taxes as so many promi- nent, people in their own party are doing. Let us point out that many of the weightiest and Most influential Conservative newspapers—papers like the "Times." the "Daily Telegraph," the "Spec- tator," the "Saturday Review," and the "Sunday Times," in London; the "Liverpool Courier," the "Manchester Courier," the "Yorkshire Post," the "Glasgow Herald," the "Nottingham Guar- dian," and others in the provinces—are turning their backs on the policy of the Food Taxers, and that a large majority of even the Tory, Mem- • bers of Parliament are doing the same. After all they are only following in the footsteps of others, and those the m6st eminent members of the Con- servative Party who would not stand or tolerate this infamous, iniquitous attempt to "gamble with the people's food"—as Lord Goshen, one of the most distinguished among them, described the Birmingham policy. He and Lord James of Hereford, Lord Balfour of Burleigh, Lord George Hamilton, Mr. Ritchie, and last but most impor- tant of all, the late and universally respected Duke of Devonshire, stood out against their own Party rather than support Mr. Chamberlain's monstrous and reckless proposals. Let us also point out how one after another the Tariff Reformers' statements have been exposed to ridicule, and riddled by criticism which has shown them to be utterly false and .worthless. They invited us to let the foreigner pay our taxes. We asked if 'there was a single Protec- tionist country where the foreigner was ' so oblig- ing as to do it. We pointed out to the Bread Riots and storming of shops in Germany and Austria• by men and women, who were furious at the famine prices charged for bread and meat. Their anger no doubt should have been vented not on the shopkeeper but on the political parties and responsible statesmen who with their Protective duties created these artificially high prices. But what particularly concerns us women is the fact that the housewives of Austria and Germany and other protected countries have learned by very bitter experience that it is they themselves and nobody else that pay these food taxes. Then they told us that the foerigner would take our trade if we did not adopt Protection. If we bought what it suited our Manufacturers and our- selves to buy from abroad, our bread winners, they said, would lose their occupation. Well, we are . still buying as ferely as ever just what we find it convenient') to purchase. Has this cost us our trade? No; we have more trade than ever, and are busier than ever. The Tariff Reformers, led by Mr. Chamberlain, warned us that without Pro- tection our trade would go,—and it has gone, but not where they meant. They meant, and said, our trade would go to ruin. It has, on the con- trary gone to markets all over the world, and never was British trade so large and powerful and flourishing as to-day. • They talked their parrot cry of "More Employ- ment," and we told them it was nonsense to pre- tend people could buy more with everything dear- er. Naturally, they would have to do with less and buy less.; less food, less clothing. \ And so our manufacturers would have less demand, less to produce, and therefore less and not more to pay in wages. Then the Tariff Reformers tried to make party capital for their movement out of the Colonies, and occasionally even Colonial statesmen. But now we have the Colonies and their leaders one after another repudiating with surprise, and some- thing like indignation, the idea. that the hard.= pressed poorer people of our towns and cities should feel the pinch of greater poverty so as to add to the prosperity of well-to-do farmers in Canada. Canadian statesmen will have none of and the answer from other Colonies is just the</p>
        <p>same. And so the Tariff Reforrners have been proved wrong all along the line, and how then can we trust them?, How can we trust the "Die-hards" and "Confederates" who have been the leaders— may I not say ringleaders—in this conspiracy? It would be madness to do so. Free Trade is the question which above all others specially and vitally concerns women, be- cause the health and decent comfort of the people of our country, young and old, men and women, depend so largely on their vigilance. And there- fore let us use the far-reaching influence which women alteady possess in politics, and exert our- selves' in defence of a great and noble 'cause, which in such a vital sense is our own cause. As long as there remains one woman in Warrington who is still deluded by Tariff Reform quackery, there is still work for us to do.—Yours very sin- cerely, DOMINI CROSFIELD. 2 Rue Saigon, Paris,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Christmas Tree.</head>
        <p>Christmas Tree.</p>
        <p>Op Thursday evening the Christmas tea in con- nection with the Women's Bible Class, Workingmen's Mission, Thewlie-street, was held in the schoolroom. There was a good attendance, about 100 ladies sitting down to an excellent tea. The after-proceedings were presided over by Mrs Sherburn, of Stockton Heath. when various items were rendered by the members of the class, including a solo by Miss Bherburn. After- mirda, the members, who number 145 indulged in games. Ask for Whitbread's Famous Ales and Stouts in— screw-bottles.—Sole agent, PETER LEIGH, Hone- market st.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <p>. Mrs. A. H. Crosfield MRS. CROSFIELD'S has a direct and - STRIKING LETTER. homely way of setting forth the "bread and i butter" aspect of the fiscal question, with which the women are so vitally concerned, and the striking letter from her pen, Which we publish in another part of this issue, should be read by those of the workers who desire further enlightenment on Protection and its disastrous consequences to the homes. But Mrs. Crosfield does not confine herself to the' mere negative aspect of the question. She shows that, in spite of ',all the jeremiads of the tariff reformers, Free Trade has con- tinued to spell boundless prosperity to our industries—in fact it is the foundation and main spring of our national well being.</p>
        <p>Mr A H Crosfield's MR. CROSFIELD article in another AND THE TORY column throws a strong • PLIGHT. light on the pitiable position in which the Tory Party finds itself in its desperate efforts to find some means, however ignoble, of scrambling back to office. Aptly does he describe those.professing politicians as "quick change" artistes who, like all performers with an eye to the gallery, are most accom- modating in the production of new " turns " and the dropping of old -ones in the hope of capturing popular favour. When Mr. Chamberlain first launched his Protec- tionist proposals he was frank enough to admit that it would be impossible to give the Colonies a preference without putting a tax on food. Although some of his most eminent followers refused to identify them- selves with such a policy, food taxation has until recently remained an integral part of tariff reform, as preached by the Tory party. But latterly the conviction has dawned upon a powerful section of the Unionist press that the British workingman will never consent to .have his food made dearer for any imaginary commercial gain, and so the scribes of Carmelite House have been busily clamouring for the abandonment of this most unpopular part of the Tory programme. Mr. Bonar Law sought to find a way out of the difficulty by cynically throwing upon the Colonies the responsibility of saying that the food supply of the mother country shall be taxed. But, as Mr Crosfield points out, Colonial opinion is dead against that amazing proposal and one influential newspaper goes so far as to declare We never asked, nor do we desire that women and children living under well-nigh hopeles9 conditions in the slums of London, Manchester, and Liverpool shall pay more for their bread and clothing in order that we may prosper by their impovrishment, nor can we offer any hope that our markets will in the future be more open to British manufactures than they have been hitherto. Mr, Crosfield scathingly condemns the placing of tactics before principle which is the distinguishing feature of present day Conservatism, and his letter presents a ; formidable indictment of which his political opponents will fi'd it impossible to clear themselves.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY JANUARY 11, 1913.</p>
        <p>Those politicians and PROTECTIONISTS journalists who are PREDICAMENT. quarrelling with each other, not really as to whether they believe in food taxes or not, but as to whether a food tax policy is good electioneering business, will find something that should cause them to "think furiously' in the "Accounts Relating to the Trade and' Navigation of the United Kingdom," issued from the offices of the Board rof Trade yesterday. Whatever may now be represented as the basis of the Tariff Reform movement, it would not become Liberals to forget that the greatest imposture since the time of the South Sea Bubble was founded upon the re- presentation that our trade was decaying and that our industries were dying out. This in- dustry was "going," and that was "gone," was the contention that Mr Joseph Chamber- lain put forward under corer of fallacious figures. He was splendidly supported. Con - servative newspapers throughout the country Published maps of our industrial rivers, show- ing whether this or the other industry had died out and left a blank. These maps seemed to indicate a general dissolution. And why? For two reasons. There was no explanation why any industry had removed from one part of the country to another in order that it might be carried on under more favourable circumstances, and the spaces that had been covered by new shipyards, factories, engineering work", and what not were left blank. The public was asked to believe that many of the main -Centres of industries were deserted because for example, a glass-blowing factory has been deserte,d or some chemical works had been removed to a place at which salt was more easily obtain- able. The fact that the spaces left blank had been filled up by more important and more modern industries, as well as all the spaces between them, was carefully con- cealed from the minds of the uninquiring. That is an indication of the spirit and a measure of the honesty with which the Tariff Reform agitation has been carried on. As the English people are in the main sane and reasonable, how can there be any wonder that the Conservative Party has been entangled in inextricable confusion by Tariff " Reform " ? No prophecies ever made since the time of the Witch of Endor have ever gone so foolishly wrong as those of the Tariff Reformer. It has gone worst of all with the ' prophecies of Mr Joseph Chamberlain. We do not stop to inquire whether he himself believed in them. We merely point to their refutation. It is nearly ten years since he began to insist that we were a ruined nation, that the tides of commercial and industrial prosperity were leaving us, that our place in the world was one of growing insignificance. What are the facts to-day? This country has never stood at the top of a higher wave of prospe •ity than at the present moment. All the trades that were ' going " or " gone " have reached the maximum of success. All our industries, large or small, from the tailor's shop to the cotton factory, are running full time or over, In five years our exports have risen from something over £294,000,000 to nearly £600,000,000. During the same period our imports, which include the profits on our shipping and our foreign investments, have risen from £451,000,000 to £744,000,000. Is there any necessity for "rubbing it in " ? The facts speak so clearly for themselves that they do not need to be insisted on. Mr Bonar Law thought that " two bad winters" might give victory to the Tariff Reforlers. That was a possibility, at least. But what extraordinary fatuity was it that made Mr Bonar Law wish for two bad winters?</p>
        <p>A German Chemist, LIGHT AND POWER Professor rionig, has OUT OF SLUDGE. perfected a process for manufacturing gas for lighting purposes from town sludge. It is stated that this gas is of greater calorific value than coal gas, and gives three or four times as much ammonia. Works are being constructed in Brunn, Austria, for making the gas by this process and for several months 37 tons of dry sludge have been treated daily. The yield of ammonia is said to be 17 lbs. per ton of dry sludge. If sludge can be converted into light it can be more readily converted into power gas, in the making of which no smoke is evolved. Perhaps we have in this suggestion the prospect of laying the smoke fiend and making money out of the process. We com- mend these ideas to the attention of the chairman of the Street Improvement and Lighting Committee. Why import coals when we have the home article in our streets and back yards which is costly to destroy? Why not give a preference to our Warrington sludge?</p>
        <p>'Cripples' Guild Entertainment. The annual tea, entertainment and Christmas Tree was given to about MO cripples by the members of the Bold-street Cripples' Guild in the Wesleyan schools on Wednesday evening. Tea was served by the Ladies' Committee and afterwards an entertain- ment was provided by the children attending People's College school. In the absence of Mr W H Bellian the Rev Hugh Williamson presided. Toys and other articles were distributed from the Christmas Tree.</p>
        <p>The Exammei, atuiday, January 11 1913.</p>
        <p>Labour in ',his district has sustained a heavy loss by the death of Mr. Thos. Glover, ex M.P., for St.. Helens. He ,was indeed a self-made man, having begun work in the pit at the age of nine years, and his trans- parent sincerity commanded the repect of all parties. * * *</p>
        <p>Three January Milestones : January Ist, 1909: State Pensions of ss. a week first paid to old people of 70 and over. January Ist, 1911: Past receipt of Poor Relief ceased to disqualify for an Old Age</p>
        <p>Pension. January 13th, 1913: Medical, Sickness and Maternity Benefits begin under the Insurance Act.</p>
        <p>* * It is interesting to note that under State nsurance there are no fixed" pay -nights." if a man , fell ill on Tuesday his first ten shillings (if he were at the full rate) should be sent not later than the evening of Satur- day in the next week. If, however, there was any reason to suppose that he was in urgent need of money the society could forward five shillings on, perhaps, the Wednesday. At no time should more than a week 'a benefit be owing. * *</p>
        <p>Lord Deerhurst has been complaining that the Liberal Party " cribbed " Old Age. Pensions from the Unionist Party. Well, a charge of theft is always unpleasant, but the Liberal Party could not " crib" from the Tories what the Tories never possessed. All the Tories ever had in connection with Old Age Pensions was a promise, given in 1895, and broken from 1895 to 1905. What the Liberal Party did was, not to promise pensions, but to see that the old people got them.</p>
        <p>Mr. F S Bennett, brother of Dr James Bennett and son of Mr John Bennett of Padgate (late of Bewsey Hall) is one of the leading figures of the motor world. Recently he has been instructing little Prince Olaf, heir to the throne of Norway, in the art of motoring. The miniature Cadilla car, which was given to the Prince as a Christmas present by Queen Alexandra, was con- structed une.er the personal suvervision of Mr Bennett, who is tbe manag- ing director of F S Bennett, Limited, Shaftesbury Avenue, London, W.C. It was originally intended for his own children Mona and Stanley and when exhibited in Paris, created such tremendous interest that Mr. Bennett was presented to M. Fallieres' at the President's request. Mr. Bennett spent a most interesting afternoon at Sand- ringham explaining the details of the car— which weighs only 3001 b.—to the Royal relatives of Prince Olaf. *</p>
        <p>* Here are a few gems from accounts of "How I spent Christmas" by boys aged 8-10 in one of our local schools : After dinner I went a walk, for I felt sick. I, only went a hundred yards and I was sick. ,I was going away ,to Heywood but I felt the head-ache coming on, I fell asleep in a chair and my mother took our Joseph instead. My present was a bad one. It was a pen- knife. I could not sleep for cuts on my fingers and I cut my foot twice and my toe once, and I could not walk We were going to have a talking machine on, only my father was drunk and carried on I went to Yorkshire. There were no dirty streets there. My uncle gave me sixpence but my mother takes everything off me and then a lump comes in my throat. We had a do at our house. My father danced a cake walk with a girl. Then they pulled the rug up and tried to dance again, but they couldn't. Everywhere we went we had plum udding.</p>
