      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>"G. R. JEBB" TAKES THE PLUNGE</head>
        <p>THE "G. R. JEBB" TAKES THE PLUNGE</p>
        <p>• Memories of the days when Warrington was a ship-building centre were conjured up to old inhabitants on Thursday, when a light ship was launched in the St. Helens and Sankey Canal at Sankey Bridges. The vessel, which has been built by Messrs. Clare and Ridgway to the order of the Upper Mersey Navigation Commissioners, is to be stationed in the Upper Mersey, exposed to stress of weather and heavy seas. It has been christened the "G. R. Jebb "—as a complirwmt to Mr. GI R. Jebb, the chairman of the Commissioners. The length of the ship is 70f. 6 inches and the main breadth 17ft. It is built chiefly of English oak and will be fitted with a tower, 20ft above the deck, from which the lights will be shown. The building of the ship has been watched with much interest and a large crowd lined the banks of the canal to witness the launch. Mrs. Inman, wife of the deputy chairman of the Commissioners, " christened " the vessel by dashing a bottle of wine against the bows. Then Miss Fa,irclough cut the dagger, releasing the ship which swiftly and gracefully slid down the slips into the canal, causing an over-flow of the water on to the opposite bank. The ceremony over, a number of visitors assembled in the offices of Messrs. Clare and Ridgway. Amongst those present were: Mr. and Mrs. Ernest Ridgway, Captain Ridgway, Miss. Ridgway, Miss Joan Ridgway, Mrs. Secker, Mr. and Mrs. E. Inman, Mr. R. T. Fairclough, Mrs. Fairclough, Miss Nannie Fairclough, Rev. H. R. Wild, Mr. P. C. Garnett, Captain Yates (superintendent of the lighting and buoying of the Upper Mersey), and Mr. Jones (Bishop's Wharf Carrying Co.) "Success to the G. R. Jebb "was drunk, on the proposition of Mr. Ernest Ridgway, who expressed regret at the absence of Mr. Cawley, who had been mainly responsible forthe construction of the boat. He referred to the difficult and often thankless nature of the duties of the Upper Mersey Commissioners in maintaining an open channel in the Mersey. Mr. Inman proposed "Success to Messrs. Clare and Ridgway," and said the Com- missioners were confident, in view of the excellent reputation of the firm, that the vessel would be capable of withstanding the stress of weather which it would have to encounter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>CHILDREN'S BALL.</head>
        <p>THE CHILDREN'S BALL.</p>
        <p>Photo Burrell. ETARIES AND GROUP OF GUESTS.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR AND MAYORESS, SECEI</p>
        <p>There were bright- and nicturesque scenes at the ' Parr Hall on Tuesday ivening, the occasion being the annual children's ball in aid of the " Children of Warrington Cot " at t Infirmary. It was good to see the happy throng of light hearted children gaily "tripping the light fantastic toe "and one could not help comtnendivg. the enterprise of the proutote,s in thus cotiihlnieg the assistance of a wixthy object with the arrangement of a function which becomes ircreasiegly popular as yeb.t s go on. On ent4ing the hall the guests were received by tut- .y (CoumAil r D. Joieph and the Mayoress (\lrs Arnold Nieldt. and a pretty procession round the building was headed iw Miss Cooper, daughter of Mr and Mrs Turner Cooper, of Lower Walton, who presented a buttonhole to the , Mayor, while Master Philip Collin Houghton, son of , l'and Mrs II B Houghton, of St.ckton Heatn,presented a beautiful bouquet to the Mayoress Afterwards dancing began to the strains of Messrs. Crosfield's band Waich provided a very ch,ice programme of sixtesit dances. Variety was lent to the proceedings by a very graceful and artistic display of fancy dancing by • pupils of the Misses Spiuks, their toe dancing being I;xceptionally clever. T he performers , were n,fter- wards presented with boxes of chocolates by Master it Partridge. Pupils of the People's College presented some of the most accept:l-Alit items flour their recent concert programme and. Mr Buckle, of Manchester, 'afforded great pleasure, as well as mystification, by an original ouijuring entertainment. The catering was in the hands of the Winmarleigh Cafe Co. Special praise is doe to the enereetie bon secretary,</p>
        <p>Mr James Henry Smethurst and his canable assistant, Mr James Houghton, fur the exctllence of the arrangements. The stewards were Drs Bowden, C D Law, Murray, Sutherland, Aldermen Smethurst and Burton, Messrs W Athertou, J Allison, Bainbridge, Jos. Bennett, F Bracecamp, .J Brooks, B Browne, H Collin, T R Critchley. C A Coard, H Dolan. C Eden, A H Fawcett, J H J Harupson, 0- Hatton, L Hensliall, A liodgkinson, G II Hodgson, a W Houghton, H B Houghton, J I Hughes, It Isherwood, F Baby Jolley, F G Ki rtland, J Lightburn, F V L Mathias, F E A Murphy, J Moore Murray, A Nield, R Parkinson, S L Partridge, W A Proud, J Richardson, W Richards )11, Fl Rushworth, I Sankey, J Sankey, F Short. F 0 Stansfield, J E 0 Thompson, W S Tinsley, G 0 Venn, R J Venn, J W Weisby, E A White, and J G Withensbaw. The ladies' committee consisted of the Mayoreis (Mrs Arnold Nield), Mesdames Joseph Bennett. BoWden, Bracecamp, Turner, Cooper, Jobn Evans, E J Fox, Fergiison, J C.'oote }Ebben, Jackson Holmes, J Houghton. jun, Harold Jolley, Harold Joseph, Lightburn, F V L Matthias, Murray, J Moore Murray, F Parsonage. E A Robson, J Sankey, James Smethurst, J H Smethurst, F Short, Morley Stevenson, G F F White, E A White, Misses Burton F E Clarke, Forshaw, Edith Forshaw, Hammond, H Houghton, Lomas, Monks, Pattington Plinston, D Reid, Sankey, Smethurst, A E Swethurst, Spiuks, Etbel Spinks. Wright. Among f.tber ladies assisting were Misses Broadbent, Misses Tinsley, Miss Bolton (Penketh), Mrs W A Proud and Mrs Partridge.</p>
        <p>Picturedrome. "Bobbies Father" is the stir picture for Monday and Tuesday--a very pretty story with a moral. On Wednesday and Thursday " Bill Wilson's Gal" will be presented. "Nan in Fairyland" is a pretty. fairy story, and deals with the dream of a poor waif. This picture will be shown on Friday and Saturday. Mr Coop.r begs to announce that commencing 1 Monday, January 20th. The programme will only be., changed twice weealy—every Monday and Thursday. LAMPS, Oil Stove, Coke itoves.—J.S. GARLAND Bridge rt., Warrington.—Advt. " Praying Billy " at Academy-street Chapel Mr W Edge (known ai "Praying Billy ") told a remarkable story of his own conversion from a life of drunkenness and irreligion to that of an evangelist and temperanceovorker, at the Academy-street Chapel, on Wednesday evening. "A. miracle of God's saving grace " was the term which he applied to his trans- formation and he held the rapt attention of a large congregation by a homely address of extraordinary power. Miss Morton, president of the British Women's Temperance Association, presided. Ask for Whitbread's Famous Ales and Stouts in sorew-bottles.—Sole agent, PETER LEIGH, Horse- market st.---Advt.</p>
        <p>Successful Concert at the Workhouse. The annual concerts arranged by Mr W C Brooks, one of the members of the Board, are always looked forward to with keen anticipation by the inmates of the Warrington Workbou3e and this year's concert which was given on Wednesday evening proved to be the most successful of any. Messrs Crosfield's band instrumental quartette gave self ctions and Mr J Blackburn a cornet solo. Mr and Miss Kilduff, Mr and Mrs Hannan, Mr and Mrs Dutton, Miss C Rigby, Mrs E( A Sanders, and Mr Boscow were responsible for many excellent songs and a male quartette con- sisting of Messrs Cook, Critehley, Walker, and Aukland rendere,l " Robin Adair" and " Annie Laurie" in first-class style. The humorous portion of the programme which was exceed- ingly popular, was well carried out by Mr J Newns, Mr E A Sanders and Mr E Gibson Quite a feature of the concert was a very fine render- ing of the "Soldiers chorus" .from Faust, and the "Hallelujah chorus" froth "The Messiah" by a choir of 30 voices, conducted by Mr W C Brooks. Mr and Miss \A. Eckersley were the accompanists. At the close of the concert Mr A J Jolley, vice-chairman of the Board on behalf of the inmates and visitors thanked Mr Brooks and his friends for the splendid inment they had provided,</p>
        <p>entertai</p>
        <p>Mr. E. J. Guests Will As briefly announced in our last issue, 131 r Edvt in SORE CAUSED GREAT PAIN. James Guest (of the firm of Messrs Guest Brothers, 3, Russell St., Hull, Yorks, Bog.—" I had been tanners, of Warrington), whose death occurred on the value of 18th October last, left property of the gi, e. 4 troubled with a sore under my knee which caused me £13,053 17s. 10d with net personality £11,777 los. gd great pain through its constant itching. The part affected was the flesh just under my knee about eight Probate has been granted to Mrs Annie Guest, of i Lynton, Colwyn Bay ; Mrs Edith Annie.Anderaon, of inches long. It began by a constant itching nearly uabearable, and was always running. I could get Beach Cottage, Wynne Avenue, Colwyn Bay ; and Miss little sleep as it was always worse when my body was Gertrude Elizabeth Guest of 4, Palmyra-square, Warrington, his daughters, and Mr George Guest of warm at night. "I tried several rem, diept which utterly failed. I the same address, and Mr Herbert William Guest of happPned to see an advertisement one day about Oakdene, Padgate, Warrington, his sons. The will is Cuticura Ointment and I thought I would try it. I dated 12th March, 1912. The testator leaves shares used only one half a tin of Cuticura Ointment and of the nominal value of £1,250, in Guest Brothers that completely healed the sore which I had been Limited to each of his children, Edith, Annie Ander- troubled with for no less than eighteen months." son, Gertrude Elizabeth Guest, Herbert William (Signed) Fred Hudson, July 2, 1912. Guest, and Gsorge Edwin Guest; 20 shares in Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists Penrhos College, and 25 shares in John Walker and everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post Company, Ltd.. to his wife ; and interest in tannery business to his said two sons. The residue of his free from nearest depot: Newbery, 27, Charterhonse property he leaves upon trust for his wife for life: Sq., London; R. Towns &amp; (1,,., Sydney, N.S.W, ; then the proceeds of life policy to his said t . Lennon, Ltd., Cape Town ; Muller, Maclean &amp; Co. two daugkters, and the ultimate residue he leaves up-on- Calcutta and Bombay • "Cuticura," Boston' U• S' A' trust fbr his children equally. Tender-faced men should shave with Ctiucura Soap Shaving Stick. Sample free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The New Tory policy about Food Taxes. " Shat your eyes and open your mouth, and see if the Colonies tax you ! " That's not the way to keep the Empire united. * *</p>
        <p>"In all land legislation one essential prin- ciple must be recognised—that the first pur- pose of the land of this country should be, not the conferring of power and pleasure upon the favoured few, but the provision of sustenance and shelter for the multitudes who toil.—Mr. Lloyd George. * * Sickness and maternity benefit under the Insurance Act will become payable from Monday next, and from Wednesday next medical benefit may be claimed. Particulars of Mr. A. H. Crosfield's offer of silver spoons and photographs to the first recipients of the maternity benefit in Warrington are published in another column. * * * The new / representative of St. Austin's Ward is already showing that independence of attitude in the Council,which his support- ers expected of him. Councillor Osborne is no believer in " one-man" committees and he entered a warm protest against what he con- ceived to be the high-handed action of the chairman (Alderman Bolton) with regard to the letting of a certain contract. *</p>
        <p>* - The Town Council meeting on Tuesday e vening was far from edifying, the greater part of the three hours being given up to personal bickerings and a series of " scenes " which, while they afforded amusement to the ratepayers assembled, were certainly lower- ing to the dignity of a public authority. Those "good resolutions" which we are taught to associate with the opening of a New Year have either taken a wrong course or been very promptly broken by our loca' legislators.</p>
        <p>* * Quite a stir was caused at the Council meeting on Tuesday by the announcement that anonymous letters had been received by the Mayor and other members of the Council and, for a short time, the usual business had to give place to a sort of indignation meeting. Such words as "dastardly," " scurrilous " and " disgraceful " were freely bandied about and one worthy alderman announced that he would willingly spend money to have the unknown writer sent to durance vile. The anonymous letter as a means of influencing public men or anyone else is, of course, to be deprecated, but we do think an exaggerated importance was attached by many councillors to the epistles circulated on this occasion. As far as we have been able to ascertain, they took the form of type-written communications signed "A Ratepayer" on the question of the appointment of architects for the Council schools. Some of the speeches at the Council , meeting would no doubt give rise to the wrong impression that the contents consisted of nothing but personal abuse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>Lectures on Trade Unions. Mr Joseph Dalby, MA., will begin a course of lectures on " Old English Trade Unions" in the Socialist 'Hall, Wilderspool Causeway, on Monday evening next, at 8 o'clock. Ask for Whitbread's Famous Ales and Stouts in screw-bottles.—Sole agent, PETER LEIGH, Horse- market st.—Advt. Royal Theatre of Varieties. The historical drama "Jane Shore" has been presented very successfully during the week by the "Royal Stock" Co., who will give "The Black Flag" next week. Rivers and Sullivan, speciality dancers, will also appear. ANTHRACITE STOVES, clean, cheerful, smokeless, no fumes.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND. 51, Bridge st. Warrington.—Advt. Women's Liberal Association. The sewing meetings in connection with this association were resumed at the Liberal Club on Thursday, when over GO members attended. Miss Crosfield, who had a very enthusiastic recepl ion, gave a short address on current politics. Ask for Whitbread's Famous Ales and Stouts in screw-bottles.—Sole agent, PETER LEIGH, Horse - market st.—Advt. Musical Society's Concert. The Musical Society will give their second sub- scription concert of the season in the Parr Bail on Wednesday evening next. The principal work to be presented Is "A Tale of Old Japan" by the late S Coleridge-Taylor. FOR MODERN FIREPLACES and Cooking Ranges corn bining economy with simplicity try J. S. GARLAND Ironmonger, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Photographic Society. Mr T Lee Syms, F.R.P.S., of the Leigh Photo Society, gave a most welcome lecture On Tuesday to a large audience at the Academy on the subject of "control." It was a talk and demonstration on the working account of Negatives and Prints, and not the strictest purist could object to Mr Syms' methods, especially as shown by the specimens treated before the audience. The lecture was enlivened with many humorous references and Lancashire stories and the appreciative listeners heartily endorsed the thanks at the close. Ask for Whitbread's Famous Ales and Stouts in screw-bottles.—Sole agent., PETER LEIGH, Horse market St.—Advt. Leather Trade In Warrington and District. The actual business done during the week was only small,as trade seemed still under the holiday influence, and deliveries were almost entirely confined to odd small parcels ; inside the tanneries the staffs' energies were mainly engaged in getting out stocktaking statistics, and also getting stock ready for the time the appetite of the trade gets normal again. The question of prices is receiving much consideration, and some tanners took the earliest opportunity for revision, and from January Ist many lists took a step forward ; so far it is too early to see if this forward movement is going to be immediately successful, but local opinion seems fairly unanimous that, in a week or two, buyers will be paying new rates as readily as they paid the old. There have been plenty of prospective buyers in the district during the week, and it must be conceded they fought very shy of any further advance at present, although they had to admit that the depleted state of the leather market might easily ca(t.se it to become established once the demand becomes strong again. There was an unusually strong list of orders on hand for delivery immediately after last week, and the immediate prospect is very bright for a brisk turnover if only we might have such weather as would let tanners get their goods along quickly ; enquiry is frequent, and strong for all classes of goods, the most pressing seeming to be for rough strap butts ; all weights under 22's and over 30's are clamoured for and the foreign demand is of quite as urgent a character as the home one. —" The Leather World." CIITLERY.—Joseph Rodgers &amp; Sons' celebrated Knives and Forks, &amp;c.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
