      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, January 11, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 11, 1913.</p>
        <p>for some chip potatoes and was delayed in bringing them to his father. The defendant went out to meet him and before listeniug to an explanation be caught him by the throat and delivered a violent blow in the mouth. Other blows were received on the head and ribs and as a result the boy was in such a feeble and weak condition that he fainted in the street.—Evidence in support of Mr Browne's statement was given by the boy, Dr Peacock and a neighbour. Dr Peacock said unnecessary violence had been used, sufficient to cause unconscious- ness. In cross-examination by Mr Jenkins. the boy admitted that when his father was sober he was as good as gold to him.—On behalf of the defendant Mr H J Jenkins expressed regret for what had occurred, The father thought he was only exercising his rights and using a proper method of correction. There was no intention to inflict such an abnormal amount of punish- ment. He intended it to be a method of correction.— The magistrates imposed a flue ef X 5 including costs or one month.</p>
        <p>CLOTHES LINE TH EFTS —Elizabeth Thomason, 79, Haydock-street, was charged with two offences of theft of clothing from clothes lines in Battersby-lane and (,h.orley-street on January 7. She was also charged with receiving stolen clothing from Ellen Dagnall, her sister.—The Chief Constable said for some time past the police had received complaints of clothing being stolen from clothes lines. Detectives Holland and Craig made inquiries and prisoner was arrested.—The Prisoner having admitted the first two offences, he (the Chief Constable) desired to offer no evidence on the the third charge.—A fine of £1 in each case was imposed (£2) or 28 days.—Mary Ellen Dagnall, 11, Refuge - square, a sister of the last prisoner, was also fined £2 or 28 days in connection with two charges of stealing clothes from lines in Pierpoint-street.</p>
        <p>effected wad £4OO and one put it as high as £7OO. It only showed how misleading these things were. Messrs Wright and H i amlyn were straightforward n recognizing that great consideration had been shown to them—they had had something given to them that nobody else had had. At the meeting of the Education Committee, Councillor Roberts said the architects were not under-cutting in the price. How could he, in face of that letter in which the firm offered to do the work for 11 per cent. less than the former terms, say that he was n..t wrong? Councillor Rotimrs : Ido say so. Councillor PEMBERTON : I know you would say any- thing. Continuing he said Councillor Roberts was , reported to have said that Messrs Wright and Hamlyn were being paid 5 per cent. and 21 per cent in respec:, of the other schools. Joencillor ROBERTS : That is a downright false hood. I never made any such statement, I knew they were receiving 61 per cent. on Oakwood Avenue I school. I never made the statement; it is pure invention. Councillor PENIBERTON : That is what you are reported in the papers as having said. Councillor ROBERTS: I act not. Councillor PEMBERTON : All the members of the Council have to do is to read the Prews. There can be no mistake about it, because it is reported in both papers. And surely members of the Council remember what was said by you and by Mr Jarman. You can deny it as much PS you like, that is what you did say. Mr Jarman also said it with such an air of assurance, that I began to feel I was evidently wrong. Councillor perunerton advanced the arguments in which he has already familiarped the Education Committee in support of open competition and said the only safe way for the town was that everything should be public and shove board. Ile warmly repudiated the "vile attacks" made upon hip. The Bolton school extension, he continued was being built by the ECM of the deputy-chairman of the su.b-committee—the very man who attacked him (Councillor Pemberton) with regard to the school sites. Further remarks in this strain called forth a strung protest from Alderman SMETHURST who said : There is not a fairer or more straightforward man on the Education Committee than Mr Fairclough. It is a dastardly attack on a man who is absent and cannot defend him- self.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : I wish he was here. He concluded with an appeal to the Council to reter the resolution back because the principle of it was altogether wrong. Councillor Le SHFORD seconded the resolution. Councillor ROBERTS : I beg to move that the question be now put. Everything that has been said by Councillor Pemberton he has said on two previous occasions, with the exception of the scurrilous personalities in which he has indulged to-night. I do not intend to reply to those. I have already made my defence of the comniittee on two occasions and I think the committee is fully aware of the facts. Alderman TINNION seconded. Councillor GOUG H : I have great pleasure in support- ing Councillor Pemberton's resolution— The DEPUTY-MAYOR : You cannot go on. It is proposed and seconded that the motion be now put. Councillor GOUGH : I assure—(Cries of " chair.") The CHAIRMAN declared the proposal that the resolution be now put to the meeting, was carried. Councillor PE'MBERTON'S motion was then put and defeated by 16 votes to five. Councillor PEMBERToN : I hope you will take the names.—</p>
        <p>Almerrean SAI ETIIURST : And then send them anonymous letters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>SCHOOL ARCHITECTS.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ARCHITECTS.</p>
        <p>PLEA FOR COMPETITION AGAIN REJECTED.</p>
        <p>LIVELY COUNCIL DEBATE.</p>
        <p>The question of allotting the architectural work for new Council schools without competition was again the subject of discussion at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening. Councillor PEMBERTON moved the following resolu- tion :—" That the resolution of the Council of the 3rd December, 1912, confirming the minutes of the Educa- tion Committee of the meeting held on the 18th November, 1912, in so far as such minutes refer to the resolution of the Sites and Buildings Committee Nod E 994, be rescinded." The resolution in question was to the effect that Messrs Wright and Harul!, n be requested to prepare the plans of the Parr-street and Oliver-street schools on terms set forth in their letter to the Sites and Buildings Committee. In introducing the resolution,Councillor PEMBERTON said the Council had not had the opportunity of going into the details of the question. Many personal attacks had been made upon him in connection with his attitude, but they knew the old adage : "o case, abuse the other side." The personal abuse he received was an evidence of the strength of his case.Fortunwely for him, the facts of the case were known to many hundreds of ratepayers, and his strength in the Council lay in the fact that he had support from the pubhc outside. The public had knowledge, independent of the members of the Council. They were not.interested, but certain members of the Council were. Councillor Pemberton was proceeding to go into details with regard to the Oakwood Avenue School, when The DErcrri-MAYOR interposed with the remark that the matter had been discussed time after time. It had been before the Council au(' the Education Committee at three meetings and it had been fully debated. He thought he had better take the pleasure of the Council as to whether Councillor Pemuerton should be allowed to discuss it in the manner he wished. Councillor ROBERTS- I beg to move that the vote be now taken. Councillor PEMBERTON: I object. Ceuncillor GOUGH : Councillor Roberts ought not to interrupt. Counciltur Prntneft,ToN : It is too soon to take a vote yet. Councillor GOUGH : Councillor Pemberton never gees up but what he (Councillor Ronerts) interrupts _</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : It is nothing but a personal attack because of toy pist actioni aud my attitude against Councillor Roberts. He takes every opportunity of getting up here to make an attack on we hut I lerive other people to judge of the value or it. Very likel3, he has been hit indirectly over this and no doubt he will resent it. Councillor ROBERTS: lam not going to sit hero and nave these attacks made upon me. Councillor PEMBERTON : It is all right when you are attacking me, though. Councillor ROBERTS: I have never attacked Coun- cillor Pemberton on personal grounds. I shall attack him when I get the chance, in another place, not here. [am waiting for that chance and I hope he will give it to me.</p>
        <p>Councill.r PEMBERTON : That won't stop me from saying what I want to say, for one moment. lam here in the public interest. Neither myself, nor my father, nor my brothers have anything to do with it at all. The DEpUTY-MAYOR : I wish you would confine yourself to the motion. I think you ought to have a little consideration for the patience of the Council. Councillor PEMBERTON: I am not makinglthe inter- ruptions. I was going on very quietly. The particular case I was going to mention bears on this case. The DEPUTY-MAYOR : As long as you keep to the facts and avoid personalities it is all right. Councillor PEMBERTON : I have not commenced any personalities. The DEPUTY-MAYOR: Go on, Cuhncillor, or we shall be here all night. ' Councillor PEMBERTON proceeded to argue that it was impossible to fix a stereotyped design for Council Schools as the r:quirements of the Board of Education changed from month to month. When Messrs Wright and Hamlyn were adjudicated winners of the competi- tion in connectien with the Oakwood-avenue schools, Mr R T Fairclough (deputy-chairman of the Sites and Buildings Committee) said the schools they had pre- viously built for the committee had not required any additional expenditure upon them since their erection —in fact, they were the best in the town. Councillor 1.11 EtTLEs : Hear, hear. Councillor PEMBERTON: Yes, because your son-in- law was the architect. The DEPUTY-MAYOR: Those are personalities you ought not to make such remarks. Councllor lititmEs : He cannot help it, Mr Chair- man, but let him give me the chance— Councillor PEMBERTON: Councillor Birtlefi ought not to interruwdpitLmKe. Councillor m SON rose to speak. Councillor GOUGH : You should not interrupt. Councillor WILKINSON : You shut up. I am deplor- ing the fact that I have to sit here and listen to a speech by Councillor Pemberton which has nothing in the essence of it, only animosity and personal spleen towards members of this Council. I refuse to sit here much longer, if he d'oes'nt get on. The DEPUTY-MAYOR : It would be better if there were no interruptions. Councillor Pemberton is in order.</p>
        <p>- Councillor PEMBERTON : Because people are inter- ested in this, because of their families and themselves, I have to stand these personal attacks. It is about the most abominable position a public man can be placed in and I am going to do what. I consider my duty. Alderman TINNION : You have got no relations, have you?</p>
        <p>COUnClilor PEMBERTON : Well, they don't come me in my public positicn and ask me to get jobs for them.</p>
        <p>, Councillor WILKINSON : Do any of mine? Councillor PEMBERTON : You go and ask them. , Continuing Councillor Pemberton said he had in his , possession the letter in which Messrs. Wright and Hamlyn set forth their terms. He wondered how many ' members had seen it, although they had voted on it? ' Councillor Pemberton then read the letter whicla was headed" Private and confidential ") as followa :- 1 "We are much obliged by your letter of the 23rd , October informing us of your committee's wishes , in regard to the preparation of plans ‘'..:4 for future schools, and we desire in the firEt place to thank them for the opportunity they have given us to quote our terms for the work. We may say that we shall be pleased to undertake the whole of the work in connection with the erection of the con- templated schools, as laid down in your competi- tion conditions and including the preparation of quantities and supervising the same as they proceed upon either of the following terms :—Oak- wood Avenue schools, Parr-street schools and Oliver-street schools, 5 per cent, on actual expenditure; or as an alternative, Oakwood Avenue schools on the terms already agreed upon, namely 61 per cent, and the future schools at 4 per cent inclusive. In both cases it being understood that tho Corporation bear the cost of printing all quantities, plans for sub-contractors, &amp;c. Upon these terms it will be noted that upon an estimated expenditure of £28,000 (the approximate actual cost of the three schools at present contemplated) the cost to your committee would under the first scheme be the sum of £1,400, whereas under the second scheme it would amount to £1,440 10s, or in round figures a saving in favour of the first schema of .£5O. We may also point out that an arrangement such as either of the above would effect a saving to ,our committee on the three schools—that is to say, taking it that your fees for future work would he 61 per cent as hitherto—of £350 or £3OO respectively. May we in conclusion assure your committee that if se are entrusted with your work, it shall, at all times, have best care and attention and farther may we respectfully ask that the figures quoted may be kept as private as is possible, compatible with ' puhlic duty. Again thanking your committee for their consideration.- Yours faithfully, (Signed) WRIGHT &amp; TIAMLYN. ' Having characterised the offer to do future schools at 4 per cent as "knobsticking," Councillor PEmBERTON said some members had stated that the saving to be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WRETCHED YEAR in an Anemic Girl's Life.</head>
        <p>A WRETCHED YEAR in an Anemic Girl's Life.</p>
        <p>Her happiness now restored by Dr. William's Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>"It was a most wretched year a year of illne.s which I hope never will be repeated in mx life." This remark fell from the lips of Miss M. A, Finney when, iuterviewed at her home 73, Barker-lane, Brampton, Chesterfield, she described her intense sufferings from bloodlessness. Miss f'inney continued : - "I was about 18 years of age, when I began to feel dull and depressed. Upon getting up in the morning I felt so weak that it was all I could do to dress myself. Seldom could I touch breakfast, for my appe- tite completely left me; ;t was just a sip or two if tea and off to busi- less. So much did I Auffer from pains in the che-it and nausea, after )artaking of fond, that dreaded every meal. "1 tpok different ;dicines, but became orse, and so breath-</p>
        <p>• less that to walk, even slov.ly, made me gasp and set my heart palpitating. At last my bloodless state became so acute that I had to remain away from business. One doctor sai3 that mine was a case of Advanced Anemia. He ordered me special diet and medicine; but treatment did not seem to help me. Then I went to Hospital, and specialists there also told me that my case was one of Advanced Anternia. Many doctors said that I should need a lot of cate to pull round.</p>
        <p>A BRIGHTER TIME AUEAD. "I bad been suffering like this for a year, when someone advised me to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pill.. After taking a few boxes of the Pills I was not so dull or depresed ; my appetite was better and I awoke in the mornings feeling more energetic. After taking more of the Pills I could enjoy substantial food, and there was no nausea or pain afterwards. Gradually my strength came back; then my blood got richer and the headaches left me. kfter that I had no further trouble from palpitation, breathlessness or dizziness.</p>
        <p>So in time my bloocl was thoroughly renewed, and I became strong and returned to business. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills alone cured me." A HEALTH GUIDE FREE for victims of Blood troubles will be sent by post to all applicants to Book Dept., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London. The Pills that strengthen by making New Blood that is the great secret oc the success achieved by Dr Williams Pink Pills for Pale People. No other Pills so surely Aive tone to the vital organs and nerves of both men and women. They have cured countless cases of Anemia, Indigestion, General Weakness, Rheumatism, Sciatica, Neuralgia, St. Vitus' Dance, and Nervous Disorders. Obtainable of all dealers, or post free, direct from Dr Williams' Medicine Co., 40, Holborn Viaduct, London, EC., price 2s. 9d. for one box, or 13s Pd for six boxes.</p>
        <p>Ask plainly for Dr Williams' Pink Pills, and go elsewbete if pestered to buy, substitutes.</p>
        <p>Christmas and New Year at the Ragged School. The Superintendent of the Warrington School and Mission (Councillor Skelland), and the teachers, bad a busy time during the festive season. On Boxing Day food and entertainment were provided for upwards of a thousand poor children of the town, and a very happy time was spent. On New Yeara Day food was given to about sixty widows. On Sunday last at the invitation of the teachers, Mrs Edmund Osborne gave an encouraging address to the children and at the close of tlqe School presented each girl with a shawl, and each boy with a scarf, which had been generously sent for them by Mrs John Crosfield, of Colwyn Bay. The children showed their appreciation of the gifts and River, by hearty clapping of hands. Mrs C'rosfield also kindly gave shawls and blankets for the poorest of the parents of the children. A complete list of those who were kind enough to give food and donations will appear in the next annual report.</p>
