        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Connelllor H Roberts (in the chair), Colonel Edelsten, Messrs T Locker and C Jackson Holmes.</p>
        <p>GUARDSMAN ASSAULTS ITIS WlFE.—Alfred Peplow, 162, Liverpool-road, was charged with persistent cruelty to his wife, Elizabeth, who applied for a separation order.—Mr A Browne, who prosecuted unfolded a sordid story of brutal assault and said that the defendant had committed every possible kind of assault on his wife, but the climax was reached on Boxing night when he attempted to stab her with a bat pin and a penknife. The police were called in and the defendant was thrown out of the house. He was a man in a good writion being a gods guardsman earning from 36s to 38s a week. In answer to Mr Browne, com- plainant said her husband had "given her three black eyes in six months," and at August Bank Holiday she had two at once. He had assaulted her on all parts of the body. On Boxing Day he had five bottles of stout in front, of him on the table. He threatened to cut her head off with a knife.—By the defendant: She had never been refused drink eta public-house because she had been the worse for drink herself.—Defendant had asked his wife a number of questions when Mr Browne interposed and suggested that defendant should consent to an order. He had nu fewer than ten witnesses to call to give evidence for the complainant.—Defendant. in answer to the Chairman, said he would consent to a reasonable order.—Complainant stated that she had received as much as 38s 5d from him.—Defendant was ordered to pay lOs a week.</p>
        <p>"ONE OF THE MOST WICKED WOMEN."—James Galvin, 8, Ilelsby.-street, was charged with failing to provide proper maintenance for his wife, who now lives at 8, Ellesmere-street.—The Chief Constable stated that the defendant was a puddler, and for 11 years he had never given his wife proper wages. Three weeks ago he gave her 35., while occasionally he had given her one or two shillings a week with a steak or a chop. She had been kept by her sons, and she had teen com- pelled to leave him owing to his threats. The com- plainant did not ask the defendant to contribute to her support. All she asked was a separation order.— Defendant said be was nearly blind and had very little work. He gave his wife all the money ho could afford She was fond of dancing, and was" one of the most wicked women one could imagine." (Laughter.) She went out on a Saturday night and did not return until two o'clock on the Sunday morning.—The Chief Constable stated that there was no truth in these charges.—The order was granted. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr J W Hayward (in the chair) Messrs C Jackson Holmes and W T Williams. A SERIES OF OFFENCES.—Patrick Burke, 2, School Brow,was fined 23 6d for having used indecent language the previous day. It was slated that when the police came on the scene he put his head out of the window and swore at them.—lle was further charged with being 303 in arrears in respect of a daughter who was in a reformatory.—The Chief C mstable stated that ever since the order had been made, the prisoner had caused the police to have the greatest difficulty in obtaining payments from him.—</p>
        <p>itg 'ogee 8, ORSEMARKET STREET, WARFIIINGTON.</p>
        <p>Burke was ordered to pay the motley or go to prison for one month. There was also an adjourned charge of using threats to his wife on December 6th. The case had been adjourned from the 9th December to January 27th, but prisoeer elected to have the case tried that morning.—Mr Jenkins, who prosecuted, said that the prisoner promised at the last hearing, that he would be of good behaviour and sign the pledge. He had not done so. tie had smashed the windows and destroyed provisions in the shop.—Mrs Burke stated that her husband had threatened to finish her, stating that he wanted to go to prison and would not go for nothing. He used many filthy threats.—Burke was bound over in two sureties of £5, to be of good behaviour for six months, in default,one month's imprisonment. He bad also to pay the costs of the case.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.-Mr 3 Rymer Young (in the chair),Messrs W Roberts, A J Jolley and Dr Naden.</p>
        <p>THE CHitisTmAs BEER.—Messrs Greenell, Whitley and Co., Ltd.' Wilderspool Brewery, were summoned in ten cases by Mr W J McGighey, H.M. Inspector of Factories, Manchester, for employing women beyond the legal period on December 21st last. Mr 'l' Ridgway appeared for the company and the offences were admitted.—Mr I.llcCaghey stated that under the Factory Act women and young persons were not allowed to be ernyloyed after two o'clock on Saturday afternoon. On the date in question the defendant's premises were visited by an inspector at 3.20 in the afternoon, that 30 women were employed in the bottling department. When asked for an explanation it was stated that there had been a change in the pint,. and that there was a rush to get out orders for Christmas. Mr Ridgway said at the time there bad been a change from one bottling eepartment to another, that they were unable to work at full pressure on the new plant. They They were desirous of getting out orders, and the employers acted quite voluntarily. They were paid three-quarter of a day's wages 'for the two hours over time they worked. It was not a case of time cribbing, and it was7only due to the extraordinary circumstances which had arisen. They had never worked overtime on Saturday before. He submitted a small penalty would meet the case. A fine of ss. and costs was imposed in the first case, and Is. and costs in each of the other nine summonses. TIIE CHRISTMAS RUSII.--J Bayley Isaac, Turner- street, was summoned for employing a young person on Sunday, December 22nd, contrary to the provision of the Factory Act. Mr Pollard, one of the Factory Inspectors paid a visit to the defendant's premises and found a boy named Hear Glover working. The explanation of Mr Isaac was that at that date there was an exceptional number of orders which had to be executed. The magistrates imposed a fine of 5s and costs, ..£1 altoget her. BRUTAL FATHER.—Patrick Farrington,ll.2,Pierpoint- street, was summoned at the instance of the N.S.P.C.C. for an assault upon his thirteen year old son, Thomas Farrington.—Mr A Browne who prosecuted said the defendant and his son lodged with an aunt named Mrs Fisher. The first assault took place on Boxing Day, when he thumped the boy on the head and ribs because he did not get some Christmas cards. Later on New Year's night defendant again assaulted the boy, lie was sent</p>
        <p>_ I, Q , TS !=7? ; . • . . .</p>
        <p>Borough Police Court</p>
      </div>
