      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>1903-1913.</head>
        <p>1903-1913.</p>
        <p>THE GENESIS AND LIFE HISTORY OF TARIFF REFORM.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ernest Williams author of "Trade in Germany " relates that: "In the closing days of March (1903) I was directed by the Editor of the "Financial News " to undertake a vigorous propaganda in that journal on behalf Protection,and I was requested to supplement that journalistic work by getting together an organization formed for the same end. . . . A meeting was called. The meeting did more than discuss; it formed itself straight- way into the Protection League. Not a soul present had an inkling that the very next day Mr Chamberlain was going to light his torch at Birmingham We rose to it by altering the title of our league. Prejudice had gathered round the word Protection, and under the new develop- ment it was no longer well to use a word, however reasonable, which created prejudice [We realised that under Mr Chamberlain's [ leadership a great national movement had begun, and that we had to go out and capture the masses forthwith, and not be content with the suffrages of the chosen few who were already emancipated from prejudice. So our Association became The Tariff League. At the next meeting of our committee we changed our title once again, and hence the Imperial Tariff Reform. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>A Committee of Members of Parliament resolved itself into an organisation which came to the Imperial Tariff League and offered amalgamation. The Imperial Tariff League accepted, and handed over its membership, its effects, and its goodwill to the new men, and so gave birth to the Tariff Reform League."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>WOODEN WEDDING OF</head>
        <p>THE WOODEN WEDDING OF</p>
        <p>PROTECTION AND TARIFF REFORM.</p>
        <p>Ten years ago there lived an old maiden lady called Protection. She sprang from a family of that name who long years ago were held in honour, but who for their mis- deeds were exiled by statesman who occupied high place in our land, by name Peel, Cobden, Bright, and Gladstone. But as with the Bourbons in France, and the Stuarts in England, there was always a remnant who adhered to this family and its fortunes, and made efforts to restore it to influence and power. Their efforts were unavailing until ten years ago an inspired statesman back from the veldt and the wars became foster- parent to a youth called Colonial Preference, now generally known as Tariff Reform. This youth was ambitious and strove to attain power. Realizing he could not gain his ends unaided, he devised the plan of forming an alliance with some old family,so he wedded the lady mentioned. Miss Protection was nothing loth, and by no means coy; she was getting somewhat the worse for wear, and had made at least two serious attempts to change her name. Moreover Tariff Reform lioped by a union of forces to be able to secure the support of the populace. Now while their united forces were naturally greater, some of the youth's adherents objected to the habits, principles, and especially to the name of the lady. It was explained that Miss Protection had surrendered her name, bought a new bonnet with ribbons of blue and merged her identity in that of Fiscal Reform. Nevertheles many of the youth's followers, objected, and when the banns were cried, cursed the proposed union, by bell, book, and candle, and deserted the united standards. They asked " Can a leopard change his spots and be other than a leopard,and can Protection be other than Protection though called Fair Trade, Reciprocity, or Tariff Reform ? " But the worst misfortune to the happiness and ambitions of the allied pair was when those who remained loyal began scrapping among themselves. The adherents of the lady con- tended that while they were willing to change their name even seventy times seven if desired, they were not going to change their principles or their methods. Among these were "Make the foreigner pay," " Find two jobs for every man," "Make everything dearer for him who sells, and everything cheaper for him who buys "and the like. Now since the followers of the lady were much more numerous, self-interested, and self-assertive, than those of the young man they soon gained ascendancy, and have now made it clear that while they are quite willing to secure all the credit they could get from the name and character of the youth, it was Protection they were out for and Pro- tection they will have or have nothing. They are not therefore a very happy family, as you can imagine. Unfortunately at the time of writing there are great differences in the rival sections, and even among the sections themselves there is heard the clash of spears. Part of the Protection section is made up of those who till the earth that it may produce bread for all, but the larger part is composed of those engaged in making by machines, raiment and other necessary things for the comfort of life, but who are consumers of the equally necessary food products of the soil. Now while such are quite ready to believe the foreigner can be made to pay in the matter of manufactured articles,they have doubts about their ability to make him pay the taxes on the foodstuffs they buy. Hence a lively quarrel has arisen and whether peace will be restored time only will reveal. Still the union. has lasted ten years and Fiscal Reform and Protection are about to celebrate their Wooden Wedding. We consider the pair ill-matched, but we are against divorce unless there is grave cause, and we trust these twain will be able to patch their differences, enjoy their celebra- tion, and live happily ever after. C.E.P.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>THE DOCTORS AND THE ACT.</p>
        <p>The doctors of Runcorn formed a panel on Monday A week ago they passed a resolution refusing to do so .</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PUBLIC MAN'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>The death took place on Sunday from pqralysis of Mr Thomas Dean, of Brackley-street, Runcorn. The deceased wes for many years a member of the Runcorn Urban Council, and had acted as chairman. He gave long and useful service on the Health Committee, of which be was chairman. He served on the Runcorn Hoard of Guardians, end was a prominent Liberal. In bowling circles he was well-known, having figured on local greens for many years. POLICE COURT RECORD.</p>
        <p>At Widnes on Monday, Plache Clarice, of no fixed abode, was charged with stealing a purse containing 19s Id, the property of Arthur Outram, of 79, Church.. street, Widnes, and was committed for trial at Liverpool Sessions. Superintendent Foster stated that the prisoner was a native of Warrington, and had been convicted at Manchester, Salford, and Warrington no fewer than sixty-two times. When the depositions were being taken prisoner became very abusive and interrupted repeatedly, awl when told by the clerk (Mr Thomas), it would do her no good to interrupt she replied, " Anybody would think they would have to hang someone the way they are preaching,"</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CENSURE. HOUSING EVILS AND CHILD DISEASES.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Urban Council received a resolution on Tuesday night from the local Trades and Labour Council, as follows : " l'hat we, the members of the Runcorn Trades and Labour Council, view with grave concern the far too frequent occurrence of contagious diseases among the children attending the elementary day schools of the town, awl 'se, knowing that three schools have recently been closed through the prevalence of diphtheria, measles, and other diseases, desire to call:the attention of the members of the Rpncorn Urban District Council, as guardians of the public health,to this very unpleasant and undesirable situation.</p>
        <p>We are of opinion that, however desirable it might be to widen a road to ensure the safety of the -public and prevent possible accident, it is as doubly urgent and important that attention should be centred on this, the meat. pressing problem awaiting solution. Tniprove- me.nts are needed on the highroad of life that takes children from infancy to adult age; safeguard... to pre serve their safe passing PO that they shall not be gripped by the throat, their lungs besieged, and their bodies made the happy hunting ground for discriminat- ing and depredatory microbes—dis Timinating, because their operations are frequently carried out in the homes of the workers; depredatory, because they take life that is valuable when national requirements demand its preservation. We cannot remain complacent and in the background while epidemic conditions prevail among the children of the community. " So long as houses are allowed to remain with only one way in and out, with side streets resembling insanitary backyards that reflect disgrace on all con- cern d, so long will disease continue." The resolution ended by urging the Council to adopt the Housing and Town Planning Act. Mr Taylor said the resolution was a direct censure upon the Health Committee and its officials. He suggested that the committee should resign and let, the Trades and LAbour Council take their place. (Laughter) ir Imison raid the resolution was based on crass ignorance. The Labour Council urged the adoption of Act which the Council had already adopted and worked strenuously.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr Posnett) said there certainly was a housing problem in Runcorn. He knew two cottage houses of two rooms each in which were housed families of nine and seven children. They could not go out because no landlord would take them—and there was consumption in each family. So far as the terms of the resolution went he personally did not mind being inultedt Eventually lanyy ghooo dm comeatr woutas o f it.referred to the Health Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A PANEL FOR ST. HELENS. Tha. medical men of St. Helens and district have provisionally agreed with the local Insurance Com- mittee to work the Insurance Act, for three months, when they will consider their position in the light of the experience thus gained. There are twenty-five doctors in the town, and they are all on the panel, and are quite in touch withtthe committee, conferences having already been held. One local medical man pointed out to a Press representative that in arriving at the number of doctors who have joined the panels throughout the country the Insurance Commissioners have, perhaps unwittingly, announced figures which are R great exaggeration of the real numbers. In St. Helens local doctors have signed for the county as well as for the borough, and the medical men in the sur- rounding townships are in the same position, having agreed to go on the panel both for the county and the i borough of St. Helens. As this s no doubt the position of practitioners in all other districts, the number of signatures obtained are far in excess of the actual number of medical men concerned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>BILYCLE TIE V CAUGHT.</head>
        <p>BILYCLE TIE V CAUGHT.</p>
        <p>Arthur Butterworth, labourer, pleaded guilty at S Helens on Monday to stealing a bicycle and a numbe of account books, value £l. John Evans, 13, Grange Park-road, traveller for Messrs Aspivall and Leedhatn, said that at 7.30 on Thursday evening last he called at 12, Lough°lme-road, in the course of his business. He left his bicycle on the footpath, and ten minutes later it was missing. Constable Lys 11 said that on Saturday night he saw prisoner wheeling the bicycle along WestfHd-stre.t, and when he came up to him Butter- worth said " the bicycle I took. I give myself up." Butterworth was sent to gaol for twenty-eight days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>WHAT DYSPEPTICS SHOULD EAT.</head>
        <p>WHAT DYSPEPTICS SHOULD EAT.</p>
        <p>A PHYSICIAN'S ADVICE</p>
        <p>"Indigestion and practically all forms of stomach trouble are, nine times of ten, due to acidity; there- fore stomach sufferers should, whenever possible, avoid eating food that is acid in its nature, or which by chemical action in the stomach develops acidity. Unfortunately, such a rule eliminates most foods which are pleasant to the taste as well as those which are rich in blood, flesh and nerve building properties. This is the reason why dyspeptics and stomach sufferers are usually so thin, emaciated and lacking in that vital energy which can only come from a well- fed lraiy. For the benefit of those sufferers who have been obliged to exclude from their diet all starchy, sweet or fatty food, and are trying to keep up a miserable existence on gluten products, I would s4gest that you should try a meal of any food or foods which you may like in moderate amount, taking immediately after- wards half a teaspoonful of bisurated magnesia in a little hot or cold water. This will neutralise any acid which may be present. or which may be formed, and instead of the usual feeling of uneasiness and fulness, you will find that your food agrees with you perfectly. Bisurated magnesia is doubtless the best food corrective and antacid known. It is not a medicine, and has no direct action on the stomach ; but by neutralising the acidity of the food contents, and thus removing the source of the acid irritation which inflames the delicate stomach lining, it does more than could possibly be done by any drug or medicine whenever necessary, but I must admit that I cannot see the sense of dosing an inflamed and irritated stomach with drugs instead of getting rid of the acid—the cause of all the trouble. Get a little bisurated magnesia from your chemist, eat what you want at your next meal, take some of the bisurated magnesia as directed above, and Bee if I'm not right."</p>
      </div>
