      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>" A REPORTER'S ADVENTURES."</p>
        <p>One of the incidents narrated in last week's column tinder this heading brought to my mind a humiliating experience which an old colleague of my own had many years ago. He went to the Assizes to report 5i local murder trial, and, getting back to the station in good time for the train by which he intended to return, he entered the last carriage and proceeded to write up his copv." For a while he became oblivious of his surroundings, but eventually it dawned upm him that he had beea there much longer than he had expected to bs. He looked out, and was amazed and disgusted to find that the train had departed some time before. Hu had inadvertently ensconced himself in an empty and disused clrriage that was not attached to the train,s.nd had become so absorbed in his work that he did not discover his serious error until too late. He missed the edition in which his account of the trial should have appeared! THAT BREAD AND CHEESE.</p>
        <p>In the course of the discussion at last week's meet- N.of the Warrington Board of Guardians, Mr. Mather incidentally observed that he had to get his own bread and cheese. f was remind3d of a far-back period when! attended the fortnightly meetings of a kindred body in Yorkshire who used to have high old times. They regularly adjourned for a substantial course dinner on the premiss, invariably concluding with coffee and thick cream in abundance. One could easily guess where the cream canoe from, oh? As to the dinner asa whole, I once ventured to ask the Chair- man i how t was done, and he explained to me that the participants . subscribed the necessary fund. I was curious aid impertinent enough to inquire further as to the amount of individual subscription,and my some- what reluctant informant replied that they paid Gd each per sitting. "But," was my not unnatural. comment "surely that does not cover the cost of a big spread like this ! " " Yes, it does," the Chairman assured me, adding with brazen effrontery, "and what's more, we've a fairly good balance in hand." There was one old Quaker member who refused to be party to. or to countenance these proceedings, and he made it his , business to turn up at the Workhouse at highly incon- venient times and to mention in the presence of the Press representatives what be saw during his unexpected visits. On one of these occasions,. a member interposed with an indignant protest, urging that those attending from outside districts were entitled to a little refreshment. Ho was asked to soy what he would consider reasonable in such circumstances, and replied that he and three others had turned out on the previous Monday on special sub-committee Work, and they certainly had had a little bread and cheese. Then the Quaker critic told how, passing through the kitchen on the day in question, he had noticed a special joint in process of cooking. He asked for whom this was being prepared, and was told it was for the very sub-committee in question. The cheese was to follow—as in a case which I once personally experienced. Along with another reporter, was " doing " a Local Government Board inquiry in the West Riding, and the inspector adjourned ten minutes for a snack. We hastened to the nearest " pub" and asked for a bit of bread and cheese. The landlord declared he had no cheese, so we accepted his offer of cold roast beef. When this arrived we had five minutes left, and, the time being up, our hospit- able host s3nt in for our delectation—a piece of cheese! ANOTHER CHEESE STORY.</p>
        <p>The cases at the Warrington Police Court on Thursday of last week, in which charges of theft arising out of the fire at Messrs. Pochins, Limited were dealt with, reminded me of another titbit. During a atmilar outbreak at a provision store, the police had their work cut out in preventing poor people in the neighbourhood from making off with some of the eatables. A constable caught one fellow in the act Of sneaking away w:th a whole cheese. The offender was equal to the occasion, however. The moment he Was convinced that the officer had him under observa- -4,09, he turned, and, handing the property to the viiplant one in blue, guilelessly aclaimed "Here, Policeman; better take charge of this cheese or else ecauebody'll be running off with it ! " PADDED ROOM FOR MINOR POETS.</p>
        <p>Reverting to the local Guardians' meeting of .rather over a week ago, I find in the report of the Visiting Commissioners in Lunacy a passing reference to a Padded room. Once when I was at the Manchester Eye Hospital which I have repeatedly taken occasion to 92kention in this column, I found in a corridor Et Patient contending with a charge nurse about his dis- charge. 11l had wiled away the time by scribbling verses for the girls' albums ! I think I have given 11. ere at least one sample from his pen. But he was o‘v homesick, and had made up his mind to leave the institution on the following Saturday. The curse, however, was adamant ; she said it Was quit, out of the question—the doctor ould not permit it. "But," pleaded the poor fellow</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Colds, Headache and Influenza Relieved in 10 Minutes!</head>
        <p>Colds, Headache and Influenza Relieved in 10 Minutes!</p>
        <p>C. G. Archer, of Brewer, Maine, says: "I have had colds and catarrh for several years. Water would run from my eyes and nose for days at a time. About four months ago I was induced to try Dr. Agnew's Catarrhal Powder, and since using the wonderful remedy I have not had an attack. It relieves in 10 minutes."</p>
        <p>Dr. Agnew's Heart Cure relieve'</p>
        <p>In Jo minute*</p>
        <p>Bold _ by 11. M.A.LorroN dc HAROLD SMlTll,Chernists, WarringtOU, Rucl Boon; CASB CLIEMISTS.</p>
        <p>"there is to be a great procession of my admiring fellow-townsmen to escort me from the station to the Town Hall, where a banquet is to be given in mine honour. A brass band, too! Think of the expense! Oh, I must go, nurse." Seeing that she remained unmoved by this pathetic picture, he further urged that his sister had promised to go for him on the day mentioned, and, indeed, vowed that she would not return home without him. "That's all right," com- mented his tormentor, "there accommodation for her on the women's side." "Now, look here, nurse," pursued the rhyming patient, "if you don't consent, I'll write reams of that alleged poetry of mine, and worry the life out of you." "That threat has no terrors for me," responded the imperturbable one, "there's a padded room over the way." INFANTILE DEATH RATE.</p>
        <p>The e litorial remarks as to this in last week's " Examiner " provoke the reminder that good milk is of supreme importance in this connection. But even respecting this prime necessity the doctor's differ. At Bradford not long ago Dr Vincent actually deprecated the practice of boiling milk, which so many authorities nave been in the habit of commending. Yet when he came to summarise his chief points, Dr Vincent seemed to me to hedge a bit. Said he: " The nutritive value of milk is diminish-d by boiling, largely owing to the skin taking some of the fat with it, but the advantage gained is that the boiling destroys disease germs, such as those of tuberculosis and typhoid. Moreover, the boiled milk skill keep sweet longer than unboiled milk if the latter has been contaminated at the farm or during traasit." These remarks, be it noted, applied to infants; for them, as for everybody, pure milk, unboiled, is preferable. But where can you get it? Mr F W Richardson, analyst to important public bodies, tells us what a large proportion of dirt there is in the milk from fairly clean dairies. S, it would appear, absolutely pure milk is not readily obtainable. For years infant mortality at Burnley was scandalously high, and ultimately a depot was established for the retailing of sterilised milk. Once, when I happened to be within about a dozen miles of that to vo, I popped over to see the place and inquire into the method, and I cannot say I was greatly surprised to find that, after all the expense of preparation had been incurred, there were medical men who condemned the whole thing as utterly wrong and mischievous! THE BIBLE AND SHAKSPERE.</p>
        <p>The lite Dr Mellor, an eminent Congregational minister who spent some years in Liverpool but was for the best part of his life at Halifax (the present Dr. Jowett was trained in his church and school there) used to say that he never trave'led without his Bible, and a copy of Shakspera's plays. The other day I read in "Sub Rosa Mr Dooley has said that the only books he has in his library are the Bible and Shakspe.re, which he declares are " bully for style." He explains that he will never read them and will never read anything else until he has read them. He uses them for purposes of defence, as a breakwater against modern literature, and behind those two books lie sits" ca'm an' contint while Hall Caine rages without. " "HAMLET."</p>
        <p>By-the-way, I have just been dipping into a back number review containing the views of Signor Tommaso Salvini of "Hamlet." The actor describes the enigmatical tragedy as a work commenting on the destiny and the events of our world. He does not believe there has ever existed, or ever will exist, a man of Hamlet's temperament, and he thinks this my account for the innumerable different interpretations of the character on the stage. After detailing how, in his opinion, it ought to be presented, he draws the conviction that, had Shakspere written his play in modern times, he would have omitted the appearance of the ghost on the stage, leaving it to the imagina- tion of the public. Lie holds that the scene grows far more impressive by excluding the ghost from the stage. As to Hamlet's insanity, Signor Salvini says the mania he assumes is a mixture of truth and illusion, and in adopting it be has the advantage of being able to scrutinise the souls of those who surround him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>FOOD-TAXERS.</head>
        <p>THE FOOD-TAXERS.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 11, 1913,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>NOW ESTIMATED AS SIX PER CENT.</head>
        <p>NOW ESTIMATED AS SIX PER CENT.</p>
        <p>"The Times" says that to make light of the single point of difference with regard to the food duties would not obly be insincere but, from a party point of view, also most impolitic. The matter has to be settled, and settled quickly, otherwise the whole party will become affected with estrangement and susplcion. We doubt if even under the severest official pressure more than a small fraction of Unionist candidates will now be prepared to stake their fortunes upon this item in the party programme . . . The Ashton speech introduced an element of uncertainty. . . „ By creating this uncertainty the Unionist leaders, inten- tionally or otherwise, have in fact invited their followers-to declare their own views frankly. In these circumstances they are bound to pay attention to the opinions which have been and are being freely expressed, and it is, of course, inevitable that there should be a certain estimating of forces. According to information, upon the accuracy of which we can confidently rely, not much more than six per cent of the Unionist members of the House of Commons are in favour of ra3aking the food duties an issue at the next election. Nor is there any reason to suppose that opinion upon this point is divided differently in the constituencies. And while no one can withhold admiration for the courage and sincerity of this small minority either at Westminster or out of doors, it would be contrary to all our notions of party co-opera- tion if they were to insist upon dictating their,own policy to a reluctant majority. But the majority-'will do well to remember that mere numbers are not every- thing. In such a case as this there is always a danger that the big battalions from generosity, as well as from a feeling of security, may fail to make it clear that they hold their own opinions with sincerity and determination.</p>
        <p>From Reynolds' Newspaper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WELSH BILL.</head>
        <p>THE WELSH BILL.</p>
        <p>MR. P. M. OLIVER'S PLEA FOR COMPROMISE.</p>
        <p>"Robbing God" Allegations Blasphemous.</p>
        <p>A striking speech in support of the Welsh Church Bill, yet pleading for a spirit of compromise, was made by Mr P M Oliver, prospective Liberal candidate for the Knutsford Division, at a meeting held in the Hall Cafe, Stockton Heath, on Monday evening, under the auspices of the Liberation Society. There was a• good attendance, presided over by Mr W H Sherburn. Among others present on the platform were: The Rev Richard Richards, of London; the Rev G Curtis, Mr P M Oliver, Captain H G Roberts, Mr E Lloyd Jones, Mr J K9nworthy and Mr It Biliington. . . .</p>
        <p>The CIIA MALIN said that, as always hi-t-ppened when privilege was touched, the old watchwords " spolia- don " and "robbery " had been bandied about in opposition to the Welsh Church Bill. It was very dangerous for some people—especially those of the party of privilege—to use such words,because they might get more than they reckoned with, a 3 some members of the House of Commons had already done. They had only to look at the men who were advocating the Bill to hesitate befoie charging them with the intention to rob anybody. Toe tame cries were heard when the Irish Church Bill was before the people, yet the man at the head of the movement for the Disestablishment of the Irish Church was one of the most devout and saintly members of the Anglican Church—William Ewart Gladstone. (Applause.) We British people prided ourselves on our love of freedom, and sometimes fancied that we possessed it, because we bad more liberty than used to prevail. But they had only to go into villages where only one Church was in power and where its officers were ratber narrow-minded,io realize how little religious freedom the poor people really enjoyed in those remote places. They were often told by Anglicans that members of all denominations were equal in the sight of Gel, yet under the p esent system one denomination held the legal superiority. It was chiefly to do away with that anomaly that the present Bill was , passing through the House of Commons. (Applause.) The Rev R Richards dealt with the question in a comprehensive manner. Dealing with the principles which underlay the Bill, he first emphasised the right of all citizens to ecoality of treatment by the State, irrespective of their religious beliefs. The movement for Disestablishment was not a matter of expediency; a great principle had been working through ages past towards that consummation. The State,they demanded, should be absolutely neutral in the matter of religion. All that religion wanted from the State was a fair field aid no favonr. He (the speaker) believed in free trade in religion, without preferential tariffs for any Caurch. Having referred to the fact that several Bishops and other prominent Churchmen supported Disestablishment on the ground that the Church should h tve the right to self-government, untrammelled by State regulations, the speaker pleaded the right of a n .tion to determine whether it should have an Estab- lished Church or not. The Church in Wales had suffered through being Established at the time Wales was being conquered. She was USED BY UNSCRUPULOUS GOVERNMENTS, as a tool in the corqaest of the people, in the endeavour not so much to evangelise them as to Anglicise them. During the 155 years ending in 1870 there was not a single Bishop in Wales who could speak the Welsh language. To-day the lulk of the Welsh people were not in the fold of the State Church but in the various folds of the Nonconformist Churches. Only one fourth of the religious activities of Wales was in the hands of the State Church ;three-fourtbs were in the hands of the Nonconformists Churches. Of 6,500 places of worship in Wales, only 1,803 belonged to the State Church, while 4,600 belonged to the Free Churches. There were 6,000 ministers and preachers, but only 1,500 belonged to the State Church. Of the communicants, 193,000, or 9 per cent. of the people, were attached to the State Cuurch, and 550,000 or 27 per cent., to the Nonoo n- formists. The State Cnurch provided seating accom- modation for only 22 out of every 100 people in Wales, while the Free Churches provided for 74 per cent, To-day the Church was making efforts to</p>
        <p>UNDO THE MIsCHIEF OF BYGONE DAYS, but it was too late to re-claim the nation. It had passed out of the Church's grip. People were accustomed to Free Church life and were not able to go back to the yoke of bondage, especially while the Church was Established and bad no freedom. He believed thit many Nonconformis•s would go to the Church, when she was free. Disestablishment would not be a gain to the Free Churches in the long run, as far as numbers were concerned. There was a great pull to the Church—the social pull. The aristocracy and the people in the best social position went to Church, and when the Church was free she would attract many Nonconformists who would not go now, while the Church had no liberty to chose her own ministers. Many of the clergy in Wales felt the position most keenly. For instance, if Mr Lloyd George—(applause)—became Prime Minister, he would have to appoint archbishops and bishops and that must be a terrible prospect to some clergy who—to use the words of one of their number—hated Mr Lloyd George worse than the devil. (Laughter.) In the interests of the Church and of her dignity, surely the time had come when she should be free to elect her own officers and work out her own salvation it, her own way. Wales was practically unanimous in its demand for Disestablishment and what reason was there to deny a nation that for which it had been fighting constitutionally and patiently—perfect religious equality ? (Applause.) The speaker next dealt with the right of the State when it disestablished a National Church to control the distribution of the property of which that Church had been the trustee, in the national interests. The bulk of the money con- sisted of tithes which were practically it tax upon the whole of the cultivated land of the nation. No doubt tithes began in a voluntary way but slackness set in and the State then made them compulsory. Referring to the arguments of those who contended that advoc des of Disendowruent should favour the total abolition of tithes, the speaker pointed out that such a course would mean giving a huge present to the landlords. It had been suggested that there should be concurrent endow- ment of the various denominations, but Free Church- men could not accept compulsory tithes. They would be stultifying themselves, for it was akainst their principles to accept anything but voluntary gifts to sustain G )d's work. (Applause') Having referred at some length to the provisions of the Bill and characterised it as a generous measure the speaker said that in our own Colonies and in Ireland the Church had profited by Disestablishment ; it had been a blessing in disguise. England and Wales were the only countries where the Episcopal Church was Established to-day, and they must follow in the wake of those whose Churches had been freed for their own advantage. (Applause.) Establishment was a barrier to unity. It created a chasm in the nation's life, elevating one section of the people to a fictitious posi- tion of superiority.As Nonconformists they objected to being looked doevn upon and patronized; be would rather be persecuted than patronized. (Applause.) Their plea was that, however much the Churches might differ in doctrine and worship, they should all rest on the same foundation of religious equality. (Applause). Mr P M Oliver said he had never pretended to be an expert on what was, after all, a very difficult question, but he was there to speak as 000 who had a little commonsense and, he trusted, also a little Christian charity. (Applause.) The first point he would press home was an argument which would perhaps be more suited to a purely political platform --the groat national demand on the part of Wales for Disestablishment. The electoral figures for Wales were perhaps the most portentous to be found any- where. There were 34 Parliamentary seats in Wales and Monniouth, and 31 of them returned merahers pledged to Disestablishment. Not one of the other three could speak Welsh and all of them, by a strange coincidence, had double-barrelled names. (Laughter.) If the representation of the United Kingdom was on the same lines, it would mean that there would be 610 members supporting Disestablisment and only 40 opposed to it. The total votes cast in Wales for Government supporters were 226,000, while only 117,000 were recorded in opposition, giving a majority of 109,000 for Disestablishment. That meant that in every one of the thirty-four seats there was an average majority of 3,218 for Disestablishment. (Applause.) In seventeen constituencies the majority was greater than the total Opposition poll. The Opposition majority in the three seats they held was 362. The majority of the Government in the other thirty-one seats was 108,000. (Applause.) Such figures could not</p>
        <p>be juggled with and they could only prove one thing— that there was an overwhelming demand on the part of the Welsh ration behind the Government Bill. The Bishop of Chester, speaking not long ago, realized that, but he had refuge in the plea that Wales was not a nation. Wales was a nation and would be a nation—(applause)— and it was because of its national spirit tbat we were faced with its demand for Disestablishment. As i Liberals, continued Mr Oliver, they must realize that ' the Liberal party had been pledged to the measure for many years. In 1887 Mr Joseph Chamberlain, who was just leaving the Liberal party, wrote to 'The Baptist," an ardent plea for Welsh Disestablishment which, he said," ruu,t be past poned to the demands of 3,000,000 disloyal Irishmen." It was tbe same then as it was to-day—Home Rule and Welsh Disestablishment together —but one must wonder why Mr Chamberlain was in power for practically twenty years and did not raise a finger for the cause he advocated. In 1891 the Liberal Government introduced the first measure for Welsh Disestablishment In 1895, speaking to the National Liberal Federation, Lord Rosebery devoted pr actically the whole of his oration to the cause of Welsh Disestablishment and if anybody wished a fair, reasoned, and eloquent exposition on the subject let bi n turn to what Lord Roseherry said in 1895. He (the speaker) was waiting to hear what Lord Rosebery would say in 1913. In 1909 the Liberals once more introduced the measure. It was withdrawn through lack of time and the Prima Minister s.id " We have decided to make the Bill the first Government measure and to pass it through all its stages in this House next year." That was a definite pledge which they were not able to carry out, owing to the rejection of the Budget and the struggle in the Home of Lords, but, if there was any honesty in polities, the pledge must be redeemed by the present Government. (Applause). To give preference to a Church which was distinctly the Church of a minority was not only wrong for the State but degrading to the Church. If he were a Welsh Churchman he would not like to receive assistance or help from the State when he knew that his Church did not represent anything like a majority of the people. Even if the Church was the Church of a majority, though there was nothing wrong or immoral in establishment, he regarded it as contrary to true Christian doctrine. The Christian Church was founded as a protest against an Established religion in JudPa and it triumphed as a protest against an Established religion in Rome. It seemed to him that it was only when the Church was free and unfettered that it would triumph in the future. (Applause) On the question of disendowment, he had been what was called</p>
        <p>AN ADVOCATE OF COMPROMISE. His position was known to the Liberation Society and the fact that they had asked him to speak sn their platform was a fine testimonial to the spirit of compromise and fair-mindedness which actuated the society. He did not know whetber the Church Defence Society would offer him the same facilities. One of the reasons which influenced him in favour of compromise was that there was so much work to be done in the alleviation of poverty and sick- ness,in which social reformers desired the help not only of Nonconformists but of Churchmen, that it seemed to him a pity that there should be religious strife and bitterness, and anything that would tend to do away with that spirit would have his support. There had unfortunately been bitterness and suspicion between Church and chapel in Wales in the past and he was afraid it might continue in the future, unless they could arrive at some form of compromise. Another point was that it was very diffls.ult to know exactly what was in the minds of the pious founders when they left their endowments. He agreed with Bishop Gore, in his statement in I Convocation in 190 : " It was totally impossible to say wbat the wishes of the pious founders would be, After the great changes ,shich had come about from the time of the Reformation. The whole matter of endow- , meats must be judged quite apart from the wishes of founders who left money centuries aga and knew nothing at all about the present situation." Notwith- standing that difficulty, he (Mr Oliver) thought that if they could, by compromising a little and by offering more than generous terms to the Chirch, bring the matter through as an agreed matter, they might yield just a little bit of their ground. The endowments were given—some for national purposes, some for religious purposes and others fur the saying of Masses. The saying of Mum was a trust which the Church could not now perform ' and those endowments, or a proportion of them, along with those which were given for national purposes, should be offered up willingly and voluntarily by the Church. Some of the endowments were probably given specifi- cally for religious purposes, and those they could leave. They had left very many of them already and the present measure was really one of compromise. Of the total income received by the Church from her ancient endowments (£241,000), £143,000 was left and only about £lOO,OOO was taken. £296,000 was the total amount of the voluntary offerings to the Church in Wales ; therefore to make up for the withdrawal of endowments, all that was necessary was that the amount of those offerings should be increased to £400,000. The voluntary contributions to the Nonconformist churches in Wales were £BlB,OOO. (Applause.) As a result of compromise adopted during the passage of the measure—and be was glad to think Mr G 1 tdstone initiated it—the inccme of the Church had already been increassd to the extent of £17,000 a year. The Government having shown a spirit of generosity, had already gone far enough and they must now turn to the Church and say: "If we are to compromise any further, if we are to show any more generosity, the condition precedent to that is that the Church must show a bet,er and more Christian spirit in this matter than she has hitherto done." (Applause.) It was per- fectly obvious that the Church had a legal right to the endowments; nobody could deny that. The landowner had a legal right to the commons which he had enclosed and the sweeter had a legal right to the profits of the workman whom he had debased. But one expected from the Church of Christ a higher view of property than one expected from the landowner or the sweater. What had grieved him more than anything else in the controversy vr,s the</p>
        <p>0 '., It 0 .., k I i-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>I WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT 1 COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>I WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT 1 COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>PADGATE SEWERAGE.</p>
        <p>The monthly treating of the Warrington Rural District Council was held in Bewsey Chambers on Wed- nesday, Mr 0 Mather presiding. There were also present :—The Rev A M Mitchell, Dr Murray, Messrs A Brooks, J Backhouse, W Furness, J W Knowles, J C Sutton (clerk), B S Newcombe (surveyor), U H Burnage (alsistant surveyor), F Kerfoat (inspector, and Dr Sephton (medical offioer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>INCREASE IN RATES PROBABLE.</head>
        <p>INCREASE IN RATES PROBABLE.</p>
        <p>The following letter dated December 11th bad been received from the Secretary of the Local Government Board :—" I am directed by the Local Government Board to acknowledge the receipt of your letter of the 15th nit with reference to the question of sewerage bed disposal of the sewage of the parish of Poulton-with- Fearnhead, and I am to state that the Board have observed the remarks contained in recent annual reports of the Medical Officer of Health for the rural district was to the necessity for a thorough scheme of sewerage•. and sewage disposal for this parish. In these circum- stances it seems to them that the Council should with out delay proceed with the execution of a scheme. It must rest however with the District Council to decide whether they will adopt the scheme eutlinel by Mr Commits for them, on the merits of which the Board cannot undertake to express any opinion until it has been completely designed, and until further details, including plans, sections and detailed estimates, have been placed before them, accompanied by estima•es, on the enclosed forms and by an application of the Council for sanction to a loan for the execution of the scheme. The Board will then direct a local inquiry by one of their inspectors into the application. I am to add that the periods usually allowed by the Basil for the repayment of loans for purposes of sewerage and sewage disposal are as follows : Land and way leaves sixty years, brick or stoneware sewers, permanent buildings and similar works, thirty years; machinery, boilers, engines, compressors and ejectors with fittings, fifteen years." Mr NEWCOMI3E said be had worked out estimates based on the different periods allowed by the Local Government Board for the repsyment of loan for the various portions of the works. For the part scheme it would mean a rate of 2s. 2d., but for the whole scheme a rate of 3s. 6d. would be necessary. That was based on 4 per cent, for interest and Mri Sutton thought they might get some reduction on that There might also be some substantial economies in the carrying out of the scheme Mr FURNESS raid the matter was a very important one and he proposed that the question be deferred until the next meeting and in the meantime copies of the estimates be forwarded to each member.</p>
        <p>This was agreed to. MEDICAL OFFICERS REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officers report which was adopted showed that for the month ending January Ist., there had been 18 births and 22 deaths. The death rate was equal to 18.7 per thousand as against 20 1 the average for the last three months and 15.7 for the clrresponding period last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>YOUR NEXT-DOOR NEIGHBOUR.</head>
        <p>YOUR NEXT-DOOR NEIGHBOUR.</p>
        <p>Doubtless you are often able to render some little service t 3 your next-door neighbour, and very probably she sometimes helps you. If you notice occasionally that her face is pale and bloodless, and her eyes lack lustre, then you will sympathise with her, for you will gather that she is undergoing the penalties of her sex. Her head feels as though it would burst. In her back and down her sides she has frightful pains, and she feels such a sense of general depression that life for the time loses all interest. You can obtain her lifelong gratitude if you tell her how the suffering , can be avoided. In thousands of cases similar to he's Mrs. Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound has brought quick relief. It is a woman's medicine for women's ailments. It acts in harmony with Nature, restoring and preserving health. The greater the suffering the greater the relief. A lady at Otley, near Leeds, writes :—" Thanks for your most wonderful medicine. I have been a different woman since I took Mrs. Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Com- pound." All suffering women should write at once for a private book to women only, sent quite free by the Lady Manager, The Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 42a, Hampstead Road, London, N.W., who will also send the medicine direct at 2s. 9d. a bottle, or 4s. 6d. for a very large bottle. Any chemist will supply it at these prices.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>EARLESTOWN "CO-OP." AND INSURANCE.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN "CO-OP." AND INSURANCE.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday Mr Rupert Gwynne, on behalf of Viscount Wolmer, asked the Fecretary to the Treasury whether the Earlestown Industrial Co-operative Society has yet received the exemption order, under section 47 of the National Insurance Act, for which they applied four months ego; if so, whether the employees of the society have been notified of the fact; and whether the Government have yet teturned to them the unnecessary contribu• tions which they have been obliged to pay every week owing to the delay, and for which they have received no corresponding benefits. Mr Masterman : The special order referred to came into force on December 21st, and the Commissioners assume that the society, to whom intimation was sent on that day, has informed its employees of the fact. The answer to the last part of the question is in the negative. No application for repayment has been made to the Commissioners on behalf of the society or its employees, and any repayment of contributions, even where it was legally possible under the Act, could only be made after application from the persons concerned and careful consideration of the merits of the particular case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Buckley-street P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Buckley-street P.S.A.</p>
        <p>- The meeting of the above was held as usual, oy Sunday. There was a good attendance when the chair was taken by Mr W Shaw. Mr Harrison, of Liverpoo.l gave the address. Mr Tomkinson was the soloisqms songs " De Profundis " and " Recall the days" being well rendered. The lesson was read by Mr R Syers,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. N.,.."‘...1/4",.N,-,NewN,-,v-v-N•i..v</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. N.,.."‘...1/4",.N,-,NewN,-,v-v-N•i..v</p>
        <p>e•V.‘/,</p>
        <p>SOME JANUARY BIRTHDAYS.</p>
        <p>We are all interested in Birthdays. At any rate we are always interested in our own, and don't often forget which month it comes in.</p>
        <p>I am about to try and interest you a little in other peoples birthdays, and purpose mentioning a few good and famous people who were born in the cold month of January _</p>
        <p>_ .. First of all let me name that of Isaac Pitman who was born just a hundred years since last Saturday. All journalists and indeed everybody who writes shorthand ought to be interested in this event, for it was Isaac Pitman who invented the system which almost everybody now uses who writes shorthand. The fine old man died in 1899 when he was 86 years old. On January sth another Isaac came into the world. This was the very famous Isaac Newton who was born in 1642 in a little village in Lincolnshire. Every boy and girl has read of the thoughtful man who saw the apple fall from the tree and who by reason- ing the matter out discovered the law of gravition. He was a great toiler and enriched the world greatly by his knowledge and discoveries. Best of all he was a sincere and good man. He lived to be 85 and is buried in Westminster Abbey.</p>
        <p>Another discoverer of natural law was born on January 17th. This was Benjamin Franklin who began his career in 1706. He was a poor lad and had little education but lived to become an author. The lad who began work at the age of 12 became a philosopher and a famous statesman. We have all read how he sent up his kite in a thunderstorm with a key tied to its tail to make an experiment with the electrical current and how he was able to suggest the lightning conductor. His life is a fine example of perseverance. He also lived to be a very old man and saw 84 busy years. The next January birthday will not be forgotten by my Scottish readers. It was on January 25th 1759 that Robert Burns was born at Alloway. Surely there never was such a sweet and gifted singer as Bobbie Burns and Scotland never knew a brighter mind and kinder heart.</p>
        <p>Following soon after on the birthday list conies the very famous Christian soldier Charles George Gordon, born January 28th, 1833. We know him best, and all the world knows him as General Gordon, the man who was as brave as he was kind and as kind as he was brave. We remember him best, not as a soldier but as the friend of the orphan and outcast. Every boy should read the life of this good man.</p>
        <p>Yet another who came at the end of the month, also on January 28th, not eight years later, the Welsh Workhouse boy was born, who was to become famous as the African explorer and the discoverer of Livingstone. This was Henry M. Stanley, born in 1841, and gratefully remembered by all who admire courage and perseverance. Yes. January has some good birthdays in it, and it is rather nice to think of what good folks began their life amid the bleak winds and snows of our coldest month. JOHNNY AGAIN.</p>
        <p>It was at the New Year party that Johnny had been warned that he must put on his best manners. §everal times Johnny tried to whisper to his father, and at last was pulled up sharp by the remark: "Johnny this is bad manners. If you want to know anything speak out. " Well, Father" said Johnny "I wanted to know why the lady next door to us has got such a red nose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LIFE'S LESSONS.</head>
        <p>LIFE'S LESSONS.</p>
        <p>A. child came close to his teacher's side, His book tight clasped in his little hand. "Teacher," he said, with wistful eyes, "We're comit g to words that I don't understand; I've turned the pages over and over, And the words are so big, and they're all so new; When we come to the lesson where they are put, Oh, teacher, I don't know what I'll do!"</p>
        <p>The teacher smiled at the troubled face, And tenderly stroked the curly head. " Beforo we reach them I think you will learn "The way to read them," she gently said ; "But if you shouldn't I'll help you then, And don't you think that the wisest plan Is to learn the lessoo that comes to-day, And learn it the very best you can ? "</p>
        <p>And it seems to me it is so with us; We look at the days that are still ahead— The days that perchance may never be ours—. With a pitiful I,)nging and nameless dread. But surely the teacher who gives the task Will lovingly watch as we try to read With faltering tong and tear-dimmed eyes And will help His children in time of need. Charlotte L. Seaver.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
