      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>ATTITUDE OF TIIE CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ATTITUDE OF TIIE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Fla was not a Churchman, but he had relatives who had done very much for the Church and he had the deepest respect for the Church. But the attitude of ansolutely no compromise, which the Church had taken up, was an attitude which they could only deplore. When a vast majority of people were compelled ta pay Lithe for the upkeep of a religion in which they did not lelieve, it was not only wrong for the ma l who paid, but degrading to the Church which received the money. They heard a great deal from Church defence platforms to the effect that the money taken from the Church was going to be devoted to musty libraries and museums, and to the payment of salaries to University professors who already got much more than ' they deserved. As a matter of fact, not a fiftieth of the endowments—£2,ooo a year—was to be given to the National Museum of Wales and £12,000 a year would go to the three Universities. All the rest was to be employed for charitable or public purposes and he was informed that in the country neighbo Jr hoods, district nurses and cottage hospitals were alms- the only objects that would be supported in that wayt Over £BO,OOO would be set apart for those purposes. The disciples of Christ were sent out not only to preach. but to heal, and to talk of" robbing God in such a connection as that, even if the words were spoken from a pulpit and in the surplice of a priest, seemed to him</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>NOTHING SIIORT OF BLASPHEMY.</head>
        <p>NOTHING SIIORT OF BLASPHEMY.</p>
        <p>(Applause.) -Money,--WhiCh—w-a-S—ieally, the nation's money, and which was to be transferred to the allevia- tion of sick and poor, could not be said to be taken from God. It was being given to God, just as much as if it went to the payment of the stipend of a curate. Mr. Oliver expressed regret that the Church showed a disposition to grasp every penny, snd concluded by saying that as the Government had made 's compromise and had given up 2 L 7,000 which would otherwise have gone to the sick and poor, the Church might abate some of her claims and show some sign of generosity, some- thing approaching the spirit of Christianity, SO that the matter might be Eettled, and bitterness and raneour cease in Wales. (Applause). The Rev G Curtis moved a resolution welcoming the Welsh Church Bill as an earnest effort to meet the just</p>
        <p>demands of the Welsh people for a full measure of religious equality. Mr E Lloyd Jones seconded. He said that if he believed the measure would injure the spiritual or social activities of the Church in any way, he would not support it. 'the resolution was passed ;with five dit sentient&amp;</p>
      </div>
