      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>GUARANTEE</head>
        <p>GUARANTEE</p>
        <p>STEADY WORK</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 11, 1913.</p>
        <p>"WOMEN IN POLITICS," MISS CROSFIELD AND MSS THIRZA POTTS ADDRESS THE JUNIOR LIBERNLS.</p>
        <p>"Women in Politics" formed the subject of a lecture by Bliss Thine Potts, of Manchester, at the monthly meeting of the Junior Liberal Association, Horse- market-street, on Tuesday evening. Miss Trude Crosfleld presided. The meeting was an open one and the ladies on this occasion outnumbered the gentlemen. Miss CROSFIELD said an opinion which was formed of total ignorance and nothing else was no opinion at all, but was simply prejudice. An astonishing fact eas that a number of eeople who boldly condemned Women's Suffrage were totally ignorant of the move- ment. No one should close his or her mind to it until they had both heard a good speech or two or read that masterly book "Women and Labour." She understood it was always admitted that woman's intelligence was equal to that of man. Politics Welched her as closely as man and true Liberalism consisted in granting representation to those who paid taxation. Democracy was apt to forget and leave aside those who were not represented. If it was admitted that the vote was a protection to the working.roan then the five million women workers in England ought to bave the same protection. To work was the right and duty of every human being and the fact that half the nation was still unrepresented should stir Liberals and their idea of fair play to grant the franchise to women. The old timid objection that there were more women than men was in her opinion an extra reason for the vote being granted. Darwin spoke of the survival of the fittest and it looked as if the women judging by their preponderance, were the fitter. (Laughter.) What a loss to tte country's welfare when many of its fittest subjects were debarred from all direct voice in its laws and acts! She would like to draw the attention to how the grant- ing of the vote had succeeded in other countries Norway started it ia 1907 and Sweden followed a few years later. Denmark had followed suit with a tremendous vote in favour of women's suffrage-05 against 12. All the women workers and thinkers of this country, together with our best scientific and political leaders, were ardent advocates of the suffrage. In the words of Mr Ellis Griffiths, MP, "Let men and women co-operate in solving the problems of human ' misery, working together for the joy and happiness of the home." (Applause). Miss Potts gave an exceptionally able advocacy of the cause of women's suffrage. She had never yet heard of women canvassers being refused at the time of an election to advocate the claims of one politician or another, yet they bad been denied the first and fundamental right of the vote. It was only in a country like England that such a condition of things would be tolerated. The time had come when many of them were saying "We are going to have none of this." Until they got the vote they were not going to do much else. At the present time they had the energies of the best women workers locked up in the question of the vote. It was not that they were not keenly interested in other questions. Many of them were deeply interested in temperance, housing reform and land reform ; but they were compelled to leave all those important questions on one side whilst they advocated the fundamental principle of the vote. If the vote were granted, it would let bore a body of social reformers whe would be a valuable acquisition to the Liberal ranks. Referring to the White Slave Act, Miss Potts said the Act would have been very different if women had had the vote. As it was, the women largely contributed to the passing of it. She did not believe the balance of parties would be greatly changed by the granting of the franclab e, but on the other hand she thought a large percentage of women banded together would help forward very strongly that type of legislation such as temperance. They desired a body of people who would rise above party. The balance of the thinking women of the country was on the side of the suffrage. They had a curious illustration of that fact not very long ago. Mrs Humphry Ward made a complaint that nearly all the high school mistresses were suffragists and that they were bringing up the girls to be suffragists which was a very serious thing for anti-suffragism. Did they think that the mistresses were appointed because they were suffragists? The economic position of women had led raany people to think seriously on the question. She believed it would improve the economic position of womsn (and it surely needed improvement). If they examined the mass of sweated workers they would find that nine. tenths of them were women. But of the five million women workers half were engaged in the professional class whilst the other half were in industrialism, earn- ing a weekly wage. There was no good in arguing the fact whether women should be in the industrial world. It was largely duo to the substitution of the work in the factory for the work in the home and furthermore the substitution of the machinery for brute force. In all industrial undertakings muscle was counting for less and less Physical force was wasted on a delicate machine. These were the causes which have led to women going out and earning their own living. They had evidence that the vote had an appreciable effect on the conditions of labour and if granted the franelise, women would have a direct effect on the wages paid to women workers. She held that they could not benefit one-half of the community without bettering the other half. If they injured one, they injured the other. They as men and women stood together, and it passed her compre- hension why some men did not see that fact. Women's work was cheap, and employers would have cheap labour, which meant they would have the work of the women in preference to the men ; and in consequence many men were unemployed. She wanted woman to stand on her own merits. She did not wonder when men retorted that women were the worst blacklegs they had to face. The only curse open was, for man and woman to stand together and agitate for equal pay and equal work. Politics were becoming more domesticated, as instanced by the passing of such acts as the Children's, Employers' Liability and Insurance Acts, and women had the right in the making of laws which regulated Ike home life. The only way they could attack prejudice was by trying to substitute a nobler idea. (Applause). Mr E LLOYD JONES in opening the discussion said he believed the quality of the fraughise to women was not really a woman's question but tbat it was a question for the men. In his opinion men would gain considerably greater protection themselves by granting the women the right to vote. The fact that woman had not the power to protect herself by the vote made unfair competition in the industrial world. On the proposition of Mrs RANSOME, seconded by Mr G TOULMIN a hearty vote of thanks was accorded Miss Potts and Miss Crosfield.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the meeting Mr Llosd Jones presented Mks Crosfield on behalf of the Junior Liberals with a framed photograph of herself in com- pany with Mr and Mra A II Crc,sfield taken at the opening of the club in June last.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>CAPTAIN BALFOUR.</head>
        <p>CAPTAIN BALFOUR.</p>
        <p>L' Among his numerous other honours and titles, Mr. Asquith is now entitled to the rank of Captain. This is by virture of the Royal Warrant granting the rank of Captain to the Elder Brethren of Trinity House . . . Mr Balfour will also be entitled to the rank of Captain in the future. " Warrington Examiner."] Three cheers for Captain Balfour T. Since " Captain" he must be, (The " Die Hard " crew refused him And shouted " 8.M.G.") You'll see him on the captain's bridge A-whispering orders low, Suggesting to the enginernen, (Weird things that live below). That they should go " full steam ahead," But to the old tars bent He'll give due weight in cautious tones To every argument. "For, just suppose those enginemen (They're very treachercus) Speed up their steeds infernal And make it dangerous. Doubtless it's better, when you view The case from every side For tkose on deck (to avoid shipwreck) To let her genCy glido. And not release the hawFers That bind her to the shore The ship's quite safe in harbour And—what can we do more ? " So though you might not think it well That " Captain "be should ho Yet now some of the " Die Hards " say That Boner L. M. G.</p>
        <p>ONTARIO'S FAVOURABLE CLIMATE.</p>
        <p>The mildness of Ontario's climate is well illustrated by an extract from an Ontario Paper, which states that the weather was so mild in December that a fruit farmer was able to plant his new strawberry plants on a farm in Grimsby during that month. Situated in the Niagara district, Grimsby is right in the centre of that famous fruit belt v.hich produces such an abundance of peaches, grapes and other semi-tropical fruits. The tempering influence of Lake Ontario is said to be responsible for the favourable climate which means so much to the fruit grower, particularly in the early part of the year. It is not generally remembered that the most southerly point of Ontario is over 100 miles south of the Manitoba lino, and in the same latitude as Rome and Southern France. That is the reason farmers are able to grow even tobacco and peaches in the open air. The farmers in Southern Ontario are able to commence operations earlicr than those in the West. Early vegetable growing, which has been much extended of late years, is said to be the most profitable form of agriculture in all Canada.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>SMORE NUISANCE.</head>
        <p>THE SMORE NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>EVILS OF AIR POLLUTION.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL TO THE " EXAMINER."</p>
        <p>[Ur. Arthur G. Ruston B.A„ B.Sc., of the Depart- ment of Agriculture, Leeds, University, has written two of articles for the " Examiner ' on the evils of air pollution caused by the smoke nuisance Mr. Ruston is recognize' as one of the great authori- ties on this question and his articles are a valuable contribution to the controversy.] [FIRST ARTICLE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>AIR POLLUTION BY COAL SMOKE.</head>
        <p>AIR POLLUTION BY COAL SMOKE.</p>
        <p>Among the many subjects that in recent years have engaged the attention of those interested in the improvement of the public health of our city com- munities, the question of atmosphere pollution has obtained a place which is slowly gaining in import- ance. The fiubjeet is commonly discussed, however, as if the pollution were rigidly confined within the municipal boundaries, and solely of importance in its beating upon the health of the ratepayer, and the appearance and durability of his property. It must be apparent, however, even to the casual observer on his approach to the town, that the question has a s'gnificance for the agriculture and horticulture of the neighbouring semi-urban districts which cannot altogether be estimated, but which must be very great. This aspect has been, as yet, but little investi- gated or even di=cussecl, and in view of the undoubted damage done by smoke pollution to vegetation in general, and if you like to your own garden in particular, some account of the work done in this direction may be interesting. The original ssurce of this pollution is the coal itself, and coal is far from being an elementary Sub- stance, as a glance at the following analysis of two samples will show</p>
        <p>- , • Common Coal used for Boiler Good House Coal. Purposes. Carbon 7647 per cent. 6930 per cent. Hydrogen Nitrogen 133 „ • 110 ~ Ash Sulphur Chlorine 027 , 027 „ Arsenic It will be seen that a good coal contains a 'large amount of carbonaceous matter, leaves a small residue or ash on burning, and has a low sulphur and arsenic content. On burning under either domestic or boiler conditions, some of the carbonaceous matter burns, the gaseous products escaping into the air in the form either of carbon monoxide or carbon dioxide, some distils and comes over as tar, and some goes up the chimney unconstimed, being deposited as soct. When one considers the varying conditions under which coal is burnt, it is obvious that the character and composition of soot must vary considerably. It might, indeed, be expected that the higher tempera- ture and stronger draught of a factory furnace would produce a soot more by mechanical removal and less by mechanical removal and less by incomplete com- ttustion than the domestic grate, in other words, a. soot containing more ash and less tar. Not only is this the case, but the soot varies in character with the distance from the grate at which it has been deposited; still another factor, which will influence the character and composition cf the soot, will be the kird of coal used.</p>
        <p>The following analyses illustrate these facts very clearly : ANALYSES OF SOOT FROM THE UNIFORMITY. Domestic Soot. Boiler soot. Base. 70ft. up. Top 110 ft Per cent. Per cent. Per cent. Per cent Carbon Hydrogen 1%. itiogen Ash Tar Sulphur Chlorine s'l9 011 1•46 1•60 Arsenio Acidity as H 037 1'33 058 056 The difterencea between the domestic and boiler soot, thus obtained from the same coal, are striking and instructive. The sample from the base of the boiler chimney. is little more than fine dust ; practically all, the hydro carbons have been burnt, while what few have volatilised have at the temperature there prevailing no chance of recondensing. Further up, the more volatile constituents, ammonium chloride and sulphate, and the arsenic accumulate. The sample of domestic soot is typical of all those which have been analysed by reason of its high content of tar and volatile sub- stances and its low content of ash. The amount of tar may even in some cases of domestic soot reach as high as 40 per cent.</p>
        <p>of the ri-ctual amount of soot emitted from factory chimneys,it is clearly impossible to obtain trustworthy data. It will vary with the quality of the coal, the size and structure of the chimney, and the method of firing.</p>
        <p>Eiperimental evidence goes to show that one would not be very wide of ttie mark in stating that as far as the householder is concerned, for every tcn of coal he buys, one hundredweight goes up the chimney uncon- sumed ; while as far as the factory is concerned there is at least aloes of one stone out of every ton of coal. If we take 6 per cent as the minimum loss on the eiitimated yearly consumption of coal for houshcld purposes, and 05 per cent as the minimum loss on the estimated factory consumption for the United King- dom, the loss of fuel in the form of soot will be: 6 per cent. on 32 million tons (domestic consumption) .................... 05 per cant on 100 million tons (factory consumption).</p>
        <p>500,000</p>
        <p>Or a total loss of t 0052,420,00 That is to sly, nearly 2} million tons of soot annually escap3 into the air owing to smoke pollution. Of this a certain amount falls in situ, of which a fraction sticks by virtue of the tar which it contains. The great bulk is blown away, polluting the atmosphere for miles round all our grea,t manufacturing centres. The portion which deposits -where the fuel is burnt has been estimated by several independent observers, tv.ing different m3thods.</p>
        <p>Undoubtedly the m—o;t complete records have been kept at Leeds.</p>
        <p>With a view to estima'%ing the nature and amount of these impurities in the air of an industrial town, ten representative stations were selected in Leeds, end one on the University Experimental Farm at Garforth some 7 miles due east of Leeds. Of the Leeds stations some were in the heart of the industrial area, and otheis in various residential quarters. These impuri- ties will be partly in the form of suspended matter, consisting of soot, tar, sand and mineral muter; and partly in solution, .consisting of sulphurous and sulphuric acids or their salts, chlorides, largely in the form of hydrochloric acid or common salt, and nitrogenous matter.</p>
        <p>Le-e-ds lies on north-eastern fringe of the Yorkshire coalfield, the industrial area being almost entirely confined to the southern half of the city, whilst other industrial tciwns and coal-mining villages lie not far distant on this side in all directions, ranging from the south-east, round by south, to west. To the north and east the ciq- merges into a vast tract of purely agricultural country. The local character of the solid impurities is well illustrated, therefore, by the fact that they diminish rapidly as ,we pass north- wards from the centre of the town Thus at the Philosophical Hall, in the centre of the town, they represent roughly 850'ls. per acre ; while at the Observatory, less than :a mile to the north, they fall to 400Ibs. por acre, c.,r less than one-half; and at Westwood Lane, little more than two miles to the north, they represent .only 1501bs. to the acres, or little more than one-sixth of the deposit in the centre of the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <p>.. SUSPENDEI) MkrTER 1.........• SOO T • . , - -'-' TA ‘IZ A S i-i i - ' Aro .. ; • 1.0:1.1)s FORI.ii. f. ' 2:IIUNSLE.I' • - • 2 . 3 f.s!.f SI•oN HILI.. - • 'f —. 4•1'1111.0i:01,11tCALITALI. . . .. :72.414-r.lol\il..i • ' B„kkAII.I.V !•• •g' 7. 015513$01A1(,1•A' -,''''' • 8. kißNsrAt.l.- .. 9 \‘'` , i Nkf)(&gt;l)l...Vkii: • , • . .:.,-' ,:IinIMUNDHA \ , ' . .: ,?:: WA • 5 ' • ' t ' . ' • ":4,,,:.-J%::'•:. :: .., .. , • • i• 5yct.,..•.,,,,:-,j, ~ 1 'llit .' !. ;I. i ' 1 ..i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <p>The influence of the industrial centre upon the amount of these solid impurities stands out most con- spicuously, for at Leeds Forge they amount to nearly 1,900 lbs per acre, and at Huuslet ti 1,550 lbs per acre as compared to 901bs at Roundhay. In other words, in the chief industrial centres of Leeds the solid impurities due to smoke pollution are roughly twenty times as abundant as in the purer atmosphere of Itoundhay, some three miles due north-east of the town. . _</p>
        <p>The prevailing winds are west and south-west and we should naturally expect therefore that the general drift of these impurities will be more towards the east than towards the west. This is specially evident in the case of the stations at Headingley and Kirkstall bath are roughly the same distance from the centre of the town, both lie to the north-west, both over- look the industrial Kirkstall section of the Aire valley; Headingley from the east, however, and Kirkstall from the west, with the result that the solid impurities at Headingley amount to 660 lbs. per acre as against 3501bs per acre at Kirkstall in a coriesponding position on the opposite side of the valley. By far the most deleterious and objectionable of all the suspended impurities is the tarry matter. At all stations an appreciable amount of tar was found to be deposited, the amount being greatest in and near the industrial area and diminishing rapidly towards the north as can be seen from the following table : Amount of tar deposited per annum 150 lbs. per acre 80 „ 32 „ t, 25 „ 14 „</p>
        <p>Station. Industrial area Centre of town 1 mile to North 2 miles to North 3 miles to North-east</p>
        <p>It is interesting,however, to note that the suspended matters in the suburban areas, though smaller in amount, are much richer in tar than those of the industrial area ; thus while in the suburban stations they formed. on an average 17 per cent. of the total suspended matter, the proportion in the case of the industrial stations worked out at only 5 per cent. Tere is thus a characteristic difference between the contamination arising from the factory shaft and that attributable to the very imperfect combustion of the domestic range. For the last year, with the assistance of a Govern- ment grant from the Development Fund, the investiga- tions have been continued over a wider area by the Agricultural Department of the University of Leeds. Although these results are not yet ready for publica- tion, it may, perhaps be pointed out that they go to prove conclusively that the smoke pollution is far worse to the south than to the north of the city. Thus, if we take a ring three miles from the centre of Leeds, we find the annual deposits to be :- 3 miles S.E of Leeds 230 lbs. per acre 3 „ South of Leeds ... 190 „ 19 3 ~ S.W. of Leeds ... 184 ~ 3 N.E. of Leeds ... ... 81 ~ f 3 , North of Leeds ... ... 90 The annual deposit is again found to diminish as we work further north from the industrial area : Station. Annual Deposit. Industrial Leeds „, ... 1,900 lbs. per acre 850 „ PO 900 „ If</p>
        <p>Centre of Leeds 1 mile to North</p>
        <p>2 Miles to North</p>
        <p>150 108 „ 62 „ JO 46 „ 9/ Perhaps one of the most interesting points brought out was the effect of the coal strike in March last in diminishing this pollution and clearing the air, while the improvements was found to be most marked in the industiial areas, and least walked in the rural areas. Measuring the smoke polla tion by the average amount of solid impurities in the rain (parts per 100,000) in each month, we get : _</p>
        <p>5 II If 7</p>
        <p>Feb. Afar:Apr. May. ?:F; 194 16 4l Industrial areas. sludden drop during the month olPnfatrheteasclishetrreialk:e:,'°alGvn:d.g.a.elmta:(!st, marked recovery to its normal level* when the strike was over. This will fully explain the remarkable clearness and brightness of the atmosphere during the coal strike, reference to which was so frequently made in the papers, by astronomers particularly. These solid impurities, present in the air of towns and due to pollution by coal smoke, play a very important part in detrimentally affecting the growth of plants. Their effect in diminishing the amount of sunlight in our industrial towns may be gathered from the fact that in 1907, the number of home of bright sunshine registered in the centre of Leeds was 1,167 as compared with 1,402 at Adel, some four miles to the north, in other words, the smoke-cloud banging over the centre of the town curtailed the duration of bright sunshine by fully 17 per cent. If, however, we measure, not the number of hours of bright sunshine, but the actual intensity of the light, we find not only a greater curtailment, but a sharply defined correla- tion between that intensity and the known solid impurities in the air, as the following table will show :</p>
        <p>Solid Impurities Intensity of reaching the Ground Light Arbitrary Yearly. Scale. Hunslet 1,565 lb. per acre 32 Beeston Hill Phflosophictil Hall 819 „ „ 40 Headingley Observatory 399 72 If 47 Westwood-lane</p>
        <p>INFLUENCE OF Suspended 11.4 a tterm tyo f Liht '</p>
        <p>' BEESfON "ThOsoPHICAL hAIL 'NGLEV</p>
        <p>In Hunslet, therefore, the centre of one of our chief industrial areas, fully 40 per cent. of the light is thus shut off. The energy of sunlight is required by the green leaf for the conversion of carbon dioxide into carbohydrates, and when 40 per cent. of that energy is cut off by the smoke cloud, the effective growth of the plant must be very effectively checked. Again, the greater part of the material of which the leaves of plants i 3 composed is taken from the atmosphere. The leaves of plants possess minute pores, or stomata, by means of which they absorb carbon dioxide from the air, this carbon di-oxide being converted in the plants into starches, sugars or other carbohydrates. Anything, therefore, which will tend to hinder this intake and assimilation of carbon di-oxide will effectively binder the growth of the plant. Now soot, as has already ;been pointed out, is not pure carbon, but contains varying amounts, occasionally as much as forty per cent. of a thick oil or tar. This adhesive tarry matter present will cause it to adhere tenaciously to anything with which it may come into contact, so that it cannot easily be removed by the rain. (To be continue?)</p>
        <p>3.. 2 4 PH. 7 OBSER‘.‘ I ORv WIiFIWOOD LAM, it . lifj 1 7 • !, .1 1, I :; b</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>YOUNG MAN'S SAD END.</head>
        <p>YOUNG MAN'S SAD END.</p>
        <p>A verdict that" Death was due to heart failure, caused by excessive drinking" was returned at an inquest conducted by Mr F' A JOllO3, deputy coroner, at War- rington, on Monday, on the body of Walter Campbell, aged 31 years, of Chorlton-street.—The mother of the deceased said her son served in the 21st Lancers, and on leaving the service three years ago with an lexemplary character, he went to work in Altrincham. In June last he had about £2OO in the bank which had been left to him by his father. He had always been fond of a drink, and since ceasing work last June he had been drinking heavily and had often been ill from the effects. On Friday lie returned home very drunk, about 530 and complained of feeling ill, and she persuaded him to go to bed. He appeared strange in his manner. He went worse and died the following morning. In reply to the deputy-coroner witness said she did not think all the money had gone. She did not know any particular house where he got his drink. Dr Robinson said (3 inapbell probably died from the effects of drinking whilst suffering from a weak heart</p>
        <p>DON'T Loo x OLD—Keep the ravages of time at bay by darkening the grey streaks with LOCKYER'S SULPHUR HAIR RESTORER,wh!ch deepensto a natural colour ins few days.Lockyer's maintains a youthful anpearance.Large bots everywhere. Post freel/6.l3edford Laboratory, Lotalcr. S.E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>WOMEN'S GHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S GHAT.</p>
        <p>The Premier Peer of Ireland.—The late Duke of Abercorn was the head oi is very large family and his death has placed a remarkable number of people in mourning. Born in 1838, he was in his 75th year, and succeeded his father, as the second holder of the duke- dom, in 1895. Although the premier peer of Ireland, he was of purely Scottish origin, being head of the male line of the great Scottish house of Hamilton, and his title was taken from a village in Linlithgow. Few families have been more honoured with Court favours than the Hamiltons, and the late Duke's titles numbered not far short of a score. The Duke of Atholl and the Duke of Buecleuch, could no doubt each muster a larger number of titles, but the Duke of Abercorn was one of three noblemen, the others being the Marquis of Lansdowne and the Earl of Perularn, to hold separate peerages in England, Scotland and Ireland. An intimate friend of the late King Edward, the Duke had held appointments in the Royal Household, and had enter- tained members of the Royal Family on several occasions at Barons Court, his Irish seat in County Tyrone, not far from Newtown Stewart. Coronets and Business.—The late Duke of Abercorn, like his brothers Lord Claud, Lord George, Lord Ernest and Lord Frederick Hamilton, was distinguished for his business capabilities, qualities which the widowed Duchess possesses in no small measure. She is the youngest daughter of the first Earl Howe, and married the late Duke in 1869. The Duchess is one ( f the most domesticated members of society, and has always been I exceedingly fond of her Irish home, where her great hobby has been the superintendence of a model dairy. It is a thoroughly practical concern, and is run on strict business lines., the Duchess supplying several large Belfast firms with butter and cream. It is not by any means the only dairy under coronetted management and not very long ago Lord Tennyson got into trouble as a milk-seller. At one time his name appeared on the carts which delivered milk to customers. Less dis- tinguished dairymen protested that this gave a peer an ' undue advantage in the market and to meet their wishes the name was removed. But the law requires that every cart must bear its owner's name, and in try- ing to avoid offending his neighbours, Lord Tennyson had some little trouble wite the authorities. Titlis and Telent.—The drama and amateur theatricals have many rotaries in society among the latest being Lady Kathleen Hastings, who has appeared this week at Nuneaton in a public performance of a ' play, of which she is author. Lady Kathleen is the 'youngest daughter of Lord and Lady Huntingdon, not 1 yet twenty, and it seems likely that she will become I distinguished not only rs an amateur performsr, but also as a playwright. Playwriting as a hobby is not perhaps a very serious business, but it wants brains, and among the rich and the well-to-do, the higher education of girls of the present day, lif.is. brought with it, a desire to ,exeraise their capabilities. To be good- looking is always an advantage, but it is not quite so important a matter as in Victorian times, when in wealthy and middle-class families, a girl's life had but one object in view, and was usually a vacuous affair. The girl who is clever, capable and business-like will always push aside her merely pre.tty. companion, and should the necessity arise, she is infinitely better equipped with resources to face the realities of life. Women Doctors.—About three and a half million women come under the provision of the Insurance Act, but it would be safe to say that if they had their choice, in the matter, the vast majority would prefer the attendance of a male to a female doctor. Thera are now about one thousand woman practitioners in this country, about one to every forty ME dical men,but it is a well ascertained fact that women as a rule, are antagonistic to doctors of their own sex. :They are apparently less ready to believe in the capability of a woman than a man and especially is this the case whni the woman doctor is some years younger than her patient. It is many s ears since women first entered the profession, but, although they are gradually making headway against the prejudice, they hove not yet succeeled in uprooting the tradition that doctoring is the immemorial privilege of men, The Year's Fashions.—With the departure of 1912, a review of the fsahions that prevailed during its course, may not be out of place. The most noteworthy feature was perhaps the revival of the bobble skirt, which was launched the previous autumn and then dis- carded. The severe type of narrow skirt followed the revival as a natural consequence. Then came rumours of the crinoline, and some enterprising firms showed models of distended skirts side by side with the hobble. But except on the stage the crinoline was tabooed. The lamentations of manufacturers who found the tight skirts most unprofitable, induced the great modistes to devise other means of putting more material into their gowns, and pullers were introduced. It was not possible to puff them cut, for the silhouette had taken a firm hold on feminine taste, and the rimier degenerated into a mere veiling, taking new curves from wrist to knee. But it did not esdisappear, it took instead innumerable modifications, and sheath-like ball gowns swathed in draperies, made a change that found a warm welcome, for they accorded well with the fish-like tail train ef the early pit of the season. Day-light Wear Sets the Fashions.—].t is not how- ever, ballroom modes and styles, that lead the fashions, but rather what is worn duringithe day ; and the tailors have more influence than the Court dressmakers in deciding what styles shall prevail, and what shell be relegated to oblivion. The best tailors made their suits to look Hie one continuous piece and only towards the end of the summer, did they introduce the slightest of draperiere The fashions during the past few months show a tendency to go back a couple of generations, when the skimpy and slender silhouette, with short waist and sandalled shoes, were the vogue. America is said to have pronounced in favour of narrow backs and fronts, and curveless hipless styles, for the coming year—which begins with the reign of the straight line and moderately high waist. Long Ccats for the Tall.—There is great diversity of opinion with regard to the suitability of the long coat to short figures, and many women whose stature scarcely exceeds fivefeet wear it because they are under the impression that by so doing they add to their height. This is undoubtedly a mistake. Tall figures look by far the best in these becoming and useful garments, which have a tendency to dwarf a short woman, especially if she be given to embonpoint. The same may be said of draperies. The stout and the short should avoid them, but tall women may wear them with advantage, as may also the quite elderly, if they have retained a slim figure. Needless to say, draperies of every kind rqeuire the manipulation of an artist in their successful arrangement. The Little Waistcoat.—The short .pieces of rich fabrics available at the sales, are most suitable for the little waistcoats that tailors are adopting from Paris and Berlin Rouses. They are made of every kind of decorative m +trials the richer the better, but they must not match the facings of the suits with which they are worn. In some c tses, striped silks and moirdl are used with very good effect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>BANK REPORTS.</head>
        <p>BANK REPORTS.</p>
        <p>UNION BANK OF MANCHESTER, LIMITED. The nett profits of the Union Bank of Manchester, Limited, for the half-year ended December 31st, 1912, including £10,362 brought forward, amount to £62,045. The Directors have declared a dividend of 124 per cent less income tax, absorbing £32,370, and have made the usual appropriations of £2,500 to premises reduction account, £2,000 to Pension Fend, and £5,000 to Reserve Fund raising that fund to £340,000, and leaving the amount to be carried forward £20,175, as against £8,020 this time last year. THE LONDON CITY AND MIDLAND BANK</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LIMITED.</head>
        <p>LIMITED.</p>
        <p>The Directors of the London City and Midland Bank Limited report that the profits for the year ending 31st December last are £1,009,918, which, with the balance brought forward of £122,992, amount to £1,132,910,and they recommend a dividend of £1 2s 6d per share, pay- able on the Ist February, making, with the interim dividend paid in July last, 10 per cent. per annum, amounting to £718,062 appropriating £41,986 to pay income tax for shareholders, £160,000 to write down investments to below market value, £50,000 to building Redemption Fund, £20,000 to Officers' Pension Fund, £lO,OOO to Staff Widows' Fund, and carrying for ward £132,992. The dividend for the year 1911 was at the same rate, with £lO,OOO to Building Redemption Fund, and £lO,OOO to Officers' Pension Fund, and £122,192 carried forward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>MANCHESTER AND LIVERPOOL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>THE MANCHESTER AND LIVERPOOL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>BANKING COMPANY LIMITED.</p>
        <p>The Directors report that the gross profit for the half year to December 31st., after writing down invest- ments to market value and making provision for bad and doubtful debts, was £309,274. The expenses amounted to £117,031, leaving with £36,430 brought forward, a net sum available of £228,730. The directors recommend the usual dividend (free of Income tax) at the rate of 17.1 per cent, per annum ; £lO,OOO is added to Reserve Fund (which will now stand at £1,680,000) and £lO,OOO to the Pension Fund, £42,830 is carried forward, an increase of £11,119 upon the balance carried forward a year ago. At the corresponding period last year, a gross profit of £304,384 was reported; the expenses amounted to £113,245, £10,0,0 was transferred to the Reserve Fund, and £5,000 was added to the Pension Fund. The present balance sheet shows : Current deposit and other accounts £25,070,071 ; acceptances, £2,950,356 ; cash in band and Bank of England balance, £3,114,668; money at call and short notice, £3,874,068 ; investments, £3,616,318 ; Bills of Exchange, £1 500,615 ; advances in current accounts &amp;c., £13,220,500 ; Bank Property, £500,000. The total assets amount to £31,806,557,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>USEFUL BOOK FREE.</head>
        <p>A USEFUL BOOK FREE.</p>
        <p>A book almanack which should be in every home has just been published. It can be obtained free from any chemist, and it is really worth possessing, for it is ' crowded from cover to cover with useful information, valuable advice on health, entertaining articles and clever pictures. Every copy also carries with it an insurance for .£250. It is called "Holloway's Almanac and Family Friend for 1913," and it is issued by the proprietors of the litmus remedies, "Holloway's Pills and Ointment." You can obtain a copy free from ,your chemists, or by sending a postcard to Thomas Holloway, 113, Southwark-street, S.E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
