      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>..._ . , _ ' 4,...- ._...... , , ..,_, t ''''' 'llk' IST 7 ~ , I , _.. . t•N • , i ,Ll'.. ' ' • ' 3 „,,,...-. , ~ ICT , , • __ _. ,.</head>
        <p>..._ . , _ ' 4,...- ._...... , , ..,_, t ''''' 'llk' IST 7 ~ , I , _.. . t•N • , i ,Ll'.. ' ' • ' 3 „,,,...-. , ~ ICT , , • __ _. ,.</p>
        <p>Miss E Hardacre, " Moon" and " Ypsilante "; Mr Tom Laing, "Everybody's doing it" and "Ragtime violin." The large number of handsome prizes for the elder scholars were presented by Miss Hardwick. BRITISH WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE ASSOCIATIoN.—The recently formed Lymm Branch of the National British Women's Temperance Association held their first public meeting in the Primitive Methodist schoolroom on Tuesday night. That the Branch was very fortunate in securing Mrs Johnson-Wright as the speaker was shown by the excellent attendance. Mrs Johnson-Wright is a native of Lymm but has been a resident in New Zealand for many years where she has taken a prominent part in the struegle against the liquor traffic. The Rector' the Rev G B Thurston, M.A.. was the chairman and in his address stated his regret at the absence of the Branch President, Mrs G L Welford, who had to respond to an urgent message calling her away from home. He said he was pleased to publicly identify himself with so splendid a cause Speaking from his sixteen years experience in Lymm he said that on the whole they had not a great deal to complain of until recent times, when the improved trade condi- tions and more plentiful supply of money bad tended to put temptations into the way of the working classes, more especially affecting the women and for this reason he commended the work of the British Women's Temperance Association. He also drew attention to the pledges which could be signed at the end of the meeting. (Applause).—At this state of the meeting a sone was effectively rendered by Miss E Goodwin, of Birkenhead. tlrs Johnson-Wright speak- ing of the origin of the movement, told of the intense misery drink had inflicted on the women and children of America, and how that Frances Willard ("America's uncrowned Queen ") vas so touched by the suffering around her that she and several other women banded themselves together in order to try and drive the evil out of the land. The outcome of this American Crusade was the Woman's Christian Temperance Union, of which the British Women's Temperance Association is a pert. A description of how the movement came to New Zealand was next given. One of the results was that petitions were sent to the Government asking that a jaw might be passed to prevent women from becom- ing bar-maids. For a long time their efforts were of no avail but ultimately a law necessitating the registration of bar-maids was passed which means that in a few years hence women will not be allowed to serve behind the bar. Some amusement was caused , by the speaker's reference to the woman s franchise which existed in New Zealand. The audience were told how this right to vote was achieved, and that it was largely due to the Woman's Christian Temperance Union. Reference was made to the prominent part which women had taken in convert- ing to the electorate to the prohibitionist view. The sectional victories achieved FO encouraged the mem- bers that they turned their efforts to an attempt to gain Natiocal Prohibition. An Act authorizing such an opportunity to vote was passed hut in larder to make the efforts of the Temperance Party as difficult as possible the Liquor Party had it so arranged that in order to obtain national prohibition a majority of three-fifths must he trained. The result at the following election was 55?2- out of every 100 votes, another 4-i out of every 100 votes would have secured the much desired National Prohibition. Mrs. Johnson Wright said that victory would be theirs at the next election and they would then be able to rejoice in having a country free from the drink curse. The Rector spoke of the pleasure he had derived from Mrs Johnson-Wright's address and incidentally remarked that Norway was a country of prohibition, testifying from personal experience, to its beneficial effects upon the people of that country. A vote of thanks was proposed by Mr G L Welford, seconded by the Secretary, Mrs Frank Brown, and carried unani- mously. MR. KEBTY-FLETCHER, M.P., ON "SENSATION MONGERS." Mr J It Kebty-Fletcher,M.P. for Altrincham Division writing in the" Standard," says he is at one with those who urge the necessity of finding "what I may call a pacificatory bridge hetween the two sections of the Unionist party—a via media which shall ensure unity and peace in our ranks. . . . I hold that instead of encouraging unscrupulous sensation mongers we should let the whole subject drop for the moment. If we do anything it should only be to pass resolutions of con- fidence in cur leader. I have no coLfidence or belief in the Referendum. It is all very well to be able to place before the people Home R ule,Welsh Disestablish- ment,and Tariff Reform, but would the people who now clamour for the Referendum be willing to place before the democratic constituencies of the country the pro- posal that all the Imperial taxes should be paid by the citizens in receipt ot over £2OO a year? I have lived in a country where the Referendum is in force, and I can confidently say that it would not be in the interest of this country to have it introduced here. The Referendum controversy was put forward at the last 'noir ent in the last election, and I believe it did us an iacalculable amount of harm. I should regret to see an alteration in the traditional policy of our party brought up on the eve of another great contest."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>LY3/31.</head>
        <p>LY3/31.</p>
        <p>SCHOLARS' PARTY.—The scholars of the Congrega- aional Sunday School celebrated their annual Christmas Party on Thursday and Friday evening. THE WESLEYANS.—The local Wesleyans are pleased to have Mr Harry Perrin at work amongst them once more,'after his long illness. He occupied the pulpit on Sunday last and gave earnest New Year addresses.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL DANCE.—We beg to draw our readers atten- tion to the advertisment which appears on our front Page relating to the annual Tea, Entertainment, and Dance which the Lymm Liberal Association are to hold in the Drill Hall on Wednesday next, January 15th. Extensive arrangements are well in band and a good time is promihed to all who attend, Owing to an unfortunate printing error the prsters state that the price of the tickets is nine pence each, this is not so, the prices are the same as in past 3.cars, namely one shilling each if bought before January 15th, but one Lhilling and six pence each if bought on the day. . .</p>
        <p>LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.—Tbe Lymm Liberal Associa- tion held their annual meeting for the purpose of electing representatives to the Central Council (Altrinehain), in Hurst's Pavilion on Tuesday night. The Chairman, Mr J D B Smith presided over a fair attendance,and in his opening remarks said that thougu they had but little business to transact, that little, was of vital importance to the welfare of the party, as in rase they had to prepare for a general election within the next two years it was adviaable to appoint a strcng ward committee. The followihg gentlemen were elected :—A Appleton, R Barker, C J Barnes J H Brazendale, J Brearley, H Brookes, A Clare, E Clare, D Daniels, H Drinkwater' R H Gotobed, W Gould, G Holt, jun., G Hurst, L James, W Mclntyre, J H Mather, A Mercer, F A Morris,W Moss, jun.,C Moss' J Oakes, R H S Pawlyn, R Percival, J Radcliff,W Seymour, J D B Smith, A Thomas, J Tilzey, .1 Water- worth, J Webb, jun., E Wright, and T Wyatt.</p>
        <p>SCHOLARS' CONCERT.—On Wednesday night the last of a series of concerts in aidlof the Weslean Sunday School Funds was held in the sehoolroom when there</p>
        <p>was a good attendance of parents and friends to witness the programme provided by the teachers and scholars. The first part of the programme was given by the scholars of the primary school whose efforts reflected great credit upon Miss F Rogerson, and Miss E Winstanley, their teachers. The items were "Welcome song ": a children's play called " Jessie's dream "; a song "Under the old umbrella "; and a recitation "Teddy and I" by Kathleen Hudson. The second portion of the programme was opened by Miss M Winstanley who gave a good interpretation of the song "The Better Land." Miss Moston came next with the recitation " Dora " (Tennyson). A number of _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>the morning scholars,A Daniels,B I:eigh A Lewie,Bessie Lord, Ida Lord, G Ogden, E Soanes, D Winstanley, and May Winstenley, contributed the action song" Grand- mother's Dream." This followed by the humorous song "Dear little jemmy face," sung by Mrs Daher. The concluding item was a well performed dialogue "Toe Jumble Sale "contributed by Mrs Daher, the Misses Florence Gitsharn, Cl. ra Winfield, and May Winstanley. Mr H S Brown presided over the concert snd the duties of accompanists were executed by Mrs F Brown, the Misses F Rogerson, and M Winstanley. WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.—The Lymm and District Women's Liberal Association held a public meeting in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom on Wednesday night. The speaker was Miss Robinson of the Free Trade Union,who gave a very clear address on the subject of Free Trade. Unfortunately the attendance was not all that could be desired, but this was no doubt due to the fact of three other meetings being held in the village on the sa-ne evening. The chair was taken by the President, Mrs Dodds of Penketh, who, in a brief eddress said that Free Trade was a vital question as it concerned the food supply and was therefore matter of great interest to the housewife. " Protection," she said "will mean a shortage of 'spare money' and therefore the mothers will he unable to provide for their children all the good and necessary things they require." Miss Robinson prefaced her address by touching upon the recent speeches that have been made by Lord Lansdowne and Mr Boner Law, and said that she felt quite insulted by that of the latter gentleman. "Our policy is not only against having fresh taxes, but also to remove even the Present ones." She denoted her pleasure at the excellent trade conditions as shown by the trade returns published in that morning's paper, and said that it was far better to test by experience than to trust to the prophetic utterances of the Tariff Reforme rs She made comparisons between the present Free Trade, and Tariff Reform methods of raising State Revenues and said that she was opposed to Tariff Reform because it meant increased cost of living, placing heavy burdens Si those least able to bear them, and putting more money into the pockets of the manufacturer than into the State Revenue.—Mrs:Dodds said they ought to be proud of being British women and expressed her appreciation of Miss Robinson's lucid method of explaining Free Tiede. She also gave several valvahle and personal illustrations of the ill effects of Protection. The meeting was enhanced by a contralto solo sung by Miss E Goodwin, of Birkenhead. THE BOY SCOUTS.—SbouId there be any of our readers who have any doubts as to the merits of the Scout Movement surely the following account will COBViDC9 them of their mistaken ideas :—Scoutmaster Nash and his scout hand bell ringers have again been working hard during the last few weeks, and they could have been seen ouc nearly every night visiting the good friends of the scouts at their homes, with the bells. They have been received everywhere with delight and Pleasure and their ringing has been greatly praised. Their object this year was to raise a fund to give New Year cheer for cripples and chronic invalids, and these efforts have met with great success, as the amount collected up-to-date is about £l4. Mrs T Ridgway, with the assistance of Nurse Nash, very kindly and willingly undertook the dnty of selecting the names, and the articles, which undoubtedly was a very big task and each parcel bore a card with the name and good wishes from the Scouts. All were made up under the personal superintendance of Mrs Ridgway. Although Saturday last turned out a wretched afternoon, the boys in charge of Scoutmaster Nash, met at Mrs Ridgways house and with the aid of two motor cars toured the district, distributing the parcels. The happiness that Mr Nash and the boys gave to our suffering and afflicted friends was very much appreciated, as could be seen by the delight of each recipient on such an Unexpected parcel being handed to them. Last year the fund was devoted to a tea and entertainment to the poor of the children of the district. Mr Nash wishes to take this opportunity of thanking all the csany kind friends who have so readily and willingly given to the fund again this year. CHILDREN'S TREAT AT THE DRILL HALL.—Had any stranger to Lymm passed the Drill Hall on Wednesday evening and seen the laaghing crowds of children that were about he might readily have concluded that the Pied Piper of Hamelin, or Santa Claus, was in the neighbourhood. This however was not the case, the Youthful ebullitions being due to the Rector entertain- ing the 300 children of the Pepper-stree4, Statham, and Nevvfield Sunday School 3 on the occasion of their annual prize giving, tea and entertainment. At 5.30 Pan. the infants had their tea after which they Participated in the joys of a Christmas Tree which was growing " in the recreation room, Miss D Norris gracefully making the presentation of the prizes. At 6.30 p.m. the senior scholars displayed their abilities at the tea tables, the preparation of va hich had been in the capable hands of Mesdames Bell, Smith, Watson, and the Misses Brazendale and Dalton. Tea over, parents Were admitted, and they, together with the scholars, Were entertained by the Lymm and Statham Choirs who saag several carols. The rest of the entertainment was divided into two parts, when Mrs Watkin's house party kindly took charge of affairs. Their first item was a play Betay Baker" in which the various characters were well sustained by Misses H Watkin, end Violet Johnson, llesers Alan Tudor and Edmund Hartley. The second Portion of their programme took the form of a Pierrots derformance, when the following items merited tie hearty applause given :—Songs by Miss Watkin, When I walked out with a soldier" and " Mandy" ; "1„,18a H Watkin, "Teddy Bear" and " Babyland " ; "iiss V Johnson, "Teeny, tiny,touch" and "Yorkshire";</p>
