      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL MEETING. The quarterly meeting of the Grappenball Parish Council was held on Wednesday evening in tbe Council schools. Mr A Lister-Kaye presided and there were present :—Messrs I Fox, J Bradford, J Carter, F Ellison, and F L Garside. THE FOOTPATHS. Mr Garside stated that some of the public footpaths in the parish were in an almost impassable condition. He referred chiefly to the one going through Eaton fields. The styles also were very difficult to negotiate, especially for women. Mr Garside would like to know what powers they had as a council in the question of Clerk ti ye ap tl ihead that . the asa u c acsoe on council lo open can ef no Thecolt: isaectd footpaths. oa no ootnplayt hr es Pthe r farmer has a perfect right to plough them up. We can only claim a right of way. The Chairman suggested that the Clerk write to the land agent (Mr Mclntyre) and ask for the styles to be put in order across Eaton's field. He felt sure this wculd be done, because when requested to do so in the past Mr Mclntyre had always very kindly done so. This was agreed to. Mr Fox also suggested that a letter be sent to the District Council requesting them to erect a finger-post signs to show exactly where the footpaths were. Mr Carter seconded and it vies carried. DANGEROUS DITCH. Mr Fox stated that if all those who had built property had complied more strictly with the Public Health Act we should not be faced with a bilge sewer- age scheme. And inasmuch as this scheme would not tboe b e done completed f of or r tthwoo eo nrctlhorsei tie g yoefa rts h, e something dangerous odui gtchht behind Mavfield-road. He (Mr Fox) therefore moved that the District Council be requested to enclose this ditch, and also those ditches as were considered a public danger. Mr Carter seconded and it was carried. TRIF.NNIAL ELECTION. It was decided to hold the triennial election of the new Council on Monday, March 17th.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 11, 1913</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>- DEATH OF MR. PETER HIGHAM.—We regret to record the death of a well-known inhabitant of the village in the person of Mr Peter Higham, who passed away at the age of 67 on Wednesday evening. For the past 35 years the deceased gentleman had been in the employ of Messrs Bennett Bros., of Bruche Farm, having had charge of the cattle on their Tidal-lane Farm. He was one of the non-too-common, old-fashioned, faithful type of employee, and his death under peculiarly sad conditions is the cause of great regret to his employers and a large circle of friends. About eight weeks ago while attending to his duties he was knocked down and trampled upon by one of the cows under his care. As soon as he was discovered he was taken to his home and attended by Drs Bennett, Sutherland and Fox, but dispite all their attention, he died as stated above. He leaves a widow and nine children. The funeral service will take place in the Wesleyan Church at 2.30 o'clock on Sunday afternoon, followed by the interment in the village churchyard. TEA MEETING AND ENTERTAINMENT.—The annual tea meeting and entertainment in connection with the Wesleyan Church was hold on Thursday evening. A ' good number sat down to tea served by the ladies connected with the school and church. The Rev E Morgan presided over the entertainment which followed and in a very brief speech commended the work of the Sunday School. Songs by the Misses Nicholson, Duckworth and Brough were much appreciated and recitations by Miss Ilenshall including that popular one "The one legg'd goose" were greatly enjoyed. The report of the Sunday School Council of the years work was presented by Mr A Ward (sscretary). At present there are 290 scholars (150 males and 140 females) upon the books exclusive of the cradle roll of 31. There are 33 teachers (16 males and 17 females). This is exclusive of helpers in the primary department, which flourishes under the careful and enthusiastic superintendence of the Misses Lilian Wright and Ethel Mason. Reference was made to the great success of the Mayflower Bazaar held in May and to the good work accomplished by Mr Proffitt and other workers in providing high class entertain- ments during the winter months ; the object of both these efforts being to reduce the debt on the new school pi emises. During the year the sum of £93 had been paid into the savings club by 40 contribu tors and that amount plus intereq, had been recently repaid to them. The officers elected for the present, year are :—Superintendents, Messrs F Wright and 11 E Roscollor ; secretaries, Messrs A Ward and L Bennett; treasurer, Mr J Gaudy; registration secretaries, Messrs George Brough and R Lunt; club secretary and librarian, Mr H Brough. Songs by Mr Hannan and Mr Warburton were well received The Rev J Gunnell gave some interesting reminiscences of Sunday school work and methods of between 60 and 70 years ago. Mr H M Ashton was responsible for the humorous element and we need scarcely add that that portion of the programme was provocative of enthusi- astic, appreciation. The accompaniments were sympa- thetically played by Miss Weaver.</p>
      </div>
