      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>.jaihna11."111"...."""1".-.-__</head>
        <p>.jaihna11."111"...."""1".-.-__</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>BIBLE CLASS PRIZE DISTRIBULIoN.—The annual prize distribution in connection witn St. Thomas's Men's Class, 13, took place on Sunday afternoon. The Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph) ho had very kindly consented to distribute the prizes,was welcomed by the Vicar (Rev Collins) and given a hearty reception by the members. His Worship said how pleased he was to come amongst such a body of men as those present who from what he knew, were not only trying to lead such 'lives as were beneficial to themselves and their families, but what was even better and more Christlike, they were trying to brighten and cheer those around them. He wished them every success in their good work. (Applause.) He then distributed the prizes with a kindly remark to each recipient. On the motion of Mr Stannard and Mr Morris, supported by Mr Hatton, a hearty vote of thanks was passed to the Mayor for so kindly coming amongst them. The Mas or, in responding, said he thould be pleased to assist tharn at any time. (Applause.) The hymn " Fight the good fight " brought a bright meeting to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee was held in the Hall Cafe on '1 tresday evening, Mr G Pilling presiding. There were also present :—Messrs F Pearson, W Mclntyre, J Whitley, F Warburton, C Hallows, J Hallows, .1 Wood, El J Westbrook, F Rose, G F Ashton (clerk), J Farrington inilwctor) and S L Vright (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>FLOODED ROAD.</head>
        <p>A FLOODED ROAD.</p>
        <p>The fearful state of the road at the Swing Bridge was referred to by Mr J Hallows, who said that pedestrians could not get over it without going through pools of water. All the road was fl,oded over It was a scandalous affair and something ought to be done vith</p>
        <p>it. The Clerk pointed out that the County Council were responsible for it beciuse it was their road On the motion of Mr Hallows, it was decided that the County Council be written to on the matter. The Chairman said the state of the road was dis graceful without doubt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>PARISH COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Afterwards a meeting of the Parish Council was held Mr J Whitley presidir,g. Mr Mclntre proposed that the Warrington Corpora- tion be written to aski;ig them to repair the road between the tram lines from the Swing Bridg Victoria-road. It was extremely dangerous for cyclists and other vehicles. This was seconded and carried</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH GARDEN SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH GARDEN SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A RECORD YEAR.</p>
        <p>The remarkable progress which the Stockton Heath Garden society has made during the past year was undi r review at the annual meeting hela in the Hall Cafe on Tuesday evening. There was a large attendance. Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., presided, and at the commence- ment of the meeting remarked that they had cause to cmgratulate themselves on a most successful year. Not only had they been able to wipe out a deficit of £ll, hut they bad made a profit on the year's working of £5O, leaving a credit balance of £lB 15s 2d. That was exceedingly good when they took into consideration the fact that they lost nearly La on the October show (Hear, hear.) He had a great deal of doubt in his own mind whether they were wise in continuing the October show. If it had not been for the concert promoted by Mrs C Garton Stove, which brought them in £6 13s 11d, they would have lost over £lor What they as a society must strive to do was to furt he improve their August show. lany of the exhibits were of the highest quality, but the competition in many of the classes was not so keen as a show of that standing ought to command. They must not allow the October show to go on without due regard to the upkeep, for otherwise it would cripple their endeavour to make the summer show—which was the climax of the summer's work—a greater success than ever before. The large increase in membership nad been the most encouraging feature in the year's work, and that proved that the society was doing a good work, and that the people of the district appteciated it. The society was deeply indebted to its honorary officers for the willing help they had given, and the good work they bad accom- plished. Mr. White, their energetic secretary, was deserving of their warmest thanks for the time and thought that he so uugrudgingly gave to the society for the attainment of its objects.</p>
        <p>THE ANNUAL REPORT. The Secretary (Mr E G White) in his annual report said :—On every Land we are able to report progress, and the society may now be considered to have reached a position which shows that its existence is quite justified, and that the public are beginning to appre- ciate the work which it has been steadily doing during the last eight years. (Applause.) If members would regularly hear in mind the objects of the society it is quite easy to see that Stockton Heath would become much more attractive as a residential suburb than it is at present. (Hear, hear). The growth of the society from its humble beginning to its present considerable organization is ample proof of the general acceptance of its principles, but no one can be in doubt for a single moment that there is yet a great deal of work to he done on educational lines before the committee can be satisfied with their efforts. The shows are held not as the real objects of the society, but as the means to that object—ahat we are really trying to instil into the minds of all the love of living plants and flowers, to guide each one to care for them, and to cultivate them, and it is only when we keep this continually in mind that any casual walk through the village will show how much there is yet to do, and how little really we seem so far to have accomplished. Many of the front gardens in the main thoroughfares are still uncared for, or practically SO ; weeds grow apace still in villa paths, and ro :gh overgrown hedges still usurp the place of flowers. You may see the cottager at his door smoking his pipe,whilst he gazes,apparently contentedly on his little plot, filled with uncared-for, sickly-look- ing plants and smothering weeds. This should not be. A little effort, regularly made, will turn the unkempt garden plot into a place of continual interest and delight, and it must be ours, as a society, to foster this love of the garden and of garden work, and not, to let our efforts he devoted solely to the competitions of the show ground. (Applause.) The summer show was this year held on the Bank Holiday in August. The choice of day was very fortunate,for not only was V- e weather much better than for some weeks before, but the holiday itself seemed to have set -more people than usual at liberty and, fortunately for us, they decided to patronize our show. It is estimated that between 3,CCO and 4,000 people were present during the afternoon and evening,and favourable reports of the exhibits were heard on every hand. Considering the unsatisfactory weather of the summer, there is no doubt that the exhibits were very good, but there is yet room for much improvement. We want to see the classes better filled and more competition all round. To make this possible the schedule has again been given careful attention. Several additions have been made, with considerable increase in the prize-money, and it is now calculated to appeal to all members of the society. Therefore, let members decide early in the year to compete, make ample provision of seeds and plants early in the season, and then "come up smiling' on the show day and reap their rich reward. During the latter portion of the year the committee were approached to take over the canal banks portion of the Tree-planting Association, as that body felt that they had concluded their labours on the trees being banned over to the councils. After earnest consideration it was decided to take this work over, and this has been done and the association has now been disbanded and handed over to your society. The Chairman proposed the adoption of the report and Mr Ellison having seconded, it was adopted unanimorly by the meeting. THE ACCOUNTS. Mr D E Watson (treasurer) submitted the accorints, comparing this years figures with those of last year. For many years they had not been able to show a credit balance at all, in fact this was the first year that they could do so. (Applause.) The total receipts of the August show came to £52 as against £27 19. last year, the gate money being £36 as against £lB. On the expenditure side the prize money totalled £36 15s. Gri. as against £32 55., the total expenditure was £79 9s. 4d. In the general account the subsoiptions totalled £72 16s. 6d. The total receipts were £lO9 3s. as against £O6 1 la. last year. They now bad a balance in hand of £3B 15s. 2d. (Applause.) They bad also plant valued, at the lowest, at £l2 103. If the Committee received the support in the future that they bad done in the past there was no reason why they should not add £5O each year to their banking account. (Applause.) The accounts having been passed the election of officers was then proceeded with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr J H Fletcher secondati by Mr Bradshaw, Sir Gilbert Greensll was re-elected es president. The President briefly returned thanks. The Secretary proposed and Mr F Jenkins seconded the motion that the following gentlemen be re-elected as vice-presidents: Revs Jsmes Collins and H V Pigot, Messrs H R Armitage, T RaxtPr, J H Clarke, L G Davies, Vere 13 Davies, E Hall,W H J Jenkins, Roger C Parr, A J Willett and R I Wynne Jones. Mr D E Watson and Mr White were re-elected treasurer and secretary respectively. In returning thanks Air White eppealed to all met-niters to aid in the colleotion of subscriptions &amp;c. In response to his appeal last year one member obtained no fewer than 40 members. He urged them to strive to get the entries up to 500. The show wai now a financial success but they wanted to make it a success from the exhibitors point of view. (Hear, bear.) ; On the proposition of Mr F A Jenkins seconded by Mr Plevin the following Committee was elected : Messrs k Baker, J B Baxter, J Bell, 0 A Dunlop, T Edden. J Ellison, ft Gorton, j Morris, F A Rathbone, W Skelton and 11 W Whitehouse. The classification was left to the Schedule Committee as is usual.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>TIIIS YEAR'S SHOWS.</head>
        <p>TIIIS YEAR'S SHOWS.</p>
        <p>It was unanimously decided to hold two shows one on August 4th and the other on SApteEnber 27th. The Chairman remarked that if the October sbovr kept the members together it might be kept on, but they should not forget that they lost by it financially. A vote of thanks to Mr Brooke for his assistance as subscription secretary was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>RISLEY.</head>
        <p>RISLEY.</p>
        <p>INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHURCH, FEARNIIEAD.— An "open Sunday" wee held on Sunday in the school, Mr Joseph Mason presided, and Mr E James gave a helpful address to the scholars and teachers. Duets, solos and recitations were given by hoth the adults and juveniles..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD.—The first meeting of the year took place on Wednesday evening. It took the form of a devotional and sacramental service. The Rev H iddlewood gave an address entitled "The relationship of young people to the Church," in his very earnest and impressive manner. During the service an anthem was rendered by the choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN CHURCH.—The monthly meeting of the Devotional Section of the Wesley Guild was held on Wednesday evening in the Wesleyan Sunday school, when Mr W Foxley presided in the unavoidable absence of Mrs Dodds. The address was given by,.Mr AE</p>
        <p>Robinson, of Widnes. His subject was taken from the Guilders' Devotional booklet entitled" The song of the Huguenots." The speaker dealt with the different topics arising out of the subject-Ithe Psalms and French Protestantism, The Psalms in the experiences of notable. Frenchmen, [he Camisards and their brave struggle for freedom, and the pastors of the deserts. I-bums were sung, inis3 M Watson being the accompanist. PEN-REM LIBERAL CLUB, FIFTH ANNUAL WHIST DRIVE AND DANCE —The members and friends of the above club gathered together in good numbers on Thursday evening of last week at the St Mary's Infants Sunday School for this annual event. The proceedings began and eighteen tables were occupied by whist players. The WC's. for the evening's entertainment were Messrs H Davies and W Dwerryhouse. The prize- winners for the whist drive were as follows :—Ladies,</p>
        <p>1, MiS3 Barbara Scott ; 2, Miss Bassi+) Scott ; consola- tion, Mies Darlington. Gents : 1, Glmb ;2, J Turistall ; consolation, W Foden. The accompanist's task was very capably undertaken by Mr J Hall. Mrs Rontledge had charge of the refreshments and Mrs Dv‘errybouse gracefully presented the prizes. OLD FOLK'S TREAT.—Tbis popular annual event took place in the Wesleyan Sunday school last evening week. One hundred and forty-nine invitations had been irsued and 105 attended-6S women and 37 men. Parcels of groceries were tsent to those who were unable to be present. After en excellent tea, the usual entertain- ment was presided over by Dr Murray (president of the committee). An excellent programme included lantern pictures by Mr Parkinson, of Warrington, comic songs and concertina solos by Mr S Aukland anti songs and dano:-ts by Miss Doris Welsby. Nir A i row was an f fficient accom- panist. Before leaving, the old people were provided with supper and each old man received a present of tobacco and each old lady a packet of tea. The subscribers and helpers were cormally thanked on the proposition of Dr Murray, who mentioned that during the coal strike the committee gave help to deserving ntses. On the motion of Mr Ambler, seconded by Mr Griffiths, the beaomaster of the Council School, Dr lurray was thanked for presioing. Much praise is due to the following committee: Messrs J T Ambler, chairman; Mr Frank Ward, secretary; Mr R Routledge treasurer; Messrs H Fairclough, D R Griffiths, W H Ire!and, J Randall, W Taylor and W Woods.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>FUTURE OF LYMM.</head>
        <p>THE FUTURE OF LYMM.</p>
        <p>ATEPAYERS' MEETING APPROVES THE PARLIAMENTARY BILL.</p>
        <p>WILL THERE BE A POLL?</p>
        <p>Although the ratepayers' meeting held in tie Council Chamber, Lymm, on Wednesday evening, to consider the Bill which the Urban District Council is promoting in the next session of Parliament to take over the waterworks and lake and carry out various improvements, was divided in ( pinion, there was a large majority in favour of the measure. It was, however, hinted that a poll miglA be demanded, but several appeala were made to the opposition to avoid the bitter feeling and extra expense ahich it was felt such a procedure would entail. Befote the meeting of ratepayers began the Urban District Council had met and formally confirmed the promotion of the Bill. Interest in the project was evident from the large attendance at the public meeting, the room being uncomfortably crowded. Mr W G Groves (chairman of the Council) presided and the members of the Council present were :—Messrs G L Welford, 0 G Smith, it 11 Gotobed, Holt, S W Gould and F Lockyer, with W Mullard (clerk). There was also in attendance Messrs W Beckett (consulting engineer), It H S Butterworth (of Messrs Butterworth and Co., London, Parliamentary agents), and Thomas Ridgway (solicitor). At the outset Mr Ridgway briefly explained the provisions of the Bill, the primary object of which was to acquire the waterworks undertaking. The Council had been negotiating since last May—be thought before that—with the Lyrum Estates Development Co., and Sir William Lever, Wit h regard to the purchase of the waterworks and the agreement was only signed and sealed by the Council in December. The Council had agreed to pay £20,250 for the waterworks, the lake, the land round the lake, and the dingle. Powers were being taken to supply the parish of Warburton with gas and water should occasion arise. At the present time the North Cheshire Water Company had powers to supply water to Warbur.ton,but they had never exercised 'hem and they were quite in harmony with the Bill. Mr Ridgway explained that powers with regard to streets, build logs and infectious diseases were being sought in view of the contemplated development of Lyrocre and other provisions were included to avoid repeated applications for provisional orders. At this stage the inevitable" Voice "asked : Is it fair to have a meeting in a place where the ratepayers cannot get in? (Applause). Mr Welford tben.moved a resolution approving the application by the Council in the next session of Parliament for a Bill to authorise them to purchase the water works &amp;c. He gave an extremely lucid and interesting exposition of the measure and emphasised the anxiety of the members of the Council, most of whom were old residents of Lymm, that the develop- ment of the district should proceed on the right lines. He detail( d the negotiations which took place with the Dewhurst family and afterwards with Lever Bros. for the purchase of the waterworks. The Council felt it was a ,ery wise thing that the ownership of the water supply should, whenever possible, be vested in the local authority, and they would have been guilty of a grave dereliction of duty if they had allowed an oppor- tunit y to pass, of purchaseag the undertakings on fairly reasotable terms. Messrs Lever Bros. imp sad the condition that the purchase should include the lake. Though the Council regietted that proviso, they were reconciled to it from the commercial and social aspects of the case. They found that the water of the lake was used for power purposes, and that the consequent saving in expenditure which would otherwise be incurred in the consumption of coal was something like £lOO a year. Then again, if the council did not buy the lake, it might be sold to anybody. flier° would he plenty of people ready to exploit the lake and it might tail into the hands of those who would make it a mere pleasure resort. They knew what that might mean to Lymm—the destruction, very largely,. f its character as the pleasant place which they all knew it to be. He thought that tne Council made a very fair bargain "i am not here to say "continued "Mr Welford that Sir Wiliam Lever gave us anything, but I am here to say that we gave nothing away. I am quite satisfied that if he gets his £20,250. he gets nothing but what he gives an adequate considera- tion for, sod we are not asking the ratepayers to pay one penny of an unfair price. The price we are paying for air we are getting—which includes a great deal more than we were negotiating to purchase from the Dewhursts —is ounsiderably less than the Dewhursts asked us for the waterworks alone." Mr Welford explained that by proceeding by Bill, the Council would obtain much better terms for the repayment of the loan than would be possible if applications were made to the Local Government Board. The net income of the waterworks was £BOO and he thought that was capable of expansion. Assuming that the repayment of the £20,250 was spread over 45 years and that the rate of interest did not exceed 4 per cent, it would mean that the outside sum which the committee would have to find each year would be £1,113. It had been said that there would be an addition of Is. 7d. in the to the rates; some more modest people had put it at Is. As he had explained, they had £BOO a year coming in Irons the waterworks, so the largest amount they could possibly have to provide from the rates would be £313. One penny in the £ produced close upon £lOO, so that the extra rater burden would not be more than 3d. in the £. Explaining other provisions of the Bill, Mr Welford pointed out that the existing powers of the Council were not sufficient to deal with the industrial developments which were expected cinotuhnecil-li,eaitnleypdroismtroituitn.g tFinallyhe B i Ll e hdaedc beenarcl thatt:i a I a tt eti de by a single eye to the well-being of the district as a whole. (Applause). Mr 0 G Smith seconded the resolution. ;Ile Council be said, had given most careful and anxious thought and study to the problem, and they were unanimous that it would be a wise thing to go on with the Bill and a most unwise thing to think of turning back. He wasa stout believer in public control of such undertakings as waterworks, and he was convinced that what Mr Welford had said had been an over-statement rather than an understatement of the responsibilities that were likely to fall upon the ratepayers of Lymm. In reply to Mr Ewart, Mr Welford gave an assurance that the £BOO men- tioned was clear profit from the waterworks, after depreciation and renewals bad been charged against revenue. The profit had risen steadily year by year as the number of houses using the water had increased. Mr Ewart expressed doubt as to whether the Council</p>
        <p>mind goods being Sold Below Cost,so long as room is made for new 1913 cloths that will shortly arrive, and therefore " WE" are offer- ing to make suits from 12/11 to measure; trousers from 4/9 to measure; whilst overcoats (ready for service) are similarly reduced to those who will call and order NOW. is FAMOUS hi WARRINGTON. "SUITS, LIMITED'S" huge success At this moment there are over 500 rolls of cloth at their shop in HORSEMARKET STREET,WAR- RINGTON, and every one has been thoroughly tested, and is fully guaranteed to wear well and give the utmost satisfaction.</p>
        <p>at once visit "SUITS, LIMITED'S" establishment, and select what suits them from the very varied selection that they will find. Whether you require with an Overcoat, Suit, or only an extra Pair of Trousers—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <p>. . . . .•• . .</p>
        <p>negotiated with the Dewhurst family with the serious intention of purchasing, and also ventured the view that the last offer from the Dewhurst family would have been £l,OOO or £2,000 less than they were paying to Lever Bros. Notwithstanding the eloquence of Mr Welford, he did not believe that 3d in the £ would be sufficient to meet the responsibilities the Council wished to incur. Mr Welford said the Council were anxious to purchase the waterworks from the Dewhurst family twelve years ago and when the negotiations were re-opened at a more recent date they were even keener in their desire. Mr C Cowper said he agreed that the Council ought to buy the waterworks, but he was shown a beautiful painting of a fine road which Sir William Lever was going to construct round the Dam at a cost of £6,000. Was he carrying that out? The Chairman: Sir William Lever is going to make a road round the Dam. We have already passed a plan for that and one side is being completed now. Mr Cowper : The general report is that he is not carrying that out. The Chairman: Sir William agreed to construct the whole of the road round the Dam within five years. A Voice: We are discussing the Bill, not the new road. Mr Ridgway said the question of the roadway was not a vital one in considering the Bill. It was not on the agreement in the schedule. Sir William was not bound to take the road right round the lake. Mr Cowper: Then why were we shown the plan? You seem to have great powers. You might think Lymm was a great mercantile city, judging from the powers you are seeking. Why, there are three or four of the biggest houses to let now. Who are the rates to come from? The people who are here on a rental can walk out any day, and the burden will fall upon us who are settled here. I think the powers are tremendous lam not against buying the water works, but does Heatley want water and gas down there? The Chairman : The obtaining of these powers does not entail any more cost to us. Mr Beckett, consulting engineer, presented his valuta Hon, made up as follows: watetworks and adjoining land, £16,754; lake and surrounding I land (including dingle) £4,000; total £20,754 9s. 6d, 1 After Mr Newton (the clerk to the Warburton Parish Councillor) had raised a protest against that body not having been officially consulted, Mr Molesworth asked why the Council could not go forward with the purchase of the waterworks and leave the other part of the Bill alone? It was all very well to say that there was no obligation to incur the expenditure involved by all the provisions of the Bill, but if the Council got the power to spend money, they would certainly spend it. What on earth did they want with parks at Lymm ? It was a piece of folly. Mr Ridgway explained that the Council had no intention of buying parks. The term "park" was applied to tbe land round the lake by Sir William Lever, but it was really a misnomer. Mr Holt made a point of the fact that under private management the water rate was a heavy one. There was no chance of reducing it until the local authority took over the management, and he believed that ultimately the Council would also be- able to give free baths and free water closets. Mr Ratcliffe, who had asked several questions,rose to address the meeting when The Chairman said: I shall proceed to put the resolution. Mr Welford: I think Mr Ratcliffe has had more than his innimis already. (fiear, hear and " No, no"). A Voice: It is for the Chairman to deal with the meeting, not Mr Welford. Mr Ratcliffe was allowed to proceed, and, criticising the purchase of the lake, he said he bad no fear of Sir William Lever coming in the dead of night and "sneaking" the lake. (Laughter). Nor had ha any qualms with regard to possible developments of the lake, because the Estate Company Owned all the land surrounding it and no-one's interesst would be greater than their's, in keeping it nice and select. Mr Ratcliffe next came into conflict with the engineer I by remarking: I don't know what you are an expert at. 1 , The Chairman: i must ask you to withdraw that statement. Mr Ratcliffe: I apologise, if I conveyed anything in the nature of a reflection. Continuing, Mr Rateliff e pointed out that the public already had the right to walk on the side of the lake. Later Mr Ratcliffe was called upon to withdraw the word "juggle,' BS applied to the figures submitted, but in doing so. he added that it was possib.e for competent accountants to keep within the range of the law and to manipulate the accounts so as to apportion expenditure under different headings. He complained that £4,000 had been appropriated to the district fate, while the water rate was kept separate. After. further discussion the resolution was put to the meeting and declared carried. Mr Ratcliffe: Will it be necessary to give notice that we demand a poll? Mr Ridgway: You can demand a poll, under the Borough Funds Act, within seven days. Mr Holt: It will be nothing but a scandal if the ratepayers are put to the expense of a poll, after a meeting like this. In reply to Mr Cowper, it was stated that the cost of a poll would be anything from £2O to £5O. Mr Welford, in appealing to the opposition not to persist in their demand for a poll, stated that if the present project were defeated, they would never again have the chance of acquiring the waterworks. Mr Ratcliffe : That is a mistake. Mr T J Ridgway, as probably the oldest inhabitant of the village, said he was perfectly satisfied that, looking ahead and not merely at to-day, the Council had acted wisely. He trusted a poll would not be demanded.</p>
        <p>Mr 0 0 Smith said that if the Bill was lost the legal expenses already incurred would have to ba charged on the rates. Mr Gotobed also appealed to the opposition not to put the district to the expense and unnecessary feeling of a poll. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>NOBLE HUSBANDMAN.</head>
        <p>A NOBLE HUSBANDMAN.</p>
        <p>Laertes is depicted by Homer as a typical Greek noble. He owned much land, and took a personal and practical interest in his property. Even in his advanced age, he worked in the garden, tending the beautiful and fertile vines for which Gm- ece has always been noted. Perhaps to-day, some of the 60,000 Greek farmers who toil in their vineyards that we may enjoy delicious Currants, are descendants of the• noble Laertes. At any rate their products are worthy of the best Grecian traditions. The Currant—the little dried 'grape of Greece—is a concentrated treat. Where the Currant is, there is savouriness and nutriment. This new way of using it should interest you. Hom 'NY PUDDING.</p>
        <p>2ozs hominy, a, pint milk, 1 egg, loz castor sugar, loz currants, and hala teaspoonful vanilla flavouring essence. METHOD.—BoiI up the milk and stir in the hominy, and allow it to stand for about an hour. Beat in the egg, sweeten to taste, and add the flavouring essence, also the currants. Pour the mixture into a buttered pie dish, and bake for about 20 minutes in a moder- ately heated oven. Dredge with castor sugar and serve hot. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>After covering about five miles, A Gregson, P Antrobus, J Hanzon and T Torton were the first four home of a small pack of Warrington A.C., who were uneer a cloud owing to the death of a former captain, S D Parsonage, snd the majority of the older members preferred to attend at the graveside. It is over five years since poor Sammy took an active part, but his demise is none the less regretted by the entire member- ship who proudly remember a splendid performance in the" Northern" of 1900 by as good a sportsman and comrade as ever donned a pump. He was a close.friend , and an ardent admirer of C J Straw and Alfred Shrubb land the latter is now coalhing the eminently successful country team of the Harvard University, will be another Borrower when be learns the news. TO-DAY'S FIXTURE. Warrington A.C. meet at their pavilion at 2.45 p.m. and Runcorn Tally Ho are expected to provide the opposition. 1 The Senior Points Competition will be decided and a medal awarded to the first novice home of the loc a club.</p>
