      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <p>NESS HOURS 9 to 8 DAILY</p>
        <p>FOREIGN TARIFFS AND BRITISH INDUSTRY.</p>
        <p>[To TIIE EDITOR OF THE EXAMINER.]</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln your recent obituary of Mr. Septimus Kenyon you clearly show how hostile foreign tariffs shackle British trade.</p>
        <p>Referring to Mr. Kenyon's intention of dipping.. wire varnish you say —" A firm in Germany also• interested itself in the process, but Mr Kenyon ultimately received a letter stating that the duty on the varnish was so great that they could not advantageously make use of it." And again—" the passing of the McKinley Tariff also did great injury to Mr Kenyon's varnish trade." Thus what might have developed into a flourishing Warrington industry was practically snuffed out by the fiscal systems of our commercial rivals. This is by no means an isolated case but it is sufficient to show that the weapon of relatiatory tariffs might well be used to the advantage of the British workman. Of what advantage is so called 67teap food to the working man if be does not draw the wages to procure it? A sixpenny loaf is cheap to the man with a shilling in his pocket, whereas a penny loaf is dear if he is out of work. .</p>
        <p>-- With powers to negotiate no doubt many of the existing tariff walls could be broken down, an British commerce would flourish in greater degree than ever.—Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>W A GLEAVE.</p>
        <p>1, Brighton Road, The Avenue, Southampton. [The late Mr. Kenyon's experience demon- strated one fact which tariff reformers conveni- ently ignore—namely, that the consumer, and not the foreigner, pays the tax. Owing to the Protective duty, the price of the varnish to the manufacturer in a Protected country was enhanced to such an extent that he was unable advantage- ously to avail himself of an:uivention which would otherwise have been useful in his business. The weapon of retaliation, instead of being " to the advantage of the British workman " would simi:arly hamper our own manufacturers by limiting their sources of supply and raising their cost of production, with the result that not only would our trade in the markets of the world be jeopardised, but the home consumer would inevitably be penalized. As to the prosperity of British trade, the latest Board of Trade returns. are sufficiently convincing.—En." Ex."]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>NEW TONIC FOOD FROM TURTLE.</head>
        <p>NEW TONIC FOOD FROM TURTLE.</p>
        <p>EXCEPTIONALLY NOURISHING, YET QUITE ECONOMICAL.</p>
        <p>An entirely new food, remarkable for its strengthen- ing and building-up properties, has recently come before the public in the form of Bellis's Turtle Cup. This new preparation has already met with wide appreciation, and, what is perhaps more important in regard to a food, it has also received the full and spontaneous .endorsement of the medical profession. Turtle Cup is real turtle in a concentrated form. Scientists regard the turtle as of extraordinarily high value as an article of food, so rich is it in phosphorus of an easily assimilable nature. Doctors prescribe turtle, especially for invalids, but hitherto only the very wcalthy have been able to afford the high prices which the turtle commands. To-day Turtle Cup places the benefits of real turtle within the reach of everyone. It not only makes a liquid food which is exceedingly strengthening; it is also delicious to the taste. Turtle Cup is far superior in every respect to meat extracts or beef tea, yet it is quite as reasonable in price. A 2oz. bottle of Turtle Cup costs one shilling, and, with simply the addition of boiling water, will make eight cups of the most sustaining nourishment it is possible to obtain. You can obtain Turtle Cup from any grocer or stores, or post free by sending price direct to Turtle Cup (1911), Ltd., Dep. 03, 10, Bury Street, St. Mary Axe, London, E.C. Buy some to-day, but insist strongly on having Belhs's Turtle Cup. No other will do.</p>
      </div>
