      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <p>10</p>
        <p>FOOD TAXERS' DEBACLE. By A. H. Crostield. The best friends of the Conservative leaders—if they have any friends just now—would scarcely claim that they are monuments of political stabil- ity; but some of them might shine as quick-change artists on a music-hall stage. For the last few years a pennon gaily floating —or, at any rate, floating—from the Conservative mast has borne the inscription "Referendum." Only the other day Lord Lansdowne and Mr. Bonar Law assisted at a great demonstration in London in hauling down this flag; and now, heigh, presto! the Conservatives of Lancashire are doing their level best to haul it up again. But while the Liverpool Courier and the Man- chester Courier, as representative organs of Lan- cashire. Toryism, are urging this, the Birmingham Post refuses to listen to anything of the kind, and the Satsman (the chief Tory organ north of the Tweed) tells U 3 it would be "the end of ,represen- tative Government." By way of finding escape from this deadlock, the Daily Telegraph argues that the country must be guaranteed a second General Election before it is asked to consent to the new Food Taxes. And this by no means exhausts the list of new "turns" on the Tory stage. A few year's since I heard Mr. Chamberlain in the House of Com- mons make his oft-quoted declaration that Pre- ference for the Colonies is impossible without a tax on food. A section of the Conservative Party have suddenly discovered that that important item in their political play bill, as now adopted, had better be left out. They stand for Preference for Colonial products other than food stuffs and raw materials, only to be told by the Morning Post and the Birmingham Post that this is sheer nonsense. Another, and that far and away the largest section of all, now tacitly or avowedly leave out the Colonies and go in frankly for Protection, while to complete the picture Mr. Bonar Law comes down to Ashton and says that while the Tariff Reformers don't want to tax food, we may have to do so to please the Colonies and Colonial statesmen; so that if after conferring with them this were found to be necessary, the Colonists waild, in effect, bear the responsibility. And that is the climax! Let us see what some of the Colonies have had to say about this amazing pro- posal. I take first the Montreal Star, a paper which is known to be in very close touch with the Canadian Prime Minister, Mr. Borden; and the Star writes as follows : "The last thing we would desire in this prosperous country would be that the least important citizen of the United Kingdom should feel for a moment that he was finding his daily loaf a crumb the smaller in order that fat farm- ers of the Canadian prairies should pocket larger profits. Nothing would so quickly turn the entire democracy of Great Britain against Im- perial unity, which is a necessity alike to Canada and to the Mother-country." Australian opinion is just ' as strong in its con- demnation of Mr. Bonar Law's proposal. The Melbourne Argus writes : "Mr. Bonar Law's proposal to submit Tariff Reform to a Colonial Conference is most dan- gerous. That the Dominions should take a hand in the determination of the internal taxa- tion of the United Kingdom would naturally arouse the strong of British taxpayers. The most fundamental of the principles upon which the Empire is based is the right of each part -to decide for itself the amount and charac- ter of its own taxation. We cannot conceive even the hottest Australian champions of Cham- berlainism wishing that the inhabitants of the Motherland should subject themselves to taxes which they did not feel to be to their own advan- tage, but solely for the benefit of the Dominions. The promise to restrict the duties to about 10 per cent. on manufactured gbods and 5 per cent. of food stuffs does not deceive anybody. The whole history of Protective duties proves that this is only a beginning, and the Australian, Canadian and American examples demonstrate this. In Germany the persistent upward ten- dency is still more pronounced." • I have used italics for the last Part of the quotation just to emphasise what is so well worth the attention of those who are beguiled by Tariff Reformers, who tell them not to mind paying a little more for their food or clothing because, after all, "it will only be a little." The Sydney Herald writes : "We never asked, nor do we desire that women and children living under well-nigh hope- less•conditions in the slums of London, Man- chester, and Liverpool shall pay more for their bread and clothing in order that we may pros- per by their impoverishment, nor can we offer any hope that our markets will in the future be more open to British manufacture than they have been hitherto. Our ambition is to manu- facture ourselves the goods we need, and whilst we freely offer the small preference now granted to British trade (and most of us would increase it), and we have a protectionist system, it will be frankly used to benefit Australian rather than English manufacturers." Mark in each of the quotations I have given how strongly the Colonist repudiates the mon- strous proposal that the poorest of our population at home should feel the pinch of greater poverty in order to help to make Colonists who are already :well-to-do more prosperous still. If we turn to opinion at home on this extraordin- ary performance of Mr. Bonar Law's at Ashton, I don't think any Liberal pen could successfully compete with the description of the situation given by "A Tory" writing in the Times newspaper. That a blunder was committed, says "A Tory," it is "not worth while to deny" "An announcement upon the highest authority that a Unionist victory at the next election might not mean a tax on food after all, but, on the other hand, that it might be a shaking up of all the settled but easily disturbed convic- tions of the timid, of all the hopes of the reluc- tant converts, of all the angry passions of the orthodox faithful, and of all the indignation of the ordinary man who likes to know whether he is standing on his head or his heels I—clearly it was a blunder of no ordinary magnitude. Indeed it was a blunder on so sublime a scale as to make it quite incredible that any human brain 'could have compassed it ,without assistance." As we know on authority that Mr. Bonar Law only spoke after consultation with his colleagues —(was F. E., I wonder, one of them !)—that last remark is a peculiarly unkind cut for all of them. "F. C. G." hits off the Ashton episode with his usual acumen'; and humour. He draws Mr. Bonar Law as a hen in the Ashton poultry yard looking with .dismay at what she expected to find an egg that she had just laid—with dismay on her countenance because she discovers that in- stead of an egg it is a smoking bomb from which the other occupants of the poultry yard are hurry- ing away in terror. No wonder Mr. F. E. Smith had to go to Dudley to try and explain the speech away, and make vain efforts to extricate the party from its impossible dilemma. In the course of that speech he said : "The very object of discussing the method of carrying out Imperial Preference with the Colonies is in order that we may be informed by them with authority what in their judgment is vital, in order that the policy may be carried out." It may interest Mr. Smith to hear what the Colonists are already saying on the question, and so I quote the following from the Sydney Daily Telegraph : "Mr. Chamberlain knew more than Mr. Bonar Law knows. He laid down at the outset of his campaign that if there was to be Prefer- ence there must be duties on food. For obvi- ous reasons, only in that way could the pro- ducts of the outlying parts of the Empire be given a tariff advantage. In paltering with that inescapable policy t•Mr. Bonar Law is only trying to bamboozle the people." I shall have a further word later on to say about Mr. F. E. Smith's position, but I will not prolong this letter just now except to draw attention before I conclude to the way in which the whole controversy now raging in the Conservative party is based upon tactics. Principles don't seem to have a look in at all, and in fact it is clear enough that the Conservative party ,has no definite, clear- cut conception of future policy at all. Even in the matter of Food Taxes, those in the party who now profess their willingness to discard such taxa-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>WARRINGTON DRAMATIC SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON DRAMATIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>' Sunday ' at the Royal Court Theatre</p>
        <p>MR. J. W. PRIYJELA.RD</p>
        <p>(as "Jacky ")</p>
        <p>tion are not to be trusted. I have quoted from a letter in The Times that has received a -good deal of attention, the letter I mean from "A Tory." Now he writes a second letter to The Times, urging that these proposed Food Taxes should bo dropped, but in the same breath expresses "a per- fect confidence that if once we make a beginning with Tariff Reform and Preference we shall go steadily forward with it, and that in the end we ishall arrive, by one means or Another, at a comprehensive system." Precisely, but forewarned is forearmed, and I don't in the least doubt that responsible, level- headed citizens who have no intention whatever of. being caught napping . are .making a very care- ful note of all these • devious proceedings. Tory wirepullers may think such tactics mighty clever, but they will find that in England political parties cannot sqbsist or win elections either en mere tactics. British electors can respect a stern fight for principle, but the spectacle of the Tory party with its discredited leaders and disgusted follow- ers making its desperate, contradictory and con- fused efforts to scramble back into office. and power by mere tactical devices that everyone can .see through. That is a spectacle which only excites. as it richly deserves universal derision and Con- tempt.</p>
        <p>Contrast with all these far-fetched, preposterous attempts to sow the seeds, not of Imperial union, but of serious discord in the Empire, with, the recent decision of the Committee of Defence and the admirable despatch published the other day by the Colonial Secretary as a,result of the wise. and timely negotiations carried through last year. That way indeed lies unity and ever-increasing Imperial strength, and that deep and abiding over-sea loyalty of which we have recently had such splendid evidence.</p>
        <p>A. H. CROSFTELD.</p>
        <p>riot° MISS NELLIE MULLINS (as "Sunday.")</p>
        <p>MR. STANLEY WRI (as " Lively.")</p>
        <p>Cur readets are reminded cf the Warrington Dramatic Society's perfo:manci s of " Sunday" which. will take place at the Royal Court Theatre on Thurs- day, Friday and S.durday next Miss Nellie Mullins, who plays the tftle part, will make her first appearance . in 'drama. She is, however, well known for her work on the amateur operatic st2ge, having figured prominently in "San Toy" and " Dorothy." Mr. Sydney Wallbead, an old and talented member of the society, has, in addition to impersonating Torn Oxley, undertaken the onerous duties of stage manager of the production. Mr. Stanley Wright, who was for many years •a member of the society and made a reputation as the Rev. Robert Spalding- in "The Private Seere-. tary," will make a welc •me re-anpearance in the cast Mr. J. W. Pritchard is splendidly cast as Jacky. 4le is a new member of the society, but is very highly spoken of as an amateur actor. Seats may be booked doily at the Theatre box office. ' The proceeds will be devoted to local charities, for which the society has already raised over £2,000. Mr J. W. Buller' is, as usual. the energetic hon. secretary and Mr Arthur Browne is the hon. treasurer.</p>
