      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>INSURANCE BENEFITS.</head>
        <p>INSURANCE BENEFITS.</p>
        <p>The Pond for Warrington.- The medical benefits of the Insurance Act come int:, force on Wednesday next, end insured persons in Warrington may select their doctor from the following panel : JOSEPH ADAMS, I, Tanners-lane. JAMES BENNETT, Lynton Grange, Padgate, 1, O'Leary-street. and 1, Palmyra-square. GEORGE BINNS, Stoodley, Walton-rood, Stockton Heath, and for messages only 17. Si. Mary's-street. ItIRNEST EDWARD BOWDEN, 29, Bold-street. DONALD FERGUSON, Orford Villas, Orford-lane, and 48, Horsemarket-street, E J GEARY, Egypt-street. JOHN ALBERT HOWARD, Th 9 White House, Walton-road, Stockton Heath. ANDREW JAMES HUTTON, 3, Grappenhall-road Stockton Heath. JAMES DORE L15T0N,..6, Academy-street. WILLIAM ANDREW McKEE, I, Shatp-street. JOHN SINCLAIR MAN SON, 8, Winmarleigh-gtreet. JOHN MARTIN, 13, Palmyra-square. THOMAS ALEXANDER MURRAY, Penketh. JOHN BASSErr NADEN, 111, Liverpool-road. lAN OGILVIE, 117, Wilderspool-road. WILLIAM JOHN PEACOCKE, 2, James-street. CLARENCE R.OBIIVSON, 715, Knntsford-road. FRANK R. SEAGAR, 1, Bewsey-road. REGINALD SMITH. 2, Museum-street.</p>
        <p>ROBER r WILSON SUTHERLAND, 1, Palmyra Square ; Lynton Grange, Padgate, and 1, O'Leary-street.</p>
        <p>FREE CHOICE OF DOCTOR.</p>
        <p>Insured persons desiring attendance and treatment frcm a doctor which is not on the panel should apply to the local Insurance Committee fur permission to mahe their own arrhiigements. If such permission is granted, the committee have power to refund a sum proportionate to the medical benefit to which the insured workers would be entitled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>NEW BRIGHTON TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>NEW BRIGHTON TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>Former Warrington Barmaid Shot.</p>
        <p>A shocking discovery was made on a lonely part of the,foreshore at New Brighton on Wednesday night. The dead bodies of a young lady named Miss Jones, a barmaid at the Hotel Victoria, New Brighton, and a gentleman named Harold Foster Farrar, of Wrexham, were found lying together with bullet wounds in the bead.</p>
        <p>From inquiries in Warrington it has been ascertained that the deceased young lady was formerly employed as a barmaid at the Lion Hotel for about twelve months. Whilst at the hotel she was spoken of as being a very pleasant sort of girl and very popular with the customers. She was known as " Peggy." Miss Jones left Warrington last summer to take up a position as the Belle Vue Hotel, Port Erin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>YOUR CHILD'S WEAK THROAT.</head>
        <p>YOUR CHILD'S WEAK THROAT.</p>
        <p>PEPS are the Real Safeguard Against Holiday Coughs and Colds.</p>
        <p>There is always a lot (1 throat trouble among children in the early days of the new year. The little ones so thoroughly enter into the spirit of parties ad dances, and the delights of the pantomime, that they run great risk of catching colds in the raw night air on the way home. Even if they escape bronchitis or croup, how easy it is for a seemingly simple cold to bring about serious and oft-times permanent chest mischief. There is danger, too, of pleurisy or pneumonia, for the cheats of children are susceptible to sudden changes from heat to cold. - -</p>
        <p>_ The careful mother will guard against these dangers by keeping handy a box of Peps, the pleasant breath- able medicine in taolet form, which is a real safe- guard against coughs, colds, and throat soreness. The great fault of the old-fashioned home remedies for coughs and coldi is that they do not cure but merely hide for a time the prominent symptoms. Ordinary cough-mixtures, sold over the counter, are often even worse, for they usually contain opium, chloral, morphia, and other pernicious drugs. Peps, on the other hand, rely on the soothing, healing, antiseptic, and germicidal properties of certain rare essences which take the form of air and are breathed into the lungs as the tablets dissolve in the mouth.</p>
        <p>All children take rapidly to Peps, which can be taken with absolute safety. One little Peps tablet at bed-time will clear the tbroatand chest of the day's dust and " cold-germs " that may have invaded the breathing passages, and so enable the little ones to sleep without coughing fits. A few Peps tablets before starting out in the raw cold morning air is also an invaluable safeguard. Breathable Peps strengthen the throat and chest by direct contact and are proved the most effective agent for warding off coughs, colds, sore throat, and other chest. perils of sudden weather changes. Of all chemists at Is. lid. or 2s. 9d. per box ; post free at same prices from Peps, Carlton Hill, Leeds. See the name Peps on the box, and stamped on each tablets. Refuse all worthless substitutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>INFIRMARY BALL</head>
        <p>INFIRMARY BALL</p>
        <p>BRILLII.NT FUNCTION AT THE PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>Always a brilliant function, the annual ball in aid of the funds of the Warrington Infirmary, which took place in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening, was more than usually successful. The attendance numbered 200 and the scene, when the dancing was in progress, was one of great beauty and animation, the handsome dresses of the ladies presenting a striking pictut e. Mr Ralph Johnson had laid a special dancing floor and had also carried out a most tasteful scheme of decoration. several cesy and well-appointed retiring rooms were arranged in the galleries Choice plants and &amp;wets kindly sent by Lady Greenall, Mrs Armitage and Mrs Shepherd Cross, had been utilized with the best possible effect by members of the ladies' committee. Mr W P Bayley's band (augmented) was in attendance and played an admirable selection of twenty dances. Supper was served in the crush roLm, the ladies' committee having made themselves responsible for the catering. This depattment was under the super- vision of Mr H H Woollam, of Peucileton, Manchester, and gave the utmost satisfaction. The joint secre- taries of the ball committee were Mr G Ireland Blackburne and Mr J E Grsenall, to whose untiring efforts the complete success of the ball is in no small measure due.</p>
        <p>It is impossible to speak too highly of the work of the Ladies' Committee, consisting of the following : President, Lady Greenall ; the Mayoress (Mrs A Nield), Mrs El It Armitage, Mrs Ziba Armitage, Mrs liailey, Mrs A S Meekly, Mrs W Bracecamp, Mrs Broadbent, Mrs Coghill, Mrs Coxon, The Hon Mrs J E Cross, Mrs C Shepherd Cross, Mrs George Dakin, Mrs Edelsten. Mrs John Faircloua, Mrs Edward Fox, Mrs Watkin, Mrs F Aylmer Frost, Mrs Green, Mrs Henry Greenall, Mrs Harinood Banner, Mrs Hutton, Mrs Lor,g, Miss Massy, Mrs Monks, Mrs H Percy Mortimer, Mrs J Charlton Parr, Mrs H V Pigot, Mrs W Peter Bylands, and Mrs Willis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>DEBATE ON SOCIALISM.</p>
        <p>Our local Parliament re-assembled after the Christ rnas recess on Thursday evening, when the question of Socialism was debated. A resolution in favour of transferring the control of produrtion and exchange to industrial guilds, raegatieg to Parliament the functions of contro ling supplies between Guilds and international exchange, was defeated. Mr C Duke, the Socialist member for West Ham, introduced the resolution and contended that exploita- tion of the workers, under the existing economic system. was the root cause of poverty. The debate followed on the accustomed lines, the Prime MiLister (Mr E A White) stating that Socialists were not,like ,Oliver Twist, satisfied with asking for " more " ; they wanted all. Mr Jarman (Leader of the Opposition), pleaded for an extension of the principle of cc-operation as one means of spreading contentment among the workers' Mr J Dalby (Soc., E. Bradford), said the workers were worst off to-day than they were 50 or 100 years ago Mr E Wynne Jones (L., Chester), said the only hope for the workers was to educate, regulate and legislate. Next week Alderman Bennett will introduce a Tariff Reform Bill.</p>
      </div>
