      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>IMPERIAL PREFERENCE.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PREFERENCE.</p>
        <p>AN OPEN LETTER TO El CHARLES E. PARKER, J.P.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Parker,—l read the Open Letter which ycu were good enough to address to roe, on Saturday last, through the columns of the "Examiner," with much interest. Like all your letters, it was character- ized by a courtesy. a moderation and a spirit of fair- play which are lamentably lacking in the effusions of s many of the yurely party politicians; and I fully recognize that you are animated by the same spirit of pe.triotism and of devotion to the Empire which I claim for myself. " But I am as profoundly convinced as ever of the wisdom and of the necessity of the policy which I have for many rears been strenuously advccating under the name of Imperial Prefer-trice. As you say, I have always regarded the question of Tariff Reform primarily, as a means to an eud -and that and is, f courA,, the eb ser u: ion of the Bri.ish Ernpit e. But my study of the question has led roe more and more to feel that alt, gether apart from this partioular phase, Tariff Perorm has much to receanmend it, at ' y rate, in :he world as we -find it to-day. We both believe that uniVelkaal Free Trade is the best and uliimate solution ; but I am not convinced that Free Trade is by any rrif ars the best policy when it is so ' utterly ene-sided as in the case of the United Kingdom. Aloi e in all the world we fling our rnark, ts epen to the foreigner, and everywhere he builds up a great tariff wall akainst us. Behind its shelter he can develop hii industries with the certainty of free entrance into his home market and with an equal chance in ours. We, upon the other hand, have no certainty of cur own home market,and must climb a tariff wall, in many cases, built especially to keep us out, isf we de3irs to e iter his. It is a lop-sided arrangement which might be tolerable in ',manta, but it does not seem to He to be applicable to any well- governed and intelligent modern state. If the height of the tariff wall dues not matter, why were we so anxious to lower it in Japan, for instance? And why do we trouble to negotiate for better terms at all? And, if it he a proved and an immense advantage to . have the tariff wall lowered by one third in Canada— as Mr Lloyd George candidly admits it to be—is it not desirable to endeavour to get it lowered still further, both in Canada and over all the Empire, and, if possible, in every one of the competing states Moreover, under the existing regime, our noblest legislative measures are but instruments with which to handicap us in our foreign trade. We pass Workmen's Compensation Acts. an Old Age Pension Act, a National Insurance Act, a Minimum Wage Act, an Act to prevent Sweating and I believe in every one of them but every one of them increases the cost of production, and not only makes it more difficult for us to climb the tariff walls of our opponents, but makes it easier for them to compete with us both here and in all other countries, especially as many of them are free from the burden of this benevolent legislation. What is the use of pre- venting sweating here if we permit the products of foreign sweated labour to pour into our ports? What is the use of our Trade Unions endeavouring to obtain a standard wage if we still permit the foreigner to send into this country free the products of his cheaper labour ? If cheapness be the only aim, why not import Chinese coolies at once ? And it has always seemed to me tbat, if by impcsing a small duty on foreign manufactures, or if you like on foreign goods of all classes, we are able to produce them here instead, we gain immensely more than we lose._</p>
        <p>The -Free frader himself a- dmits that Tariff Reform would make the rich richer. If so, the country as a whole would certainly be richer too. If the riches are not properly distributed, that is the fault of the existing social eystem and not of Tariff Reform. I will not dwell up3n the advantages of a Tariff as a medias of raising ievenue, because I believe that there is a better and a wiser way. I mean a universal income tax, properly graduated. But, if we are to raise a portion of our ever-rising, national expenditure by import duties, it seems to me to be a piece of econoluic pedantery, to raise them on the very articles we cannot possibly produce at home, and so to place the whole burden on the back of the consumer, and to tax several necessary items in his daily food to the tune of some ten million pounds per year. I am. therefore, of opinion that, under existing circumstances, Tariff Reform would be a distinct advantage to the United Kingdom. But, if that aspect of the question stood alone, I should not be by any means as earnest and insistent in its advocacy as I am to-day, and as I have been ever since I took an active interest in politics. And it is with the Imperial , aspects of the question, as you rightly say, that I am mainly concerned. I think you are mistaken in supposing that either the body over which I have the privilege to preside, or the Women's Unionist and Tariff Reform League have been •` practically silent " oa the subject. Upon the contrary, they have made it a main feature in all their propaganda ; their Dumping Exhibition was largely occupied in demonstrating the possibilities of our Imperial trade; and the moviug words of our great leader, Mr. Joseph Chamberlain, are in evidence at all our larger meet- ings. It is perfectly true that we endeavour to emphasise the weakness of the Free Trade case by local illustrations and specific references to various British industries. But the flag of Preference has ever been held aloft in Warrington, and I, at any rate," am nut the man ta haul it down."</p>
        <p>With the petty Nuabbles of my own party I have not anythitig to do. There are always a certain number of papers and of politicians in every party who can only think in votes, but I do not believe in the cult of the jumping cat myself, and I am confident that what the English people like is a policy which does not change with every passing wind, and a captain who does not trim his sails to every wind that blows.</p>
        <p>Impet ial Preference is at the very root of our pro- posals, and, to make it thoroughly effective, I am of opinion that a small tax on foreign corn will he neces Entry ; but our leaders have repeatedly declared that such a tax will not involve any increase in the aggregate cost of the food of the people. They claim, I think with justice, that it will open up enormous additional sources of supply ; that it will result in increasing preferences and larger trade with all our Colonies ; and they have pledged themselves to apply the whole of the proceeds (d the tax in direct relief of the working classes and probably to give them, as a start, " a free breakfast table," a boon for which they have so long been vainly waiting. Moreover, the utmost tax which they propose to levy is extremely low—two shillings per quarter, on foreign wheat alone or considerably less than a farthing on the four pound loaf, and they are likewise under a definite pledge not to increase it without further authorization from the people. But, even if the net result of it all should be to increase the cost of bread to the utmost extent which I have indicated, it would amount to less than the sum which every worker is paying every week without a murmur to the National Insurance Fund, and it would, I think, be more than counter-balanced</p>
        <p>full contributions have been paid. As a result of Mr. Masterman's answer, and as Mr. Crostield's intention was to make:the presentation to the insured parents of tbe children born on the first day when benefits are payable under the Act, it is felt that the date in the condition must be altered accordingly. Will the parents. or friend of the parents, therefore, obtain a certificate of the birth of the child after Sunday midnight the 12th inst and before midnight Monday, the 13th inst. It is desired that the certificate should be given to Mr. E. A. Sanders at the Liberal Offices, Bewsey-street, on or before Wednesday evening next, the 15th inst. This offer is made to all, irrespective of polttics.</p>
      </div>
