      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-11_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. -- "Babes in the Wood."</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. -- "Babes in the Wood."</p>
        <p>Unparalleled success has attended the first visit to Warrington of Mr Gar Kiddies pantomime "Babes in the Wood" which has been staged before large audiences at the Royal Court Theatre this week. There is one outstanding feature in the production and that is that the original rhyme of the story is adhered to as much as is essential to make the panto- mime go with a swing and still retain the qualities which made such forms of entertainment so popular many years ago. The scenery is on a lavish scale and the fairy-like effects in some of the sets are very picturesque, notably " Syivanias Sylvan Home." Mr Kiddie has been exceedingly fortunate in securing a number of talented singers, comedians, and. e`pert dancers who are included in the cast which is an extensive one. The dresses are magnificent and the ballets are arranged with much taste. Altogether there is a distinctive •air hovering round the production and there is not one false note struck all through. Of course, the chief character in this popular story is Robin Hood a part ably under- taken by Miss Eva May Rosalyn, who makes the most i of her opportunities. Miss Roselyn is gifted n every phase of her part and renders some catchy songs in an irresi-tible way, her song " My heart is, with you to-night" having proved very popular. She 18 also a clever dancer and her one endeavour seems to be to make the panto a spirited production,never flagging in interest from beginning to end. Miss Marion Duncan is a bewitching Maid Marion possessed of a sweet, well modulated voice which she uses to advan- tage. The Wee Sisters Wright acquit themselves with credit in the part of the Babes. They sing and dance in a really remarkable manner. Miss Mavis Muir is a capital Sylvana, her singing being very sweet and her style, distinctive. There is an abund- ance of genuire humour and those responsible for this necessary element are Mr Dan Dawson, a jocular and witty Baron Janskins ; Mr Dan Lyall, a typical " dame " who creates roars of laughter in the Part of Ararninto Koffkake ; Mr Rex Kempton who is very clever and excruciatingly funny as Soft Sammy the village idiot and " The Slaters " who appear as the "kidnappers.' The latter are a couple af clever comedians as well as clever instrumentalists, their manipulation of the "post horn" and cornet being very commendable. The Fours Krays are expert dancers and rag-timers, and contribute a number of specialities, adding to the variety of the production. Those who have not done so, should see this capital pantomime to-night if they are fond of real rollicking fun.</p>
      </div>
