        <p>That principle would settle the right share of woman- hood in the duties and responsibilities of the com- munity. That principle would settle the question of Church and State Churchman and Nonconformist. The war against the State, had begun in earnest, and in a spirit, to his mind, quite contrary to t'te spirit contained in his text. It promised ill for the future work of common religion against the tremendous forces of irreligion. If the Welsh Church Bill became law it would leave more than one-third of the churchss without a penny of endowment as it stood to-day, and 500 curates would be sent adrift without a penny of compensation, and with no present prospect of employment: What could the man in the street say of this? Could he wonder at the growing indifference of the masses ? To think that professing Christian men should be wasting their energies in an endeavour to cripple the work of other professing Christian men, while the bulk of the populations in their large cities and towns were almost untouched with the Christian religion, and hundreds of millions of the peoples of the world had never set heard the sound of the Gospel.</p>
